window.COURSES = {"fr":{"modules":[{"id":"comprendre","icon":"🧠","title":"Comprendre la programmation","desc":"Ce que fait vraiment un ordinateur, ce qu'est un algorithme et comment pense un programmeur. Sans écrire de code au début.","badge":"Esprit de programmeur","lessons":[{"id":"ordinateur","title":"Un ordinateur, c'est quoi vraiment ?","subtitle":"La machine la plus rapide du monde… et la plus obéissante.","duration":8,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Tu utilises des ordinateurs toute la journée\n\nTon téléphone, la caisse du supermarché, le distributeur de billets, la box internet, la voiture récente de ton voisin… Ce sont tous des **ordinateurs**.\n\nIls ont l'air intelligents : ils reconnaissent ton visage, traduisent des phrases, te proposent la vidéo que tu as envie de voir. Alors, un ordinateur, est-ce que ça réfléchit ?"},{"t":"quiz","q":"D'après toi, un ordinateur est-il intelligent ?","options":["Oui, il comprend ce qu'on lui demande","Non, il exécute des instructions sans les comprendre","Seulement les ordinateurs récents"],"answer":1,"explain":"Un ordinateur ne comprend rien. Il **exécute des instructions**, une par une, à une vitesse folle : des milliards par seconde. Toute « l'intelligence » vient des personnes qui ont écrit ces instructions : les **programmeurs**."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Rapide, mais terriblement littéral\n\nImagine un cuisinier capable de couper mille oignons par seconde, mais qui n'a **aucune imagination** : il fait exactement ce qu'on lui dit, ni plus, ni moins.\n\nSi tu lui dis « fais une omelette », il ne bouge pas. Il ne sait pas ce qu'est une omelette. Il faut lui dire :\n\n1. Prends 3 œufs dans le frigo.\n2. Casse chaque œuf au-dessus du bol.\n3. Jette les coquilles à la poubelle.\n4. Bats les œufs pendant 30 secondes.\n5. …\n\nEt si tu oublies l'étape 3 ? Il y aura des coquilles dans l'omelette. Il ne se dira jamais « tiens, c'est bizarre »."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"","v":"Un ordinateur fait ce que tu lui dis, pas ce que tu veux dire.\nC'est LA phrase à retenir de cette leçon."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Entrée, traitement, sortie\n\nTous les programmes, du plus simple au plus impressionnant, suivent le même schéma :"},{"t":"html","v":"<div class=\"flow\">\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Entrée</b><span>Ce qu'on donne à l'ordinateur : un clic, un texte tapé, une photo, un fichier.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Traitement</b><span>Les instructions du programme transforment ces données.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Sortie</b><span>Le résultat : un affichage, un son, un message envoyé, un fichier.</span></div>\n</div>"},{"t":"md","v":"Prends une calculatrice : tu tapes `12 × 4` (entrée), elle calcule (traitement), elle affiche `48` (sortie)."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Tu envoies un message vocal sur WhatsApp. Qu'est-ce qui est l'**entrée** ?","options":["Ta voix captée par le micro","Le message qui arrive chez ton ami","Le réseau internet"],"answer":0,"explain":"L'entrée, c'est ce que le programme reçoit : ta voix enregistrée par le micro. Le traitement la compresse et l'envoie. La sortie, c'est le son joué sur le téléphone de ton ami."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Un programme, c'est une liste d'instructions\n\nUn **programme** est une suite d'instructions écrites pour un ordinateur. **Programmer**, c'est écrire ces instructions, dans le bon ordre, sans rien oublier.\n\nEssaie tout de suite. Ce petit robot veut sa tasse de café ☕. Il ne connaît que trois instructions : avancer d'une case, tourner à gauche, tourner à droite. Construis son programme, puis lance-le."},{"t":"widget","name":"robot","config":{"levels":[{"grid":["S..G"],"dir":"E","hint":"Le robot regarde vers la droite (la flèche rouge). Combien de cases doit-il avancer ?"},{"grid":["S..","#.#","..G"],"dir":"E","hint":"Tourner ne fait pas avancer : le robot pivote sur place."},{"grid":["S.#G","#.#.","#..."],"dir":"E","hint":"Prends ton temps : suis le chemin avec ton doigt et note chaque instruction."}]}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Ce que tu viens de vivre\n\nSi le robot s'est cogné dans un rocher, c'est normal, et c'est précieux. Tu viens de découvrir trois vérités de la programmation :\n\n- **L'ordre compte.** « Avancer puis tourner » n'est pas « tourner puis avancer ».\n- **Rien n'est deviné.** Le robot n'a jamais « compris » où était le café. Il a suivi tes instructions.\n- **On se trompe, on corrige, on relance.** Les programmeurs professionnels passent une grande partie de leur temps à faire exactement ça. Ça s'appelle **déboguer**."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Ton programme fait foncer le robot dans un rocher. Que fais-tu ?","options":["Je recommence tout de zéro","Je cherche l'instruction qui pose problème et je la corrige","Je change de robot"],"answer":1,"explain":"On ne jette pas tout : on cherche l'erreur (le « bug »), on la corrige, et on relance. C'est le cycle normal de tout programmeur."}]]},{"id":"algorithmes","title":"Les algorithmes, des recettes pour machines","subtitle":"Avant d'écrire du code, on écrit une méthode.","duration":10,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Un mot savant pour une idée simple\n\nUn **algorithme**, c'est une suite d'étapes précises pour résoudre un problème. Tu en utilises tous les jours sans le savoir :\n\n- une **recette de cuisine** est un algorithme pour faire un plat ;\n- un **itinéraire** (« tout droit, puis à gauche au rond-point ») est un algorithme pour arriver quelque part ;\n- la **méthode de la division** apprise à l'école est un algorithme de calcul.\n\nUn programme, c'est un algorithme traduit dans une langue que l'ordinateur comprend."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Remets la recette dans l'ordre\n\nVoici l'algorithme pour préparer une tasse de thé. Les étapes ont été mélangées. Remets-les dans le bon ordre (avec les flèches ou en les faisant glisser)."},{"t":"widget","name":"order","config":{"prompt":"Préparer une tasse de thé","items":["Remplir la bouilloire avec de l'eau","Allumer la bouilloire et attendre que l'eau bouille","Verser l'eau bouillante dans la tasse","Plonger le sachet de thé dans l'eau chaude","Attendre 3 minutes","Retirer le sachet de la tasse"]}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## La précision avant tout\n\nUn humain comprend « ajoute un peu de sel ». Un ordinateur, non : combien de grammes ? Quel sel ? Ajouter où ?\n\nIl y a une vieille blague de programmeurs :\n\n> Une personne dit à son ami programmeur : « Va acheter une baguette. S'il y a des œufs, prends-en six. »\n> Il revient avec six baguettes."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Pourquoi le programmeur revient-il avec six baguettes ?","options":["Il avait très faim","La consigne était ambiguë : « en prendre six » pouvait désigner les baguettes","Il n'y avait plus d'œufs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Il y avait des œufs, donc il a appliqué « prends-en six »… aux baguettes, le seul objet dont on parlait. Un ordinateur aurait fait pareil. Une instruction doit avoir **un seul sens possible**."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Décider et répéter\n\nRegarde bien les recettes : elles contiennent souvent deux mots magiques.\n\n- **Si** : « *Si* tu aimes le thé sucré, ajoute un sucre. » C'est une **condition** : on fait l'action seulement dans certains cas.\n- **Tant que** / **Répète** : « *Tant que* l'eau ne bout pas, attends. » C'est une **boucle** : on répète une action.\n\nVoici un algorithme célèbre : trouver le plus grand nombre d'une liste, par exemple les notes d'une classe. Remets les étapes dans l'ordre."},{"t":"widget","name":"order","config":{"prompt":"Trouver la meilleure note de la classe","items":["Lire la première note et la retenir comme « meilleure note pour l'instant »","Passer à la note suivante","**Si** elle est plus grande que la note retenue, la retenir à la place","**Répéter** les deux étapes précédentes jusqu'à la dernière note","Annoncer la note retenue : c'est la meilleure"],"success":"Exactement. Tu viens d'écrire un vrai algorithme avec une condition et une boucle. Dans quelques chapitres, tu l'écriras en Python."}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Le robot apprend à répéter\n\nÉcrire « Avancer » six fois de suite, c'est long. Les programmeurs détestent se répéter ! Dans ce nouveau défi, tu peux cliquer sur le petit bouton **×1** d'une instruction pour la répéter 2, 3, 4 ou 5 fois.\n\nAttention : chaque niveau impose un nombre maximum de blocs."},{"t":"widget","name":"robot","config":{"loops":true,"levels":[{"grid":["S.....G"],"dir":"E","maxBlocks":1,"hint":"Une seule instruction, répétée."},{"grid":["S....",".#.#.","....G"],"dir":"E","maxBlocks":3},{"grid":["S..#","##.#","G..#"],"dir":"E","maxBlocks":5,"hint":"Le robot doit faire demi-tour par le bas."}]}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## À retenir\n\n- Un **algorithme** est une méthode en étapes précises, sans ambiguïté.\n- L'**ordre** des étapes compte.\n- Deux outils reviennent partout : la **condition** (si…) et la **boucle** (répéter…)."},{"t":"quiz","q":"« Tant qu'il reste des clients dans la file, servir le client suivant. » Qu'est-ce que c'est ?","options":["Une condition","Une boucle","Une erreur"],"answer":1,"explain":"« Tant que » répète une action plusieurs fois : c'est une **boucle**. Elle s'arrête quand la file est vide."}]]},{"id":"langages","title":"Comment parle-t-on à un ordinateur ?","subtitle":"Des 0 et des 1 jusqu'à Python.","duration":10,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Tout est électricité\n\nÀ l'intérieur d'un ordinateur, il y a des milliards de minuscules interrupteurs. Chacun n'a que deux états : **éteint** ou **allumé**. On les note **0** et **1**.\n\nUn seul 0 ou 1 s'appelle un **bit**. Un groupe de 8 bits s'appelle un **octet**. Tu connais déjà ce mot : un fichier de 3 Mo (mégaoctets) contient environ 3 millions d'octets.\n\nAvec 8 interrupteurs, on peut écrire tous les nombres de 0 à 255. Chaque interrupteur a une valeur : 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1. On additionne les valeurs des interrupteurs allumés."},{"t":"widget","name":"binary","config":{"target":5,"showChar":false,"success":"Bravo : 00000101, c'est 4 + 1 = 5. Tu sais lire le binaire !"}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les lettres aussi sont des nombres\n\nSi l'ordinateur ne connaît que des nombres, comment affiche-t-il du texte ? Grâce à une convention : chaque lettre correspond à un nombre. Par exemple, **A** vaut 65, **B** vaut 66, **a** vaut 97.\n\nAllume les bons interrupteurs pour écrire la lettre **A** (65)."},{"t":"widget","name":"binary","config":{"target":65,"success":"C'est ça : 01000001 = 64 + 1 = 65 = « A ». Quand tu tapes un message, ton téléphone stocke en réalité des nombres comme celui-ci."}},{"t":"md","v":"C'est pareil pour tout le reste : une photo est une grille de points de couleur (des **pixels**), et chaque couleur est un nombre. Une musique est une suite de nombres qui décrivent le son. **Pour un ordinateur, tout est nombre.**"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Personne n'écrit en binaire\n\nÉcrire des programmes avec des 0 et des 1 serait un cauchemar. Alors on a inventé des **langages de programmation** : des langues plus proches de l'humain, que l'on traduit ensuite automatiquement en binaire.\n\nVoici le même programme, « afficher Bonjour », dans quatre langages. Compare-les."},{"t":"widget","name":"tabs","config":{"tabs":[{"label":"Assembleur","code":"section .data\n    msg db \"Bonjour\", 10\nsection .text\n    global _start\n_start:\n    mov rax, 1\n    mov rdi, 1\n    mov rsi, msg\n    mov rdx, 8\n    syscall\n    mov rax, 60\n    xor rdi, rdi\n    syscall","note":"Très proche de la machine : on manipule directement les cases mémoire du processeur. Puissant, mais difficile à lire."},{"label":"C","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"Bonjour\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","note":"Un langage des années 1970, toujours utilisé pour les systèmes d'exploitation et les objets connectés."},{"label":"JavaScript","code":"console.log(\"Bonjour\");","note":"Le langage des pages web : il tourne dans tous les navigateurs."},{"label":"Python","code":"print(\"Bonjour\")","note":"Une seule ligne, presque de l'anglais. C'est pour ça que Python est si populaire chez les débutants… et chez les scientifiques des données."}]}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Langage de haut niveau, de bas niveau\n\nOn classe les langages selon leur distance avec la machine :\n\n| | Bas niveau | Haut niveau |\n|---|---|---|\n| Exemples | Assembleur, C | Python, JavaScript |\n| Proche de | la machine | l'humain |\n| Facile à lire | non | oui |\n| Contrôle fin du matériel | oui | moins |\n\nAucun n'est « meilleur » : on choisit l'outil selon le travail. Pour analyser des données, Python est le choix numéro un dans le monde."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Quel est l'avantage principal d'un langage de haut niveau comme Python ?","options":["Il est plus proche des 0 et des 1","Il est plus facile à lire et à écrire pour un humain","Il n'a pas besoin d'être traduit"],"answer":1,"explain":"Un langage de haut niveau est fait pour les humains. Il doit quand même être traduit pour la machine, mais ce travail est fait automatiquement."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Le traducteur : l'interpréteur Python\n\nQui traduit ton code Python en langage machine ? Un programme appelé l'**interpréteur Python**.\n\nPense à un **interprète** dans une conférence internationale : il écoute une phrase et la traduit aussitôt, phrase après phrase. L'interpréteur Python fait pareil : il lit ton code **ligne par ligne, de haut en bas**, et exécute chaque ligne.\n\nConséquence importante : si la ligne 5 contient une erreur, les lignes 1 à 4 ont déjà été exécutées, et Python s'arrête à la ligne 5."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Ton programme fait 10 lignes. La ligne 7 contient une erreur. Que se passe-t-il ?","options":["Rien ne s'exécute","Les lignes 1 à 6 s'exécutent, puis Python s'arrête et signale l'erreur ligne 7","Python saute la ligne 7 et continue"],"answer":1,"explain":"Python lit et exécute de haut en bas. Il s'arrête à la première erreur et t'indique son numéro de ligne. Très pratique pour la retrouver."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"","v":"Bonne nouvelle : sur ce site, l'interpréteur Python tourne directement dans ta page web. Tu n'as rien à installer."}]]},{"id":"super-pouvoirs","title":"Les cinq super-pouvoirs du programmeur","subtitle":"Tous les programmes du monde sont construits avec ces briques.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Le secret le mieux gardé\n\nUne application bancaire, un jeu vidéo, le site d'une compagnie aérienne… Ils semblent très différents. Pourtant, ils sont tous fabriqués avec les mêmes cinq briques de base :\n\n1. la **séquence** : faire les choses dans l'ordre ;\n2. les **variables** : se souvenir de choses ;\n3. les **conditions** : prendre des décisions ;\n4. les **boucles** : répéter ;\n5. les **fonctions** : donner un nom à un savoir-faire pour le réutiliser.\n\nTu connais déjà les trois premières sans le savoir. Découvrons-les avec du vrai code Python. Pas besoin de tout comprendre : regarde surtout l'idée."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Séquence et variables\n\nUne **variable** est une boîte avec une étiquette. On y range une valeur pour s'en souvenir plus tard. Avance pas à pas et observe la mémoire de l'ordinateur."},{"t":"widget","name":"stepper","config":{"code":"prix = 500\nquantite = 3\ntotal = prix * quantite\nprint(total)","steps":[{"line":1,"vars":{},"note":"Python lit la ligne 1 : il va créer une boîte appelée prix."},{"line":2,"vars":{"prix":"500"},"note":"La boîte prix contient 500. Ligne 2 : une deuxième boîte."},{"line":3,"vars":{"prix":"500","quantite":"3"},"note":"Ligne 3 : Python lit le contenu des deux boîtes et les multiplie (* veut dire « fois »)."},{"line":4,"vars":{"prix":"500","quantite":"3","total":"1500"},"note":"Le résultat est rangé dans une nouvelle boîte, total. Ligne 4 : on l'affiche."},{"line":4,"vars":{"prix":"500","quantite":"3","total":"1500"},"out":"1500","note":"print affiche le contenu de total. Fin du programme !"}]}},{"t":"md","v":"Remarque la **séquence** : Python exécute les lignes une par une, de haut en bas. La ligne 3 a besoin de `prix` et `quantite` : elle doit venir après eux."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les conditions\n\nUne **condition** permet au programme de choisir ce qu'il fait selon la situation. En Python, on écrit `if` (« si »), `elif` (« sinon, si ») et `else` (« sinon »).\n\nFais glisser le curseur et regarde quelle partie du code est exécutée."},{"t":"widget","name":"condition","config":{"name":"temperature","label":"Température dehors :","min":-5,"max":42,"value":22,"branches":[{"code":"if temperature < 15:","lt":15,"out":"Il fait frais, prends une veste !"},{"code":"elif temperature < 30:","lt":30,"out":"Il fait bon, profite !"},{"code":"else:","out":"Il fait très chaud, bois beaucoup d'eau !"}]}},{"t":"md","v":"Le programme n'exécute **qu'une seule** des trois branches : la première dont la condition est vraie."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Avec ce programme, qu'affiche-t-on si `temperature` vaut exactement 30 ?","options":["Il fait frais, prends une veste !","Il fait bon, profite !","Il fait très chaud, bois beaucoup d'eau !"],"answer":2,"explain":"30 n'est pas plus petit que 15, ni plus petit que 30 (30 < 30 est faux). On arrive donc dans le `else`. Les cas « pile sur la limite » sont des pièges classiques !"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les boucles\n\nUne **boucle** répète des instructions. Ici, `for` répète la ligne décalée une fois pour chaque nombre de 1 à 3. Observe la variable `tour` changer à chaque passage."},{"t":"widget","name":"stepper","config":{"code":"for tour in range(1, 4):\n    print(\"Tour numéro\", tour)\nprint(\"Fini !\")","steps":[{"line":1,"vars":{},"note":"range(1, 4) produit les nombres 1, 2, 3 (le 4 est exclu)."},{"line":2,"vars":{"tour":"1"},"note":"Premier passage : tour vaut 1."},{"line":1,"vars":{"tour":"1"},"out":"Tour numéro 1","note":"On remonte en haut de la boucle pour le nombre suivant."},{"line":2,"vars":{"tour":"2"},"out":"Tour numéro 1","note":"Deuxième passage : tour vaut 2."},{"line":1,"vars":{"tour":"2"},"out":"Tour numéro 1\nTour numéro 2"},{"line":2,"vars":{"tour":"3"},"out":"Tour numéro 1\nTour numéro 2","note":"Troisième et dernier passage."},{"line":3,"vars":{"tour":"3"},"out":"Tour numéro 1\nTour numéro 2\nTour numéro 3","note":"Plus de nombres : on sort de la boucle et on continue après."},{"line":3,"vars":{"tour":"3"},"out":"Tour numéro 1\nTour numéro 2\nTour numéro 3\nFini !","note":"Pour répéter 1000 fois, il suffirait de changer un seul nombre."}]}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les fonctions\n\nUne **fonction** est un savoir-faire auquel on donne un nom. Pense à une machine à jus : tu mets des fruits (l'**entrée**), elle fait son travail, et elle te rend du jus (la **sortie**). Tu n'as pas besoin de savoir comment elle marche à l'intérieur.\n\nTu as déjà vu une fonction : `print`. Quelqu'un l'a écrite une fois pour toutes, et des millions de programmeurs l'utilisent chaque jour."},{"t":"code","code":"def prix_ttc(prix_ht):\n    return prix_ht * 1.18\n\nprint(prix_ttc(1000))\nprint(prix_ttc(2500))","run":false},{"t":"md","v":"Ici, on crée (avec `def`) une fonction qui ajoute 18 % de taxe à un prix. On l'utilise ensuite autant de fois qu'on veut."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Reconnais les super-pouvoirs"},{"t":"quiz","q":"Une application envoie un rappel « Bois de l'eau » **toutes les heures**. Quel super-pouvoir est en jeu ?","options":["Une variable","Une condition","Une boucle"],"answer":2,"explain":"Répéter la même action à intervalle régulier, c'est une **boucle**."},{"t":"quiz","q":"« **Si** le solde du compte est inférieur au montant, refuser le paiement. »","options":["Une condition","Une boucle","Une fonction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Le programme prend une décision selon la situation : c'est une **condition**."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Ton application de livraison retient l'adresse que tu as tapée pour l'afficher sur la carte. Elle utilise…","options":["une boucle","une variable","une fonction"],"answer":1,"explain":"Garder une valeur en mémoire pour s'en servir plus tard, c'est le rôle d'une **variable**."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## La vraie compétence : découper\n\nFace à un gros problème, un programmeur ne se lance pas tête baissée. Il le **découpe** en petits problèmes simples. Par exemple, « créer une application de livraison de repas » devient :\n\n- afficher la liste des restaurants (une boucle sur les restaurants) ;\n- retenir le panier du client (des variables) ;\n- vérifier que le paiement est accepté (une condition) ;\n- calculer le prix total (une fonction) ;\n- …\n\nChaque petit morceau devient facile. Cette façon de penser s'appelle la **pensée informatique**, et elle te servira même en dehors de la programmation."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"","v":"Séquence, variables, conditions, boucles, fonctions.\nTu as maintenant la carte du territoire. Le reste du cours, c'est le voyage."}]]},{"id":"python-data","title":"Python, les données et ton premier code","subtitle":"Pourquoi ce langage, pourquoi la data, et c'est parti.","duration":10,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Pourquoi Python ?\n\nIl existe des centaines de langages. Python est l'un des plus utilisés au monde, pour de bonnes raisons :\n\n- **Il se lit presque comme de l'anglais.** On passe moins de temps sur la syntaxe, plus sur le problème.\n- **Il sert partout** : sites web (Instagram), recherche scientifique (NASA), banques, intelligence artificielle, automatisation de tâches de bureau…\n- **C'est le roi de l'analyse de données.** Les bibliothèques pandas, NumPy et Matplotlib, que tu utiliseras à la fin du cours, sont des standards mondiaux.\n- **La communauté est immense.** Quelle que soit ta question, quelqu'un l'a déjà posée et quelqu'un y a répondu."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## La data, c'est quoi ?\n\nLes **données** (*data* en anglais), ce sont des informations enregistrées : les ventes d'une boutique, les transactions Mobile Money, les notes d'une classe, la météo de chaque jour, les trajets des bus…\n\nUn **analyste de données** transforme ces données brutes en réponses utiles. Son travail suit presque toujours le même chemin :"},{"t":"html","v":"<div class=\"flow\">\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Question</b><span>Quel produit vend le mieux le samedi ?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Collecte</b><span>Récupérer le fichier des ventes.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Nettoyage</b><span>Corriger les erreurs et les trous.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Analyse</b><span>Calculer, comparer, regrouper.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Visualisation</b><span>Un graphique clair.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Décision</b><span>Commander plus de ce produit.</span></div>\n</div>"},{"t":"quiz","q":"Une pharmacie veut savoir quels médicaments commander avant la saison des pluies. Quelle est la première étape ?","options":["Formuler une question précise","Faire un graphique","Supprimer les données anciennes"],"answer":0,"explain":"Tout commence par une **question** claire, par exemple : « Quels médicaments se sont le plus vendus pendant les saisons des pluies précédentes ? » Sans question, on se perd dans les données."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Ton parcours\n\nVoici le chemin que tu vas suivre :\n\n1. **Premiers pas** : afficher, commenter, comprendre les erreurs, utiliser des variables.\n2. **Les types** : nombres, textes, vrai et faux.\n3. **Décider et répéter** : conditions et boucles.\n4. **Ranger ses données** : listes et dictionnaires.\n5. **Fonctions, modules et fichiers** : du code réutilisable.\n6. **Analyser des données** : pandas et graphiques, jusqu'au projet final.\n\nChaque leçon dure environ dix minutes. Une par jour suffit pour progresser vite."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Ton tout premier programme Python\n\nVoici une ligne de vrai Python. Appuie sur **Exécuter** pour la lancer. La première fois, Python met quelques secondes à se réveiller."},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"Bonjour ! Je suis en train d'apprendre Python.\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`print` veut dire « affiche ». Le texte entre guillemets s'affiche tel quel.\n\nMaintenant, **modifie le texte** entre les guillemets (écris ce que tu veux) et exécute à nouveau. Tu peux casser le code, ce n'est pas grave : le bouton **Réinitialiser** remet l'original."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Ton premier exercice\n\nLes exercices sont corrigés automatiquement, comme par un prof avec son stylo rouge, mais instantanément."},{"t":"exercise","id":"python-data-ex1","title":"Présente-toi","prompt":"Écris un programme qui affiche une phrase contenant **ton prénom**, par exemple `Je m'appelle Awa`.\n\nN'oublie pas les guillemets autour du texte, et les parenthèses de `print`.","starter":"# Écris ta ligne sous ce commentaire","solution":"print(\"Je m'appelle Awa\")","check":"assert \"print\" in CODE, \"Utilise la fonction print() pour afficher ton texte.\"\nassert OUT.strip() != \"\", \"Ton programme n'affiche rien pour l'instant. Écris une ligne avec print(\\\"...\\\").\"\nassert len(OUT.strip()) >= 5, \"Écris une vraie phrase avec ton prénom.\"","hint":"Copie la forme de l'exemple précédent : print(\"ton texte ici\")","inputs":""},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"","v":"Ça y est, tu es programmeur ou programmeuse.\nPas encore expert, mais tu as écrit et exécuté du code. Au prochain chapitre, on passe aux choses sérieuses."}]]}]},{"id":"premiers-pas","icon":"🐍","title":"Premiers pas en Python","desc":"Afficher, commenter, lire les erreurs et ranger des valeurs dans des variables.","badge":"Pythonien débutant","lessons":[{"id":"print","title":"Afficher avec print()","subtitle":"La première instruction de tout programmeur.","duration":10,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Faire parler ton programme\n\n`print()` affiche quelque chose à l'écran. C'est la façon la plus simple de voir ce que fait ton programme. Tu l'utiliseras tout le temps, même quand tu seras expert, pour vérifier tes calculs."},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"Bienvenue dans le chapitre 2 !\")\nprint(\"Python lit les lignes de haut en bas.\")\nprint(\"Une ligne, un print, une ligne affichée.\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Trois `print`, trois lignes affichées, dans l'ordre. Essaie d'inverser deux lignes puis exécute."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Le texte entre guillemets\n\nLe texte qu'on affiche doit être entre **guillemets**. En programmation, un texte s'appelle une **chaîne de caractères** (*string* en anglais).\n\nTu peux utiliser des guillemets doubles `\"` ou simples `'`. Le plus pratique en français : les doubles, à cause des apostrophes."},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"C'est facile avec les guillemets doubles\")\nprint('Les guillemets simples marchent aussi')","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"Si tu écris `print('C'est cassé')`, Python croit que le texte s'arrête après le `C`. Résultat : une erreur. Avec des apostrophes dans ton texte, utilise des guillemets doubles."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les nombres, sans guillemets\n\nLes nombres s'écrivent **sans** guillemets. Et là, magie : Python peut calculer."},{"t":"code","code":"print(12 + 30)\nprint(\"12 + 30\")","run":true},{"t":"quiz","code":"print(7 * 6)","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["7 * 6","42","\"42\""],"answer":1,"explain":"Sans guillemets, Python calcule `7 * 6` (le signe `*` veut dire « multiplié par ») et affiche le résultat : 42."},{"t":"quiz","code":"print(\"7 * 6\")","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["7 * 6","42","Une erreur"],"answer":0,"explain":"Avec des guillemets, c'est du texte : Python l'affiche tel quel, sans rien calculer."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Plusieurs choses à la fois\n\nTu peux donner plusieurs éléments à `print`, séparés par des **virgules**. Python les affiche sur la même ligne, séparés par un espace."},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"J'ai\", 25, \"ans\")\nprint(\"Une semaine compte\", 7 * 24, \"heures\")\nprint()\nprint(\"Le print() vide ci-dessus a affiché une ligne blanche.\")","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"","v":"La virgule permet de mélanger texte et nombres dans un seul `print`. Python ajoute les espaces tout seul."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : un ticket de caisse"},{"t":"exercise","id":"print-ex1","title":"Ton premier ticket","prompt":"Écris un programme qui affiche **trois lignes** :\n\n1. `Café Baobab`\n2. `2 cafés : 5 euros`\n3. `Merci et à bientôt !`\n\nRespecte exactement le texte (majuscules, accents et ponctuation compris).","starter":"# Une ligne par print","solution":"print(\"Café Baobab\")\nprint(\"2 cafés : 5 euros\")\nprint(\"Merci et à bientôt !\")","check":"lignes = [l.strip() for l in OUT.strip().split(\"\\n\")]\nassert len(lignes) == 3, f\"Il faut exactement 3 lignes affichées, ton programme en affiche {len(lignes)}.\"\nassert lignes[0] == \"Café Baobab\", f\"La première ligne doit être « Café Baobab », pas « {lignes[0]} ».\"\nassert lignes[1] == \"2 cafés : 5 euros\", f\"La deuxième ligne doit être « 2 cafés : 5 euros », pas « {lignes[1]} ».\"\nassert lignes[2] == \"Merci et à bientôt !\", f\"La troisième ligne doit être « Merci et à bientôt ! », pas « {lignes[2]} ».\"","hint":"Il te faut trois instructions print, une par ligne, avec chaque texte entre guillemets.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : faire calculer Python"},{"t":"exercise","id":"print-ex2","title":"Combien de minutes ?","prompt":"Une journée compte 24 heures, et une heure 60 minutes. Affiche la phrase :\n\n`Une journée compte 1440 minutes`\n\nMais **ne calcule pas toi-même** : écris `24 * 60` dans ton `print` et laisse Python faire le calcul.","starter":"print(\"Une journée compte\", ...)","solution":"print(\"Une journée compte\", 24 * 60, \"minutes\")","check":"assert \"24 * 60\" in CODE.replace(\"24*60\", \"24 * 60\") or \"60 * 24\" in CODE.replace(\"60*24\", \"60 * 24\"), \"Écris le calcul 24 * 60 dans ton code au lieu du résultat.\"\nassert OUT.strip() == \"Une journée compte 1440 minutes\", f\"Le programme doit afficher « Une journée compte 1440 minutes ». Il affiche : « {OUT.strip()} »\"","hint":"Remplace les ... par le calcul, puis ajoute une virgule et le texte \"minutes\".","inputs":""},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"","v":"Bien joué. Tu sais maintenant afficher du texte, des nombres et des calculs. C'est déjà la moitié de ce que fait un programme : montrer des résultats."}]]},{"id":"erreurs","title":"Commentaires et erreurs","subtitle":"Écrire des notes, et ne plus avoir peur des messages rouges.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Les commentaires\n\nTout ce qui suit un `#` sur une ligne est un **commentaire** : Python l'ignore complètement. C'est une note pour les humains, y compris pour toi dans trois mois."},{"t":"code","code":"# Ce programme calcule le prix d'une commande\nprix_unitaire = 3      # en euros\nprint(prix_unitaire * 4)   # 4 cappuccinos\n# print(\"Cette ligne ne s'exécute pas, elle est commentée\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Commenter une ligne (mettre un `#` devant) est aussi un réflexe pour **désactiver** du code temporairement, sans l'effacer."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Que fait Python avec la ligne `# print(\"Bonjour\")` ?","options":["Il affiche Bonjour","Rien du tout : c'est un commentaire","Il affiche une erreur"],"answer":1,"explain":"Le `#` transforme toute la ligne en commentaire. Python ne l'exécute pas."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les erreurs sont tes alliées\n\nTu vas faire des erreurs. Beaucoup. Tout le monde en fait, tous les jours, y compris les développeurs de Google. La différence entre un débutant et un pro : le pro **lit le message d'erreur**.\n\nExécute ce code. Il contient une erreur volontaire."},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"Bonjour)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Regarde le message. Il te dit trois choses :\n\n1. **Le type d'erreur** : `SyntaxError`, une faute de « grammaire ».\n2. **La ligne** où Python a bloqué.\n3. **Une explication** : ici, une chaîne de caractères qui n'est jamais fermée.\n\nCorrige le code (ajoute le guillemet manquant) et exécute à nouveau."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les trois erreurs les plus fréquentes\n\n**1. NameError : un nom inconnu.** Python ne connaît pas ce mot. Souvent une faute de frappe, ou une majuscule en trop : pour Python, `print` et `Print` sont deux mots différents."},{"t":"code","code":"Print(\"Python fait la différence entre majuscules et minuscules\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"**2. Oublier les guillemets.** Sans guillemets, Python croit que `Bonjour` est le nom de quelque chose."},{"t":"code","code":"print(Bonjour)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"**3. IndentationError : un décalage en trop.** En Python, les espaces en début de ligne ont un sens. Une ligne ne doit pas être décalée sans raison."},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"Ligne 1\")\n  print(\"Ligne 2\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Corrige ces trois exemples un par un."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Devine le résultat"},{"t":"quiz","code":"print(\"A\")\nprint(\"B\")\nprnt(\"C\")\nprint(\"D\")","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["Rien ne s'affiche","A et B s'affichent, puis une erreur NameError","A, B et D s'affichent"],"answer":1,"explain":"Python exécute de haut en bas. Les lignes 1 et 2 fonctionnent. À la ligne 3, `prnt` est inconnu : erreur, et le programme s'arrête. La ligne 4 n'est jamais exécutée."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Pour Python, `Print(\"x\")` et `print(\"x\")`…","options":["c'est pareil","ce n'est pas pareil : Python distingue majuscules et minuscules","les deux donnent une erreur"],"answer":1,"explain":"Python est **sensible à la casse** : `Print`, `PRINT` et `print` sont trois noms différents. Seul `print` existe."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## La méthode du détective\n\nFace à une erreur, suis toujours ces étapes :\n\n1. **Lis le type d'erreur** et l'explication.\n2. **Va à la ligne indiquée** (et regarde aussi la ligne juste avant : l'erreur vient souvent de là).\n3. **Compare** avec un exemple qui marche.\n4. **Change une seule chose** à la fois, puis relance."},{"t":"exercise","id":"erreurs-ex1","title":"Le code cassé","prompt":"Ce programme contient **trois erreurs**. Corrige-les pour qu'il affiche :\n\n    Je débogue\n    comme un pro\n    3 erreurs corrigées\n\nExécute-le d'abord pour lire les messages d'erreur, un par un.","starter":"print(\"Je débogue)\nPrint(\"comme un pro\")\n print(3, \"erreurs corrigées\")","solution":"print(\"Je débogue\")\nprint(\"comme un pro\")\nprint(3, \"erreurs corrigées\")","check":"attendu = [\"Je débogue\", \"comme un pro\", \"3 erreurs corrigées\"]\nlignes = [l.strip() for l in OUT.strip().split(\"\\n\")]\nassert lignes == attendu, \"Le résultat n'est pas encore exactement celui demandé. Compare ligne par ligne.\"","hint":"Un guillemet manquant, une majuscule en trop, un espace au début d'une ligne.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"variables","title":"Les variables","subtitle":"Des boîtes avec une étiquette pour ranger tes données.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Une boîte avec une étiquette\n\nUne **variable** est un nom qu'on donne à une valeur pour s'en souvenir et la réutiliser. Imagine une boîte : l'étiquette est le **nom**, le contenu est la **valeur**."},{"t":"code","code":"prenom = \"Awa\"\nage = 24\nprint(prenom)\nprint(age)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Le signe `=` ne veut pas dire « égal » comme en maths. Il veut dire **« range la valeur de droite dans la boîte de gauche »**. On dit qu'on **affecte** une valeur à une variable."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Utiliser une variable\n\nUne fois créée, la variable s'utilise à la place de sa valeur. Pas de guillemets autour du nom : sinon c'est du texte !"},{"t":"code","code":"ville = \"Abidjan\"\nprint(\"J'habite à\", ville)\nprint(\"ville\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"L'intérêt ? Si la ville change, tu modifies **une seule ligne** et tout le programme suit. Change `\"Abidjan\"` par ta ville et exécute."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Une variable peut changer\n\nUne variable peut recevoir une nouvelle valeur : l'ancienne est remplacée. Observe la mémoire pas à pas."},{"t":"widget","name":"stepper","config":{"code":"score = 0\nscore = 10\nscore = score + 5\nprint(score)","steps":[{"line":1,"vars":{},"note":"On crée la boîte score."},{"line":2,"vars":{"score":"0"},"note":"score vaut 0. On va le remplacer par 10."},{"line":3,"vars":{"score":"10"},"note":"Python calcule d'abord la droite : score + 5, soit 10 + 5 = 15…"},{"line":4,"vars":{"score":"15"},"note":"…puis range 15 dans score. L'ancienne valeur a disparu."},{"line":4,"vars":{"score":"15"},"out":"15","note":"score = score + 5 est une ligne très courante : « augmente score de 5 »."}]}},{"t":"quiz","code":"pieces = 3\npieces = pieces * 2\npieces = pieces - 1\nprint(pieces)","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["3","6","5"],"answer":2,"explain":"3, puis 3 × 2 = 6, puis 6 − 1 = 5. Chaque ligne part de la valeur précédente."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Calculer avec des variables\n\nLes variables rendent les calculs lisibles. Compare `print(4 * 3.5)` avec ceci :"},{"t":"code","code":"nombre_de_cafes = 4\nprix_du_cafe = 3.5\ntotal = nombre_de_cafes * prix_du_cafe\nprint(\"Total à payer :\", total)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Le code se lit presque comme une phrase. C'est ça, un bon programme : **compréhensible par un humain**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"info","title":"","v":"En Python, les nombres à virgule s'écrivent avec un **point** : `3.5` et pas `3,5`. C'est la notation anglaise."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Bien nommer ses variables\n\nPython impose quelques règles :\n\n- uniquement des lettres, des chiffres et le tiret bas `_` ;\n- pas d'espace, pas de tiret `-` (Python croirait à une soustraction) ;\n- ne pas commencer par un chiffre ;\n- majuscules et minuscules comptent : `age` et `Age` sont deux variables différentes.\n\nEt une convention : on écrit les noms en minuscules, avec des `_` entre les mots. C'est le style **snake_case** (« serpent »). Exemple : `prix_total`, `nombre_eleves`."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Lequel de ces noms de variable est **valide** en Python ?","options":["`prix total`","`2eme_prix`","`prix_total`","`prix-total`"],"answer":2,"explain":"`prix total` contient un espace, `2eme_prix` commence par un chiffre, `prix-total` contient un tiret. Seul `prix_total` respecte les règles."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"","v":"Choisis des noms qui disent ce que contient la boîte. `x` et `truc` ne veulent rien dire ; `note_moyenne` se comprend tout seul."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : ta fiche"},{"t":"exercise","id":"variables-ex1","title":"Ta fiche d'identité","prompt":"Crée trois variables :\n\n- `prenom` qui contient ton prénom (du texte) ;\n- `ville` qui contient ta ville (du texte) ;\n- `age` qui contient ton âge (un nombre, **sans guillemets**).\n\nPuis affiche une phrase qui les utilise, par exemple : `Awa habite à Dakar et a 24 ans`.","starter":"prenom = \nville = \nage = \n\nprint(prenom, \"habite à\", ...)","solution":"prenom = \"Awa\"\nville = \"Dakar\"\nage = 24\n\nprint(prenom, \"habite à\", ville, \"et a\", age, \"ans\")","check":"for nom in (\"prenom\", \"ville\", \"age\"):\n    assert nom in dir(), f\"La variable « {nom} » n'existe pas. Crée-la avec {nom} = ...\"\nassert isinstance(prenom, str) and prenom.strip(), \"prenom doit contenir du texte entre guillemets.\"\nassert isinstance(ville, str) and ville.strip(), \"ville doit contenir du texte entre guillemets.\"\nassert isinstance(age, int), \"age doit être un nombre entier, écrit sans guillemets (par exemple age = 24).\"\nassert prenom in OUT and ville in OUT and str(age) in OUT, \"La phrase affichée doit contenir ton prénom, ta ville et ton âge (utilise les variables dans le print).\"\nassert \"...\" not in CODE, \"Il reste des ... dans ton code : remplace-les.\"","hint":"Pour le texte, mets des guillemets : prenom = \"Awa\". Pour l'âge, pas de guillemets : age = 24. Dans le print, sépare chaque morceau par une virgule.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : la monnaie"},{"t":"exercise","id":"variables-ex2","title":"Rendre la monnaie","prompt":"Une cliente achète 3 croissants à 1.8 euro pièce et paie avec un billet de 10 euros.\n\n1. Crée une variable `total` qui calcule le prix des 3 croissants (avec un calcul, pas en l'écrivant à la main).\n2. Crée une variable `monnaie` qui calcule ce qu'on doit lui rendre.\n3. Affiche `monnaie`.","starter":"prix_croissant = 1.8\nquantite = 3\nbillet = 10\n\ntotal = \nmonnaie = \nprint(monnaie)","solution":"prix_croissant = 1.8\nquantite = 3\nbillet = 10\n\ntotal = prix_croissant * quantite\nmonnaie = billet - total\nprint(monnaie)","check":"assert \"total\" in dir() and \"monnaie\" in dir(), \"Il faut créer les variables total et monnaie.\"\nassert abs(total - 5.4) < 1e-9, f\"total devrait valoir 3 × 1.8 = 5.4, il vaut {total}.\"\nassert abs(monnaie - 4.6) < 1e-9, f\"monnaie devrait valoir 10 − 5.4 = 4.6, elle vaut {monnaie}.\"\nassert \"prix_croissant\" in CODE.split(\"total =\")[1] if \"total =\" in CODE else True, \"Calcule total à partir des variables prix_croissant et quantite.\"","hint":"total = prix_croissant * quantite. Ensuite, la monnaie, c'est le billet moins le total.","inputs":""},{"t":"callout","kind":"info","title":"","v":"Tu as peut-être vu s'afficher `4.6` ou un nombre bizarre comme `4.6000000000000005`. Ce n'est pas un bug de ton code : les ordinateurs stockent les nombres à virgule de façon approximative. On verra comment arrondir au prochain chapitre."}]]}]},{"id":"types","icon":"🔢","title":"Nombres, textes et booléens","desc":"Les types de données de base : calculer, manipuler du texte, dire vrai ou faux.","badge":"Maître des types","lessons":[{"id":"nombres","title":"Les nombres et les calculs","subtitle":"Python, la meilleure calculatrice du monde.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Deux sortes de nombres\n\nPython distingue deux types de nombres :\n\n- les **entiers** (`int`, pour *integer*) : `7`, `-3`, `2026` ;\n- les **nombres à virgule** (`float`, pour *nombre flottant*) : `3.5`, `-0.25`, `20.0`.\n\nLa fonction `type()` te dit de quel type est une valeur."},{"t":"code","code":"print(type(42))\nprint(type(3.14))\nprint(type(\"42\"))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Attention au dernier : `\"42\"` entre guillemets est du **texte** (`str`), pas un nombre."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les opérations\n\n| Symbole | Opération | Exemple | Résultat |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `+` | addition | `7 + 2` | `9` |\n| `-` | soustraction | `7 - 2` | `5` |\n| `*` | multiplication | `7 * 2` | `14` |\n| `/` | division | `7 / 2` | `3.5` |\n| `//` | division entière | `7 // 2` | `3` |\n| `%` | reste (modulo) | `7 % 2` | `1` |\n| `**` | puissance | `7 ** 2` | `49` |"},{"t":"code","code":"print(7 / 2)\nprint(7 // 2)\nprint(7 % 2)\nprint(2 ** 10)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`//` et `%` vont ensemble : 7 divisé par 2, ça fait 3, **reste** 1. Comme à l'école primaire !"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## À quoi sert le reste ?\n\nLe modulo `%` est bien plus utile qu'il n'y paraît :\n\n- un nombre est **pair** si `nombre % 2` vaut 0 ;\n- convertir des minutes en heures : `135 // 60` donne 2 heures, `135 % 60` donne 15 minutes."},{"t":"code","code":"minutes = 135\nheures = minutes // 60\nreste = minutes % 60\nprint(minutes, \"minutes =\", heures, \"h\", reste, \"min\")","run":true},{"t":"quiz","code":"print(17 % 5)","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["3","2","3.4"],"answer":1,"explain":"17 divisé par 5 : 3 fois (15), il reste 2. `17 // 5` vaut 3 et `17 % 5` vaut 2."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## L'ordre des opérations\n\nComme en maths, la multiplication passe avant l'addition. Les **parenthèses** imposent l'ordre que tu veux."},{"t":"quiz","code":"print(2 + 3 * 4)","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["20","14","24"],"answer":1,"explain":"`3 * 4` d'abord (12), puis `2 + 12` = 14. Pour obtenir 20, il faut écrire `(2 + 3) * 4`."},{"t":"code","code":"note1 = 12\nnote2 = 15\nnote3 = 17\nmoyenne_fausse = note1 + note2 + note3 / 3\nmoyenne = (note1 + note2 + note3) / 3\nprint(moyenne_fausse)\nprint(moyenne)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Le premier calcul est faux : seule `note3` a été divisée par 3. Un classique !"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Arrondir\n\n`round(nombre, chiffres)` arrondit un nombre. Sans le deuxième paramètre, on arrondit à l'entier."},{"t":"code","code":"moyenne = 14.666666666\nprint(round(moyenne))\nprint(round(moyenne, 1))\nprint(round(moyenne, 2))\nprint(round(0.1 + 0.2, 2))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Quelques autres fonctions utiles : `abs()` donne la valeur absolue, `min()` et `max()` le plus petit et le plus grand."},{"t":"code","code":"print(abs(-12))\nprint(min(4, 9, 2))\nprint(max(4, 9, 2))","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : la durée du film"},{"t":"exercise","id":"nombres-ex1","title":"Heures et minutes","prompt":"Un film dure `duree = 158` minutes. Calcule avec `//` et `%` :\n\n- `heures` : le nombre d'heures complètes ;\n- `minutes` : les minutes restantes.\n\nPuis affiche `2 h 38 min` (avec un `print` qui utilise tes deux variables).","starter":"duree = 158\n\nheures = \nminutes = \nprint(heures, \"h\", minutes, \"min\")","solution":"duree = 158\n\nheures = duree // 60\nminutes = duree % 60\nprint(heures, \"h\", minutes, \"min\")","check":"assert heures == 2, f\"heures devrait valoir 2, il vaut {heures}.\"\nassert minutes == 38, f\"minutes devrait valoir 38, il vaut {minutes}.\"\nassert \"duree\" in CODE.split(\"heures =\")[1].split(\"\\n\")[0], \"Calcule heures à partir de la variable duree (pas en écrivant 2 directement).\"\nassert OUT.strip() == \"2 h 38 min\", f\"L'affichage attendu est « 2 h 38 min », obtenu « {OUT.strip()} ».\"","hint":"Une heure compte 60 minutes. duree // 60 donne les heures, duree % 60 le reste.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : la moyenne"},{"t":"exercise","id":"nombres-ex2","title":"Moyenne du trimestre","prompt":"Awa a eu trois notes sur 20 : `13.5`, `16` et `11`.\n\nCalcule sa moyenne dans une variable `moyenne`, **arrondie à 2 chiffres après la virgule**, puis affiche-la.","starter":"note1 = 13.5\nnote2 = 16\nnote3 = 11\n\nmoyenne = \nprint(moyenne)","solution":"note1 = 13.5\nnote2 = 16\nnote3 = 11\n\nmoyenne = round((note1 + note2 + note3) / 3, 2)\nprint(moyenne)","check":"assert moyenne == 13.5, f\"La moyenne arrondie devrait être 13.5 (40.5 / 3), tu obtiens {moyenne}. As-tu mis des parenthèses autour de la somme ?\"\nassert \"round\" in CODE, \"Utilise round(..., 2) pour arrondir.\"\nassert \"13.5\" in OUT, \"Affiche la moyenne avec print(moyenne).\"","hint":"N'oublie pas les parenthèses autour de la somme, puis entoure le tout avec round(..., 2).","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"textes","title":"Le texte et les f-strings","subtitle":"Assembler, nettoyer et découper des chaînes de caractères.","duration":14,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Coller des textes\n\nLe `+` entre deux textes les **colle** l'un à l'autre (on dit *concaténer*). Le `*` répète un texte."},{"t":"code","code":"prenom = \"Moussa\"\nsalut = \"Bonjour \" + prenom + \" !\"\nprint(salut)\nprint(\"Ha\" * 3)\nprint(\"-\" * 20)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Mais attention : on ne peut pas coller du texte et un nombre avec `+`."},{"t":"code","code":"age = 30\nprint(\"J'ai \" + age + \" ans\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Python refuse (`TypeError`) : il ne sait pas s'il doit additionner ou coller. Il faut convertir le nombre en texte avec `str(age)`. Corrige le code ci-dessus."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les f-strings : la méthode moderne\n\nIl existe une façon bien plus pratique : les **f-strings**. On met un `f` devant les guillemets, et on écrit les variables **entre accolades** `{ }` directement dans le texte."},{"t":"code","code":"prenom = \"Moussa\"\nage = 30\nville = \"Kinshasa\"\nprint(f\"Je m'appelle {prenom}, j'ai {age} ans et j'habite à {ville}.\")\nprint(f\"Dans 10 ans, j'aurai {age + 10} ans.\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"On peut même faire des calculs dans les accolades, et formater les nombres. `:.2f` affiche 2 chiffres après la virgule :"},{"t":"code","code":"prix = 7.5\nquantite = 3\nprint(f\"Total : {prix * quantite:.2f} euros\")","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"","v":"Les f-strings sont la façon la plus utilisée d'afficher des résultats en Python. Tu les verras partout, surtout en analyse de données."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les méthodes de texte\n\nUn texte possède des **méthodes** : des actions qu'on lui applique avec un point. Elles renvoient un nouveau texte."},{"t":"code","code":"nom = \"  aWa diallo  \"\nprint(nom.upper())\nprint(nom.lower())\nprint(nom.strip())\nprint(nom.strip().title())\nprint(\"café au lait\".replace(\"lait\", \"miel\"))\nprint(\"banane\".count(\"a\"))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"| Méthode | Effet |\n|---|---|\n| `.upper()` | tout en majuscules |\n| `.lower()` | tout en minuscules |\n| `.title()` | Majuscule À Chaque Mot |\n| `.strip()` | enlève les espaces au début et à la fin |\n| `.replace(a, b)` | remplace a par b |\n| `.count(x)` | compte les x |\n\nCes méthodes sont essentielles pour **nettoyer des données** : dans un vrai fichier, tu trouveras « Dakar », « dakar » et « DAKAR  » qui désignent la même ville."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Longueur, positions et découpage\n\n`len()` donne le nombre de caractères. Chaque caractère a une **position** (un *index*), et on commence à compter à **0**."},{"t":"code","code":"mot = \"Python\"\nprint(len(mot))\nprint(mot[0])\nprint(mot[1])\nprint(mot[-1])\nprint(mot[0:3])\nprint(mot[2:])","run":true},{"t":"html","v":"<div class=\"flow\" aria-label=\"Positions des lettres du mot Python\">\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>P</b><span>0 ou -6</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>y</b><span>1 ou -5</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>t</b><span>2 ou -4</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>h</b><span>3 ou -3</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>o</b><span>4 ou -2</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>n</b><span>5 ou -1</span></div>\n</div>"},{"t":"md","v":"`mot[0:3]` prend de la position 0 **jusqu'à 3 exclu** : `Pyt`. Les positions négatives partent de la fin."},{"t":"quiz","code":"ville = \"Abidjan\"\nprint(ville[0] + ville[-1])","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["Ab","An","Aj"],"answer":1,"explain":"`ville[0]` est le premier caractère (A), `ville[-1]` le dernier (n). Collés : « An »."},{"t":"md","v":"Pour savoir si un texte en contient un autre, on utilise `in` :"},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"jus\" in \"jus de bissap\")\nprint(\"thé\" in \"café\")","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : le badge"},{"t":"exercise","id":"textes-ex1","title":"Le badge de conférence","prompt":"Les variables `prenom` et `entreprise` contiennent les informations d'un participant. Avec **une f-string**, affiche exactement :\n\n`Bienvenue Grace (Orange Digital) !`","starter":"prenom = \"Grace\"\nentreprise = \"Orange Digital\"\n\nprint(f\"...\")","solution":"prenom = \"Grace\"\nentreprise = \"Orange Digital\"\n\nprint(f\"Bienvenue {prenom} ({entreprise}) !\")","check":"assert 'f\"' in CODE or \"f'\" in CODE, \"Utilise une f-string : f\\\"...\\\".\"\nassert OUT.strip() == \"Bienvenue Grace (Orange Digital) !\", f\"Attendu « Bienvenue Grace (Orange Digital) ! », obtenu « {OUT.strip()} ».\"\nassert \"{prenom}\" in CODE.replace(\" \", \"\"), \"Utilise la variable prenom entre accolades plutôt que d'écrire Grace à la main.\"","hint":"Dans une f-string, écris les variables entre accolades : {prenom}. Les parenthèses autour de l'entreprise sont du simple texte.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : nettoyer un nom"},{"t":"exercise","id":"textes-ex2","title":"Un nom tout propre","prompt":"Un formulaire a été mal rempli : `brut = \"   kOFI aNNAN   \"`.\n\nCrée une variable `propre` qui contient `Kofi Annan` (sans espaces autour, avec une majuscule à chaque mot) en utilisant des méthodes de texte, puis crée `initiales` qui contient `KA` en prenant la première lettre de chaque mot.","starter":"brut = \"   kOFI aNNAN   \"\n\npropre = \nprint(propre)\n\ninitiales = \nprint(initiales)","solution":"brut = \"   kOFI aNNAN   \"\n\npropre = brut.strip().title()\nprint(propre)\n\ninitiales = propre[0] + propre[5]\nprint(initiales)","check":"assert propre == \"Kofi Annan\", f\"propre devrait valoir « Kofi Annan », il vaut « {propre} ».\"\nassert initiales == \"KA\", f\"initiales devrait valoir « KA », il vaut « {initiales} ».\"\nassert \"Kofi Annan\" not in CODE, \"Calcule propre à partir de brut avec des méthodes, sans recopier le résultat.\"","hint":"Enchaîne les méthodes : brut.strip().title(). Pour les initiales, « Kofi Annan » : le K est en position 0, le A en position 5.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"booleens","title":"Vrai ou faux, et conversions","subtitle":"Les booléens, les comparaisons et la fonction input().","duration":14,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Seulement deux valeurs\n\nUn **booléen** (`bool`) ne peut valoir que deux choses : `True` (vrai) ou `False` (faux). Avec une majuscule, sans guillemets.\n\nOn les obtient surtout en **comparant** des valeurs :\n\n| Symbole | Signification |\n|---|---|\n| `==` | est égal à |\n| `!=` | est différent de |\n| `<` / `>` | plus petit / plus grand |\n| `<=` / `>=` | plus petit ou égal / plus grand ou égal |"},{"t":"code","code":"age = 17\nprint(age >= 18)\nprint(age == 17)\nprint(\"Dakar\" == \"dakar\")\nprint(10 != 3)","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"Ne confonds pas `=` et `==`. Un seul `=` **range** une valeur dans une variable. Deux `==` **posent une question** : « est-ce égal ? »."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Combiner : and, or, not\n\n- `and` (et) : vrai si **les deux** conditions sont vraies ;\n- `or` (ou) : vrai si **au moins une** est vraie ;\n- `not` (non) : inverse vrai et faux."},{"t":"code","code":"age = 22\na_un_billet = True\nprint(age >= 18 and a_un_billet)\nprint(age < 12 or age > 65)\nprint(not a_un_billet)","run":true},{"t":"quiz","q":"Une promo s'applique si le client a une carte de fidélité **ou** si son panier dépasse 50 euros. Le client n'a pas de carte et son panier fait 60 euros. A-t-il la promo ?","options":["Oui","Non"],"answer":0,"explain":"`False or True` donne `True` : avec `or`, une seule condition vraie suffit."},{"t":"quiz","code":"x = 5\nprint(x > 1 and x < 4)","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["True","False","5"],"answer":1,"explain":"`x > 1` est vrai, mais `x < 4` est faux. Avec `and`, il faut que les deux soient vraies : le résultat est `False`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Convertir d'un type à l'autre\n\nOn passe souvent d'un type à un autre :\n\n- `int(\"25\")` transforme le texte `\"25\"` en nombre entier 25 ;\n- `float(\"3.5\")` donne le nombre à virgule 3.5 ;\n- `str(25)` donne le texte `\"25\"`."},{"t":"code","code":"texte = \"25\"\nnombre = int(texte)\nprint(texte * 2)\nprint(nombre * 2)\nprint(type(texte), type(nombre))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Même valeur affichée, comportements très différents : le texte se répète, le nombre se multiplie. Et si la conversion est impossible, Python le dit :"},{"t":"code","code":"int(\"vingt-cinq\")","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Demander une valeur avec input()\n\n`input()` pose une question à l'utilisateur et attend sa réponse. **La réponse est toujours du texte**, même si on tape un nombre.\n\nSur ce site, comme il n'y a pas de clavier « en direct » pendant l'exécution, tu tapes tes réponses à l'avance dans la case **Entrées** sous l'éditeur, une par ligne. Exécute, puis remplace les valeurs par ton prénom et ton année de naissance."},{"t":"code","code":"prenom = input(\"Comment t'appelles-tu ? \")\nannee = input(\"En quelle année es-tu né(e) ? \")\nage = 2026 - int(annee)\nprint(f\"Enchanté {prenom}, tu as (ou vas avoir) {age} ans cette année.\")","run":true,"inputs":"Awa\n2001"},{"t":"md","v":"Sans le `int(annee)`, la soustraction serait impossible : on ne soustrait pas un texte à un nombre."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : peut-il voter ?"},{"t":"exercise","id":"booleens-ex1","title":"Le droit de vote","prompt":"À partir de la variable `age`, crée une variable `peut_voter` qui vaut `True` si l'âge est supérieur **ou égal** à 18, et `False` sinon. Affiche-la.\n\nTon code doit fonctionner pour n'importe quel âge : n'écris pas `True` à la main, utilise une comparaison.","starter":"age = 20\n\npeut_voter = \nprint(peut_voter)","solution":"age = 20\n\npeut_voter = age >= 18\nprint(peut_voter)","check":"assert isinstance(peut_voter, bool), \"peut_voter doit être un booléen (True ou False), obtenu avec une comparaison.\"\nassert \">=\" in CODE or \">\" in CODE, \"Utilise une comparaison avec age.\"\ncode_test = CODE.replace(\"age = 20\", \"age = 18\", 1)\nns = {}\nexec(code_test.replace(\"print(\", \"(lambda *a, **k: None)(\"), ns)\nassert ns[\"peut_voter\"] is True, \"Avec age = 18, peut_voter devrait être True (18 ans, c'est « supérieur ou égal »).\"\nns = {}\nexec(CODE.replace(\"age = 20\", \"age = 15\", 1).replace(\"print(\", \"(lambda *a, **k: None)(\"), ns)\nassert ns[\"peut_voter\"] is False, \"Avec age = 15, peut_voter devrait être False.\"","hint":"Une comparaison donne directement True ou False : peut_voter = age >= 18","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : la caisse"},{"t":"exercise","id":"booleens-ex2","title":"La caisse enregistreuse","prompt":"Le programme demande le **prix** d'un article puis la **quantité** avec `input()`. Les réponses sont déjà dans la case Entrées : `2.5` et `4`.\n\nConvertis-les correctement (le prix peut avoir une virgule, la quantité est un entier), calcule le total et affiche :\n\n`Total : 10.0 euros`","starter":"prix = input(\"Prix de l'article ? \")\nquantite = input(\"Quantité ? \")\n\ntotal = prix * quantite\nprint(f\"Total : {total} euros\")","solution":"prix = float(input(\"Prix de l'article ? \"))\nquantite = int(input(\"Quantité ? \"))\n\ntotal = prix * quantite\nprint(f\"Total : {total} euros\")","check":"assert \"Total : 10.0 euros\" in OUT, \"Le programme doit afficher « Total : 10.0 euros » avec les entrées 2.5 et 4.\"\nassert \"float(\" in CODE, \"Le prix doit être converti avec float().\"\nassert \"int(\" in CODE, \"La quantité doit être convertie avec int().\"","hint":"input() renvoie toujours du texte. Entoure-le de float(...) pour le prix et de int(...) pour la quantité.","inputs":"2.5\n4"}]]}]},{"id":"logique","icon":"🔀","title":"Décider et répéter","desc":"Les conditions et les boucles : le moment où ton programme commence à réfléchir.","badge":"Logicien","lessons":[{"id":"conditions","title":"Prendre des décisions avec if","subtitle":"if, elif, else et l'indentation.","duration":14,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Si… alors…\n\nAvec `if`, ton programme exécute un bloc de code **seulement si** une condition est vraie."},{"t":"code","code":"solde = 15000\nprix = 12000\n\nif solde >= prix:\n    print(\"Paiement accepté\")\n    solde = solde - prix\n    print(\"Nouveau solde :\", solde)\n\nprint(\"Merci de votre visite\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Trois détails essentiels :\n\n1. la ligne du `if` se termine par **deux-points** `:` ;\n2. les lignes du bloc sont **décalées de 4 espaces** (on dit *indentées*) ;\n3. la première ligne non décalée marque la **fin du bloc** : `Merci de votre visite` s'affiche dans tous les cas.\n\nChange le prix en `20000` et exécute à nouveau."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Sinon : else\n\n`else` (« sinon ») indique quoi faire quand la condition est fausse."},{"t":"code","code":"solde = 15000\nprix = 20000\n\nif solde >= prix:\n    print(\"Paiement accepté\")\nelse:\n    manque = prix - solde\n    print(f\"Solde insuffisant, il manque {manque} francs\")","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"L'indentation n'est pas décorative en Python : c'est elle qui dit quelles lignes font partie du bloc. Utilise toujours 4 espaces (la touche Tab le fait pour toi dans l'éditeur)."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Plusieurs cas : elif\n\n`elif` (contraction de *else if*, « sinon, si ») permet d'enchaîner plusieurs conditions. Python les teste **dans l'ordre** et exécute **seulement le premier** bloc dont la condition est vraie."},{"t":"widget","name":"condition","config":{"name":"note","label":"Note sur 20 :","min":0,"max":20,"value":13,"branches":[{"code":"if note >= 16:","gte":16,"out":"Mention Très bien"},{"code":"elif note >= 14:","gte":14,"out":"Mention Bien"},{"code":"elif note >= 12:","gte":12,"out":"Mention Assez bien"},{"code":"elif note >= 10:","gte":10,"out":"Admis"},{"code":"else:","out":"Pas encore admis"}]}},{"t":"quiz","code":"note = 17\nif note >= 10:\n    print(\"Admis\")\nelif note >= 16:\n    print(\"Très bien\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Pas admis\")","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["Admis","Très bien","Admis, puis Très bien"],"answer":0,"explain":"17 >= 10 est vrai : Python exécute le premier bloc et **ignore tous les autres**. L'ordre des conditions compte : il faut tester les cas les plus exigeants en premier."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Conditions combinées\n\nLes `and`, `or` et `not` vus au chapitre précédent s'utilisent naturellement dans un `if`."},{"t":"code","code":"age = 25\netudiant = True\n\nif age < 12 or age >= 65:\n    tarif = 0\nelif etudiant and age < 26:\n    tarif = 500\nelse:\n    tarif = 1000\n\nprint(f\"Ton billet de bus coûte {tarif} francs\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Teste avec différents âges et en mettant `etudiant = False`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : pair ou impair ?"},{"t":"exercise","id":"conditions-ex1","title":"Pair ou impair","prompt":"Écris un programme qui affiche `pair` si le nombre `n` est pair, et `impair` sinon.\n\nRappel : un nombre est pair si le reste de sa division par 2 vaut 0.","starter":"n = 42","solution":"n = 42\n\nif n % 2 == 0:\n    print(\"pair\")\nelse:\n    print(\"impair\")","check":"assert \"if\" in CODE, \"Utilise une condition if.\"\ndef lance(v):\n    import io, contextlib\n    buf = io.StringIO()\n    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):\n        exec(CODE.replace(\"n = 42\", f\"n = {v}\", 1), {})\n    return buf.getvalue().strip()\nassert lance(42) == \"pair\", \"Avec n = 42, le programme doit afficher « pair ».\"\nassert lance(7) == \"impair\", \"Avec n = 7, le programme doit afficher « impair ».\"\nassert lance(0) == \"pair\", \"Avec n = 0, le programme doit afficher « pair ».\"","hint":"if n % 2 == 0: puis, en dessous et décalé, print(\"pair\"). Ensuite else: et print(\"impair\").","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : les frais de livraison"},{"t":"exercise","id":"conditions-ex2","title":"Frais de livraison","prompt":"Une boutique en ligne calcule ses frais de livraison selon le montant du panier :\n\n- **50 euros ou plus** : livraison gratuite (0) ;\n- **entre 20 (inclus) et 50 euros** : 3 euros ;\n- **moins de 20 euros** : 5 euros.\n\nComplète le programme pour que la variable `frais` ait la bonne valeur, quel que soit `panier`.","starter":"panier = 35\n\n# calcule frais avec if / elif / else\n\nprint(f\"Frais de livraison : {frais} euros\")","solution":"panier = 35\n\nif panier >= 50:\n    frais = 0\nelif panier >= 20:\n    frais = 3\nelse:\n    frais = 5\n\nprint(f\"Frais de livraison : {frais} euros\")","check":"def frais_pour(v):\n    ns = {}\n    exec(CODE.replace(\"panier = 35\", f\"panier = {v}\", 1).replace(\"print(\", \"(lambda *a, **k: None)(\"), ns)\n    return ns.get(\"frais\")\nfor v, attendu in [(35, 3), (50, 0), (80, 0), (20, 3), (19.99, 5), (5, 5)]:\n    obtenu = frais_pour(v)\n    assert obtenu == attendu, f\"Pour un panier de {v} euros, les frais devraient être {attendu}, ton programme donne {obtenu}.\"","hint":"Commence par le cas le plus élevé : if panier >= 50: frais = 0, puis elif panier >= 20: frais = 3, puis else: frais = 5.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"boucle-for","title":"Répéter avec for","subtitle":"Faire travailler l'ordinateur à ta place.","duration":14,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## La boucle for\n\nUne **boucle** répète un bloc de code. La boucle `for` le répète **pour chaque élément** d'une séquence."},{"t":"code","code":"for i in range(5):\n    print(\"Je ne dois pas bavarder en classe.\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Cinq lignes avec deux lignes de code. Pour en avoir 500, change juste le 5. Comme pour `if`, la ligne `for` finit par `:` et le bloc répété est indenté."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## range(), le générateur de nombres\n\n`range` produit une suite de nombres entiers :\n\n| Écriture | Nombres produits |\n|---|---|\n| `range(5)` | 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 |\n| `range(1, 6)` | 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 |\n| `range(0, 20, 5)` | 0, 5, 10, 15 |\n| `range(10, 0, -1)` | 10, 9, … 1 |\n\nLa valeur de fin est toujours **exclue**. La variable de boucle (ici `n`) prend chaque valeur à tour de rôle :"},{"t":"code","code":"for n in range(1, 6):\n    print(n, \"au carré =\", n ** 2)","run":true},{"t":"quiz","code":"for i in range(2, 8, 2):\n    print(i)","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["2 4 6 8","2 4 6","2 3 4 5 6 7"],"answer":1,"explain":"On part de 2, on avance de 2 en 2, et on s'arrête **avant** 8 : 2, 4, 6."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Parcourir un texte\n\n`for` fonctionne sur tout ce qui contient plusieurs éléments. Un texte, par exemple, contient des caractères :"},{"t":"code","code":"for lettre in \"Bissap\":\n    print(lettre)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Et bientôt, tu parcourras des listes de ventes, de notes ou de clients avec exactement la même syntaxe."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Le motif de l'accumulateur\n\nVoici le motif le plus important de toute la programmation de données : **accumuler** un résultat dans une variable."},{"t":"widget","name":"stepper","config":{"code":"ventes_semaine = 0\nfor jour in range(1, 4):\n    ventes_semaine = ventes_semaine + 100 * jour\nprint(ventes_semaine)","steps":[{"line":1,"vars":{},"note":"On prépare une boîte qui démarre à 0."},{"line":2,"vars":{"ventes_semaine":"0"},"note":"Début de la boucle : jour prendra les valeurs 1, 2, 3."},{"line":3,"vars":{"ventes_semaine":"0","jour":"1"},"note":"0 + 100 × 1 = 100"},{"line":3,"vars":{"ventes_semaine":"100","jour":"2"},"note":"100 + 100 × 2 = 300"},{"line":3,"vars":{"ventes_semaine":"300","jour":"3"},"note":"300 + 100 × 3 = 600"},{"line":4,"vars":{"ventes_semaine":"600","jour":"3"},"out":"600","note":"La boucle est finie : la boîte contient le total."}]}},{"t":"md","v":"En Python, `total = total + x` s'écrit aussi `total += x`. C'est plus court et très courant."},{"t":"code","code":"total = 0\nfor n in range(1, 11):\n    total += n\nprint(\"La somme de 1 à 10 vaut\", total)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Compter\n\nUn cas particulier de l'accumulateur : le **compteur**. On ajoute 1 à chaque fois qu'une condition est vraie. Ici, on combine `for` et `if` :"},{"t":"code","code":"phrase = \"les données sont partout autour de nous\"\nnb_o = 0\nfor lettre in phrase:\n    if lettre == \"o\":\n        nb_o += 1\nprint(f\"Il y a {nb_o} fois la lettre o\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Remarque les **deux niveaux d'indentation** : le `if` est dans la boucle, et le `nb_o += 1` est dans le `if`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : la table de multiplication"},{"t":"exercise","id":"boucle-for-ex1","title":"Table de 7","prompt":"Avec une boucle `for`, affiche la table de multiplication de 7, de 1 à 10, sous cette forme :\n\n    7 x 1 = 7\n    7 x 2 = 14\n    ...\n    7 x 10 = 70","starter":"n = 7","solution":"n = 7\n\nfor i in range(1, 11):\n    print(f\"{n} x {i} = {n * i}\")","check":"assert \"for\" in CODE, \"Utilise une boucle for.\"\nlignes = [l.strip() for l in OUT.strip().split(\"\\n\")]\nattendu = [f\"7 x {i} = {7 * i}\" for i in range(1, 11)]\nassert len(lignes) == 10, f\"Il faut 10 lignes, ton programme en affiche {len(lignes)}.\"\nassert lignes == attendu, f\"Vérifie le format. Première ligne attendue : « 7 x 1 = 7 », obtenue : « {lignes[0]} ».\"","hint":"for i in range(1, 11): puis, dans la boucle, print(f\"{n} x {i} = {n * i}\")","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : la somme"},{"t":"exercise","id":"boucle-for-ex2","title":"La somme des 100 premiers nombres","prompt":"On raconte qu'un élève de 9 ans, Carl Friedrich Gauss, a calculé de tête la somme 1 + 2 + 3 + … + 100. Toi, tu as Python.\n\nCalcule cette somme avec une boucle et un accumulateur, dans une variable `somme`, puis affiche-la.","starter":"somme = 0\n\nprint(somme)","solution":"somme = 0\nfor n in range(1, 101):\n    somme += n\nprint(somme)","check":"assert \"for\" in CODE, \"Utilise une boucle for.\"\nassert somme == 5050, f\"La somme devrait valoir 5050, tu obtiens {somme}. Vérifie les bornes de ton range (la fin est exclue).\"\nassert \"5050\" not in CODE, \"Ne tape pas le résultat à la main : calcule-le avec la boucle.\"","hint":"range(1, 101) produit les nombres de 1 à 100. À chaque tour : somme += n.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : compter les voyelles"},{"t":"exercise","id":"boucle-for-ex3","title":"Compteur de voyelles","prompt":"Compte le nombre de voyelles (a, e, i, o, u, y) dans la variable `phrase` et range le résultat dans `nb_voyelles`. Affiche-le.\n\nAstuce : `lettre in \"aeiouy\"` vaut `True` si la lettre est une voyelle.","starter":"phrase = \"python est un langage magnifique\"\nnb_voyelles = 0\n\nprint(nb_voyelles)","solution":"phrase = \"python est un langage magnifique\"\nnb_voyelles = 0\nfor lettre in phrase:\n    if lettre in \"aeiouy\":\n        nb_voyelles += 1\nprint(nb_voyelles)","check":"attendu = sum(1 for c in \"python est un langage magnifique\" if c in \"aeiouy\")\nassert nb_voyelles == attendu, f\"Il y a {attendu} voyelles dans la phrase, ton programme en compte {nb_voyelles}.\"\nassert \"for\" in CODE, \"Utilise une boucle for.\"","hint":"for lettre in phrase: puis if lettre in \"aeiouy\": puis nb_voyelles += 1 (attention aux deux niveaux de décalage).","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"boucle-while","title":"Répéter avec while","subtitle":"Tant que… et comment éviter la boucle infinie.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Tant que la condition est vraie\n\n`for` répète un nombre de fois connu à l'avance. Mais parfois, on ne sait pas combien de tours il faudra. On utilise alors `while` (« tant que ») : le bloc est répété **tant que** la condition reste vraie."},{"t":"code","code":"epargne = 0\nmois = 0\nwhile epargne < 100000:\n    epargne += 15000\n    mois += 1\nprint(f\"Objectif atteint en {mois} mois, avec {epargne} francs\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"On ne savait pas à l'avance qu'il faudrait 7 mois : c'est la boucle qui l'a trouvé."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## La boucle infinie\n\nSi la condition ne devient **jamais** fausse, la boucle ne s'arrête jamais. C'est la **boucle infinie**, le piège classique."},{"t":"code","code":"compteur = 1\nwhile compteur <= 5:\n    print(compteur)\n    # oups : on a oublié compteur += 1","run":false},{"t":"md","v":"Ici, `compteur` vaut toujours 1, donc `compteur <= 5` est toujours vrai. Le programme tourne pour l'éternité."},{"t":"callout","kind":"info","title":"","v":"Pas de panique si ça t'arrive sur ce site : au bout de quelques secondes, le programme est arrêté automatiquement et Python redémarre. Sur ton ordinateur, on arrête un programme bloqué avec Ctrl + C."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Que manque-t-il le plus souvent dans une boucle infinie ?","options":["Le `print`","La ligne qui fait évoluer la variable testée par la condition","Les deux-points"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pour que la boucle s'arrête, il faut qu'à chaque tour quelque chose rapproche la condition du « faux » : augmenter un compteur, diminuer un stock, etc."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Sortir plus tôt : break et continue\n\n- `break` arrête la boucle **immédiatement** ;\n- `continue` saute **au tour suivant**, sans exécuter la fin du bloc."},{"t":"code","code":"# On cherche le premier multiple de 7 supérieur à 50\nn = 51\nwhile True:\n    if n % 7 == 0:\n        break\n    n += 1\nprint(\"Trouvé :\", n)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`while True` est une boucle volontairement infinie : seul le `break` en sort. C'est un motif courant quand on cherche quelque chose."},{"t":"code","code":"for n in range(1, 11):\n    if n % 3 == 0:\n        continue\n    print(n)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Les multiples de 3 sont sautés grâce à `continue`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Un petit jeu\n\nCombinons `while`, `input` et `if`. Le programme a choisi un nombre secret ; tu as plusieurs essais. Tes propositions vont dans la case Entrées, une par ligne. Exécute avec les valeurs proposées, puis essaie les tiennes."},{"t":"code","code":"secret = 37\nessais = 0\ntrouve = False\n\nwhile not trouve:\n    proposition = int(input(\"Ton nombre ? \"))\n    essais += 1\n    if proposition < secret:\n        print(\"C'est plus !\")\n    elif proposition > secret:\n        print(\"C'est moins !\")\n    else:\n        trouve = True\n\nprint(f\"Bravo, trouvé en {essais} essais !\")","run":true,"inputs":"50\n25\n37"},{"t":"md","v":"En coupant l'intervalle en deux à chaque fois (50, puis 25, puis 37…), on trouve n'importe quel nombre entre 1 et 100 en 7 essais maximum. C'est un algorithme célèbre : la **recherche dichotomique**."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : la population"},{"t":"exercise","id":"boucle-while-ex1","title":"Le doublement","prompt":"Une ville compte `population = 500000` habitants. Elle grandit de **4 % par an** (chaque année, on multiplie la population par 1.04).\n\nAvec une boucle `while`, calcule combien d'années (`annees`) il faut pour que la population **dépasse 1 million** (strictement plus de 1 000 000). Affiche `annees`.","starter":"population = 500000\nannees = 0\n\nprint(annees)","solution":"population = 500000\nannees = 0\nwhile population <= 1000000:\n    population = population * 1.04\n    annees += 1\nprint(annees)","check":"assert \"while\" in CODE, \"Utilise une boucle while.\"\nassert annees == 18, f\"Il faut 18 ans pour dépasser 1 million, ton programme trouve {annees}.\"","hint":"while population <= 1000000: puis, dans la boucle, multiplie population par 1.04 et ajoute 1 à annees.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : le compte à rebours"},{"t":"exercise","id":"boucle-while-ex2","title":"Décollage","prompt":"Avec une boucle `while`, affiche un compte à rebours de 5 à 1, un nombre par ligne, puis `Décollage !`.","starter":"compte = 5","solution":"compte = 5\nwhile compte > 0:\n    print(compte)\n    compte -= 1\nprint(\"Décollage !\")","check":"assert \"while\" in CODE, \"Utilise une boucle while.\"\nlignes = [l.strip() for l in OUT.strip().split(\"\\n\")]\nassert lignes == [\"5\", \"4\", \"3\", \"2\", \"1\", \"Décollage !\"], f\"Attendu : 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 puis Décollage ! (une valeur par ligne). Obtenu : {lignes}\"","hint":"while compte > 0: puis print(compte) et compte -= 1. Le print(\"Décollage !\") se place après la boucle (sans décalage).","inputs":""}]]}]},{"id":"structures","icon":"📦","title":"Ranger ses données","desc":"Listes, dictionnaires, tuples et ensembles : les boîtes à données de Python.","badge":"Grand rangement","lessons":[{"id":"listes","title":"Les listes","subtitle":"Ranger plusieurs valeurs dans une seule variable.","duration":15,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Une variable, plein de valeurs\n\nImagine les notes de 30 élèves. Créer 30 variables `note1`, `note2`… serait absurde. On utilise une **liste** : une suite ordonnée de valeurs, entre **crochets**, séparées par des virgules."},{"t":"code","code":"notes = [12, 15, 9, 17, 14]\nvilles = [\"Kinshasa\", \"Abidjan\", \"Dakar\"]\nmelange = [\"Awa\", 24, True, 1.75]\n\nprint(notes)\nprint(len(villes))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`len()` donne le nombre d'éléments, comme pour un texte."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Accéder aux éléments\n\nComme pour les textes, chaque élément a une **position** qui commence à **0**."},{"t":"code","code":"villes = [\"Kinshasa\", \"Abidjan\", \"Dakar\", \"Paris\", \"Montréal\"]\nprint(villes[0])\nprint(villes[2])\nprint(villes[-1])\nprint(villes[1:3])","run":true},{"t":"quiz","code":"fruits = [\"mangue\", \"ananas\", \"papaye\", \"banane\"]\nprint(fruits[1])","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["mangue","ananas","papaye"],"answer":1,"explain":"La position 0 est « mangue », la position 1 est « ananas ». On compte toujours à partir de 0 !"},{"t":"md","v":"Et si on demande une position qui n'existe pas ?"},{"t":"code","code":"villes = [\"Kinshasa\", \"Abidjan\", \"Dakar\"]\nprint(villes[3])","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`IndexError` : la liste a 3 éléments, aux positions 0, 1 et 2. La position 3 n'existe pas."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Modifier une liste\n\nContrairement aux textes, les listes sont **modifiables**."},{"t":"code","code":"courses = [\"riz\", \"huile\"]\ncourses.append(\"tomates\")       # ajoute à la fin\ncourses.insert(0, \"pain\")       # insère à la position 0\ncourses[1] = \"riz parfumé\"      # remplace l'élément en position 1\ncourses.remove(\"huile\")         # supprime cet élément\ndernier = courses.pop()         # retire et renvoie le dernier\nprint(courses)\nprint(\"Retiré :\", dernier)\nprint(\"riz parfumé\" in courses)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"| Méthode | Effet |\n|---|---|\n| `.append(x)` | ajoute x à la fin |\n| `.insert(i, x)` | insère x à la position i |\n| `.remove(x)` | supprime la première occurrence de x |\n| `.pop()` | retire le dernier élément et le renvoie |\n| `.sort()` | trie la liste |\n| `.count(x)` | compte les x |"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Calculer sur une liste\n\nPython offre des fonctions toutes prêtes pour les listes de nombres :"},{"t":"code","code":"ventes = [120, 95, 210, 180, 75, 160, 230]\nprint(\"Total :\", sum(ventes))\nprint(\"Minimum :\", min(ventes))\nprint(\"Maximum :\", max(ventes))\nprint(\"Moyenne :\", round(sum(ventes) / len(ventes), 1))\nprint(\"Triées :\", sorted(ventes))\nprint(\"Du plus grand au plus petit :\", sorted(ventes, reverse=True))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`sorted(liste)` renvoie une **nouvelle** liste triée, alors que `liste.sort()` trie la liste elle-même."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Parcourir une liste\n\nLa boucle `for` parcourt une liste élément par élément. C'est l'association la plus utilisée de Python."},{"t":"code","code":"temperatures = [28, 31, 35, 30, 27, 33, 36]\njours_chauds = 0\nfor t in temperatures:\n    if t > 32:\n        jours_chauds += 1\n        print(f\"{t} °C : il fait chaud !\")\nprint(f\"{jours_chauds} jours au-dessus de 32 °C\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"On peut aussi **construire** une liste dans une boucle : on part d'une liste vide `[]` et on fait des `append`."},{"t":"code","code":"prix_ht = [1000, 2500, 400]\nprix_ttc = []\nfor p in prix_ht:\n    prix_ttc.append(p * 1.18)\nprint(prix_ttc)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : la meilleure note, à la main"},{"t":"exercise","id":"listes-ex1","title":"L'algorithme du chapitre 1","prompt":"Au chapitre 1, tu as remis dans l'ordre l'algorithme qui trouve la meilleure note. Écris-le maintenant en Python !\n\nTrouve la plus grande valeur de la liste `notes` **sans utiliser `max()`**, et range-la dans `meilleure`.\n\nRappel de l'algorithme : retiens la première note, puis parcours les notes ; si une note est plus grande que celle retenue, retiens-la à la place.","starter":"notes = [12, 15, 9, 17, 14, 11, 16]\n\nmeilleure = notes[0]\n\nprint(meilleure)","solution":"notes = [12, 15, 9, 17, 14, 11, 16]\n\nmeilleure = notes[0]\nfor n in notes:\n    if n > meilleure:\n        meilleure = n\n\nprint(meilleure)","check":"assert \"max(\" not in CODE, \"Pas de max() ici : le but est d'écrire l'algorithme toi-même.\"\nassert \"for\" in CODE, \"Parcours la liste avec une boucle for.\"\nassert meilleure == 17, f\"La meilleure note est 17, ton programme trouve {meilleure}.\"","hint":"for n in notes: puis if n > meilleure: puis meilleure = n","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : le bulletin"},{"t":"exercise","id":"listes-ex2","title":"Le bulletin de la classe","prompt":"À partir de la liste `notes` :\n\n1. ajoute la note `18` d'une élève absente lors de la saisie (avec `append`) ;\n2. calcule `moyenne`, la moyenne de la classe arrondie à 2 décimales ;\n3. calcule `nb_admis`, le nombre de notes supérieures ou égales à 10.","starter":"notes = [12, 8, 15, 9.5, 14, 6, 17, 11]\n\nmoyenne = \nnb_admis = 0\n\nprint(moyenne, nb_admis)","solution":"notes = [12, 8, 15, 9.5, 14, 6, 17, 11]\nnotes.append(18)\n\nmoyenne = round(sum(notes) / len(notes), 2)\nnb_admis = 0\nfor n in notes:\n    if n >= 10:\n        nb_admis += 1\n\nprint(moyenne, nb_admis)","check":"assert len(notes) == 9 and notes[-1] == 18, \"Ajoute la note 18 à la fin de la liste avec notes.append(18).\"\nassert moyenne == round(110.5 / 9, 2), f\"La moyenne attendue est {round(110.5 / 9, 2)}, tu obtiens {moyenne}.\"\nassert nb_admis == 6, f\"Il y a 6 notes supérieures ou égales à 10, tu en comptes {nb_admis}.\"","hint":"notes.append(18) ; moyenne = round(sum(notes) / len(notes), 2) ; puis un compteur dans une boucle for.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"dictionnaires","title":"Les dictionnaires","subtitle":"Associer une clé à une valeur, comme dans un vrai dictionnaire.","duration":15,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Chercher par nom, pas par position\n\nDans une liste, on retrouve un élément par sa **position**. Mais pour décrire un produit, on préfère retrouver chaque information par son **nom** : le prix, le stock, la catégorie…\n\nUn **dictionnaire** (`dict`) associe des **clés** à des **valeurs**, entre accolades :"},{"t":"code","code":"produit = {\n    \"nom\": \"Jus de bissap\",\n    \"prix\": 2.2,\n    \"stock\": 48,\n    \"categorie\": \"Boisson\",\n}\nprint(produit[\"nom\"])\nprint(produit[\"prix\"] * 3)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Comme dans un vrai dictionnaire : on cherche un mot (la clé) pour trouver sa définition (la valeur)."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Modifier, ajouter, vérifier"},{"t":"code","code":"produit = {\"nom\": \"Croissant\", \"prix\": 1.8, \"stock\": 30}\n\nproduit[\"stock\"] = produit[\"stock\"] - 5   # modifier\nproduit[\"bio\"] = True                     # ajouter une clé\nprint(produit)\n\nprint(\"prix\" in produit)                  # la clé existe-t-elle ?\nprint(produit.get(\"couleur\", \"inconnue\")) # valeur par défaut si absente","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Demander une clé qui n'existe pas avec les crochets provoque une `KeyError`. La méthode `.get(cle, defaut)` évite l'erreur."},{"t":"code","code":"produit = {\"nom\": \"Croissant\", \"prix\": 1.8}\nprint(produit[\"Prix\"])","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Majuscule ! `\"Prix\"` et `\"prix\"` sont deux clés différentes."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Parcourir un dictionnaire"},{"t":"code","code":"stock = {\"Espresso\": 120, \"Cappuccino\": 80, \"Thé à la menthe\": 45}\n\nfor produit in stock:\n    print(produit)\n\nfor produit, quantite in stock.items():\n    print(f\"{produit} : {quantite} unités\")\n\nprint(list(stock.keys()))\nprint(sum(stock.values()))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`.items()` donne les paires (clé, valeur) : c'est la façon la plus pratique de parcourir un dictionnaire."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Compter avec un dictionnaire\n\nUn usage très courant : compter combien de fois apparaît chaque élément. C'est exactement ce que fait un analyste quand il compte les ventes par ville."},{"t":"code","code":"ventes = [\"Dakar\", \"Paris\", \"Dakar\", \"Kinshasa\", \"Dakar\", \"Paris\"]\ncompte = {}\nfor ville in ventes:\n    if ville in compte:\n        compte[ville] += 1\n    else:\n        compte[ville] = 1\nprint(compte)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Une liste de dictionnaires = un tableau\n\nVoici l'idée qui fait le lien avec l'analyse de données. Une **liste de dictionnaires** ressemble exactement à un tableau Excel : chaque dictionnaire est une **ligne**, chaque clé une **colonne**."},{"t":"code","code":"eleves = [\n    {\"nom\": \"Awa\", \"ville\": \"Dakar\", \"note\": 15},\n    {\"nom\": \"Kofi\", \"ville\": \"Abidjan\", \"note\": 12},\n    {\"nom\": \"Gloire\", \"ville\": \"Kinshasa\", \"note\": 17},\n]\n\nfor e in eleves:\n    print(f\"{e['nom']:<8} {e['ville']:<10} {e['note']}\")\n\nmoyenne = sum(e[\"note\"] for e in eleves) / len(eleves)\nprint(\"Moyenne :\", round(moyenne, 2))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"| nom | ville | note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Awa | Dakar | 15 |\n| Kofi | Abidjan | 12 |\n| Gloire | Kinshasa | 17 |\n\nAu dernier chapitre, la bibliothèque **pandas** manipulera ce genre de tableaux avec des milliers de lignes, en une seule instruction."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"","v":"Dans une f-string, `{e['nom']:<8}` aligne le texte à gauche sur 8 caractères. Pratique pour afficher des tableaux propres. Note les guillemets simples à l'intérieur des guillemets doubles."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : le stock"},{"t":"exercise","id":"dictionnaires-ex1","title":"Gestion de stock","prompt":"Le dictionnaire `stock` donne les quantités en réserve. Aujourd'hui :\n\n1. on a vendu **12 croissants** (diminue le stock) ;\n2. on a reçu un nouveau produit : **`\"Sandwich\"`** avec **20** unités (ajoute-le) ;\n3. calcule `total` : le nombre total d'unités en stock (tous produits confondus).","starter":"stock = {\"Croissant\": 40, \"Beignet\": 25, \"Espresso\": 100}\n\ntotal = \nprint(stock)\nprint(total)","solution":"stock = {\"Croissant\": 40, \"Beignet\": 25, \"Espresso\": 100}\nstock[\"Croissant\"] -= 12\nstock[\"Sandwich\"] = 20\n\ntotal = sum(stock.values())\nprint(stock)\nprint(total)","check":"assert stock.get(\"Croissant\") == 28, f\"Il devrait rester 28 croissants, il y en a {stock.get('Croissant')}.\"\nassert stock.get(\"Sandwich\") == 20, \"Ajoute la clé \\\"Sandwich\\\" avec la valeur 20 (attention à la majuscule).\"\nassert total == 173, f\"Le total devrait être 28 + 25 + 100 + 20 = 173, tu obtiens {total}.\"","hint":"stock[\"Croissant\"] -= 12, puis stock[\"Sandwich\"] = 20, et enfin sum(stock.values()).","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : le chiffre d'affaires"},{"t":"exercise","id":"dictionnaires-ex2","title":"Les commandes du jour","prompt":"La liste `commandes` contient les commandes du jour (une par dictionnaire). Calcule :\n\n- `chiffre_affaires` : la somme de `quantite × prix` pour toutes les commandes ;\n- `par_ville` : un dictionnaire qui donne le nombre de commandes par ville, par exemple `{\"Dakar\": 2, ...}`.","starter":"commandes = [\n    {\"ville\": \"Dakar\", \"quantite\": 3, \"prix\": 2.5},\n    {\"ville\": \"Paris\", \"quantite\": 1, \"prix\": 5.0},\n    {\"ville\": \"Dakar\", \"quantite\": 2, \"prix\": 1.8},\n    {\"ville\": \"Kinshasa\", \"quantite\": 6, \"prix\": 1.0},\n    {\"ville\": \"Paris\", \"quantite\": 2, \"prix\": 3.5},\n]\n\nchiffre_affaires = 0\npar_ville = {}\n\nprint(chiffre_affaires)\nprint(par_ville)","solution":"commandes = [\n    {\"ville\": \"Dakar\", \"quantite\": 3, \"prix\": 2.5},\n    {\"ville\": \"Paris\", \"quantite\": 1, \"prix\": 5.0},\n    {\"ville\": \"Dakar\", \"quantite\": 2, \"prix\": 1.8},\n    {\"ville\": \"Kinshasa\", \"quantite\": 6, \"prix\": 1.0},\n    {\"ville\": \"Paris\", \"quantite\": 2, \"prix\": 3.5},\n]\n\nchiffre_affaires = 0\npar_ville = {}\nfor c in commandes:\n    chiffre_affaires += c[\"quantite\"] * c[\"prix\"]\n    if c[\"ville\"] in par_ville:\n        par_ville[c[\"ville\"]] += 1\n    else:\n        par_ville[c[\"ville\"]] = 1\n\nprint(chiffre_affaires)\nprint(par_ville)","check":"assert abs(chiffre_affaires - 29.1) < 1e-6, f\"Le chiffre d'affaires devrait être 29.1, tu obtiens {chiffre_affaires}.\"\nassert par_ville == {\"Dakar\": 2, \"Paris\": 2, \"Kinshasa\": 1}, f\"par_ville devrait être {{'Dakar': 2, 'Paris': 2, 'Kinshasa': 1}}, tu obtiens {par_ville}.\"","hint":"Une seule boucle for c in commandes suffit. Dans la boucle : chiffre_affaires += c[\"quantite\"] * c[\"prix\"], puis le motif du compteur avec le dictionnaire.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"tuples-ensembles","title":"Tuples et ensembles","subtitle":"Deux cousins de la liste, chacun avec son super-pouvoir.","duration":10,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Le tuple : une liste figée\n\nUn **tuple** s'écrit entre **parenthèses**. Il ressemble à une liste, mais on ne peut plus le modifier une fois créé. On l'utilise pour des valeurs qui vont ensemble et ne doivent pas changer : des coordonnées GPS, une date, une couleur…"},{"t":"code","code":"kinshasa = (-4.32, 15.31)\nprint(kinshasa[0])\nprint(len(kinshasa))\nkinshasa[0] = 0","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"L'erreur est voulue : un tuple est **immuable**. Cette garantie évite des modifications accidentelles."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Le déballage\n\nSuper-pouvoir du tuple : on peut le **déballer** dans plusieurs variables d'un coup."},{"t":"code","code":"point = (-4.32, 15.31)\nlatitude, longitude = point\nprint(\"Latitude :\", latitude)\nprint(\"Longitude :\", longitude)\n\n# Échanger deux variables en une ligne\na, b = 1, 2\na, b = b, a\nprint(a, b)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Tu as déjà utilisé le déballage sans le savoir : dans `for produit, quantite in stock.items()`, chaque paire est un tuple déballé."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## L'ensemble : zéro doublon\n\nUn **ensemble** (`set`) s'écrit entre accolades, sans clés. Il ne garde **qu'un exemplaire** de chaque valeur, et n'a pas d'ordre."},{"t":"code","code":"villes_clients = [\"Dakar\", \"Paris\", \"Dakar\", \"Abidjan\", \"Paris\", \"Dakar\"]\nvilles_uniques = set(villes_clients)\nprint(villes_uniques)\nprint(\"Nombre de villes différentes :\", len(villes_uniques))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`len(set(liste))` est l'astuce classique pour compter les **valeurs distinctes**. Une question qu'un analyste pose tous les jours : combien de clients différents ? Combien de produits distincts vendus ?"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Opérations d'ensembles\n\nLes ensembles savent faire les opérations des diagrammes de Venn :"},{"t":"code","code":"clients_janvier = {\"Awa\", \"Kofi\", \"Grace\", \"Moussa\"}\nclients_fevrier = {\"Grace\", \"Moussa\", \"Inès\", \"David\"}\n\nprint(\"Venus les deux mois :\", clients_janvier & clients_fevrier)\nprint(\"Tous les clients :\", clients_janvier | clients_fevrier)\nprint(\"Perdus en février :\", clients_janvier - clients_fevrier)","run":true},{"t":"quiz","q":"Quel type choisir pour stocker la liste des **pays différents** d'où viennent les visiteurs d'un site ?","options":["Une liste","Un tuple","Un ensemble"],"answer":2,"explain":"Un ensemble élimine automatiquement les doublons : chaque pays n'y apparaît qu'une fois."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Récapitulatif des structures\n\n| Type | Syntaxe | Ordonné | Modifiable | Doublons |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| liste | `[1, 2, 3]` | oui | oui | oui |\n| tuple | `(1, 2, 3)` | oui | non | oui |\n| ensemble | `{1, 2, 3}` | non | oui | non |\n| dictionnaire | `{\"a\": 1}` | oui | oui | clés uniques |"},{"t":"exercise","id":"tuples-ensembles-ex1","title":"Les clients fidèles","prompt":"Deux listes contiennent les noms des clients d'un café en mai et en juin (avec des doublons, car un client peut venir plusieurs fois).\n\nCalcule :\n\n- `nb_mai` : le nombre de clients **différents** en mai ;\n- `fideles` : l'**ensemble** des clients venus en mai **et** en juin.","starter":"mai = [\"Awa\", \"Kofi\", \"Awa\", \"Inès\", \"Moussa\", \"Kofi\", \"Awa\"]\njuin = [\"Inès\", \"David\", \"Awa\", \"Inès\", \"Rachel\"]\n\nnb_mai = \nfideles = \n\nprint(nb_mai)\nprint(fideles)","solution":"mai = [\"Awa\", \"Kofi\", \"Awa\", \"Inès\", \"Moussa\", \"Kofi\", \"Awa\"]\njuin = [\"Inès\", \"David\", \"Awa\", \"Inès\", \"Rachel\"]\n\nnb_mai = len(set(mai))\nfideles = set(mai) & set(juin)\n\nprint(nb_mai)\nprint(fideles)","check":"assert nb_mai == 4, f\"Il y a 4 clients différents en mai (Awa, Kofi, Inès, Moussa), tu en comptes {nb_mai}.\"\nassert isinstance(fideles, set), \"fideles doit être un ensemble (set).\"\nassert fideles == {\"Awa\", \"Inès\"}, f\"Les clients venus les deux mois sont Awa et Inès, tu obtiens {fideles}.\"","hint":"Transforme chaque liste en ensemble avec set(...). len() donne le nombre d'éléments, & donne l'intersection.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"comprehensions","title":"Les compréhensions de liste","subtitle":"Transformer et filtrer en une seule ligne.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Une boucle en une ligne\n\nTu as souvent écrit ce motif : créer une liste vide, la remplir dans une boucle."},{"t":"code","code":"prix = [1000, 2500, 400, 1200]\nprix_ttc = []\nfor p in prix:\n    prix_ttc.append(p * 1.18)\nprint(prix_ttc)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Python propose un raccourci très élégant, la **compréhension de liste** :"},{"t":"code","code":"prix = [1000, 2500, 400, 1200]\nprix_ttc = [p * 1.18 for p in prix]\nprint(prix_ttc)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Ça se lit presque comme une phrase : « `p * 1.18` **pour chaque** `p` **dans** `prix` »."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Filtrer avec if\n\nOn peut ajouter une condition à la fin pour ne garder que certains éléments."},{"t":"code","code":"temperatures = [28, 31, 35, 30, 27, 33, 36]\nchaudes = [t for t in temperatures if t > 32]\nprint(chaudes)\n\nprenoms = [\"Awa\", \"Kofi\", \"Amina\", \"Jean\", \"Aïcha\"]\nen_a = [p for p in prenoms if p.startswith(\"A\")]\nprint(en_a)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"La structure générale : `[expression for element in sequence if condition]`."},{"t":"quiz","code":"nombres = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\nprint([n * 10 for n in nombres if n % 2 == 0])","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["[10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60]","[20, 40, 60]","[2, 4, 6]"],"answer":1,"explain":"On garde seulement les nombres pairs (2, 4, 6), puis on les multiplie par 10."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Deux outils précieux : enumerate et zip\n\n`enumerate` donne la position **et** l'élément. Pratique pour numéroter un classement :"},{"t":"code","code":"podium = [\"Gloire\", \"Awa\", \"Kofi\"]\nfor rang, nom in enumerate(podium, start=1):\n    print(f\"{rang}. {nom}\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`zip` parcourt deux listes **en parallèle**, élément par élément :"},{"t":"code","code":"produits = [\"Espresso\", \"Croissant\", \"Sandwich\"]\nprix = [2.5, 1.8, 5.0]\nfor produit, p in zip(produits, prix):\n    print(f\"{produit} : {p} euros\")\n\ncarte = dict(zip(produits, prix))\nprint(carte)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les compréhensions de dictionnaire\n\nMême principe avec des accolades et une paire `cle: valeur` :"},{"t":"code","code":"prix_euros = {\"Espresso\": 2.5, \"Croissant\": 1.8, \"Sandwich\": 5.0}\nprix_fcfa = {produit: round(p * 655.96) for produit, p in prix_euros.items()}\nprint(prix_fcfa)","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"","v":"Les compréhensions sont pratiques, mais la lisibilité passe avant tout. Si ta compréhension devient trop longue, une boucle classique est souvent plus claire."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercices"},{"t":"exercise","id":"comprehensions-ex1","title":"Conversion de températures","prompt":"La liste `celsius` contient des températures en degrés Celsius. Avec **une compréhension de liste**, crée `fahrenheit` qui contient les mêmes températures en degrés Fahrenheit, arrondies à 1 décimale.\n\nFormule : `F = C × 9 / 5 + 32`","starter":"celsius = [0, 12.5, 25, 31, 37, 100]\n\nfahrenheit = \nprint(fahrenheit)","solution":"celsius = [0, 12.5, 25, 31, 37, 100]\n\nfahrenheit = [round(c * 9 / 5 + 32, 1) for c in celsius]\nprint(fahrenheit)","check":"assert \"for\" in CODE and \"[\" in CODE.split(\"fahrenheit =\")[1], \"Utilise une compréhension de liste : [... for c in celsius].\"\nassert fahrenheit == [32.0, 54.5, 77.0, 87.8, 98.6, 212.0], f\"Résultat attendu : [32.0, 54.5, 77.0, 87.8, 98.6, 212.0]. Obtenu : {fahrenheit}\"","hint":"[round(c * 9 / 5 + 32, 1) for c in celsius]","inputs":""},{"t":"exercise","id":"comprehensions-ex2","title":"Les grosses commandes","prompt":"Avec une compréhension de liste et un filtre, crée `grosses` : la liste des **montants** des commandes de plus de 100 euros (strictement), **après une remise de 10 %** (multiplie par 0.9).","starter":"montants = [45, 120, 300, 80, 150, 99, 101]\n\ngrosses = \nprint(grosses)","solution":"montants = [45, 120, 300, 80, 150, 99, 101]\n\ngrosses = [m * 0.9 for m in montants if m > 100]\nprint(grosses)","check":"attendu = [m * 0.9 for m in [45, 120, 300, 80, 150, 99, 101] if m > 100]\nassert [round(x, 6) for x in grosses] == [round(x, 6) for x in attendu], f\"Attendu : {attendu}. Obtenu : {grosses}\"","hint":"[m * 0.9 for m in montants if m > 100]","inputs":""}]]}]},{"id":"fonctions","icon":"🧩","title":"Fonctions, modules et fichiers","desc":"Écrire du code réutilisable, utiliser les bibliothèques et lire des fichiers.","badge":"Architecte","lessons":[{"id":"fonctions","title":"Créer tes propres fonctions","subtitle":"def, paramètres et return.","duration":15,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Pourquoi des fonctions ?\n\nTu utilises déjà des fonctions : `print()`, `len()`, `round()`, `sum()`… Quelqu'un les a écrites une fois, et tout le monde les réutilise.\n\nTu vas maintenant créer **les tiennes**. Une fonction, c'est un morceau de code auquel on donne un nom, pour :\n\n- **ne pas se répéter** : on écrit le code une fois, on l'utilise cent fois ;\n- **organiser** : un gros programme devient une suite de petites fonctions claires ;\n- **corriger plus facilement** : une erreur se corrige à un seul endroit."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Définir et appeler\n\nOn **définit** une fonction avec `def`, son nom, des parenthèses et deux-points. Le corps est indenté."},{"t":"code","code":"def saluer():\n    print(\"Bonjour !\")\n    print(\"Bienvenue au Café Baobab.\")\n\nsaluer()\nsaluer()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Définir une fonction ne l'exécute pas : on lui apprend un savoir-faire. Ensuite, on l'**appelle** en écrivant son nom suivi de parenthèses. Ici, deux appels, donc deux accueils."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les paramètres\n\nLes **paramètres** sont les entrées de la fonction : des variables qui reçoivent les valeurs données lors de l'appel."},{"t":"code","code":"def saluer(prenom, ville):\n    print(f\"Bonjour {prenom}, bienvenue au café de {ville} !\")\n\nsaluer(\"Awa\", \"Dakar\")\nsaluer(\"Gloire\", \"Kinshasa\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Lors du premier appel, `prenom` vaut `\"Awa\"` et `ville` vaut `\"Dakar\"`. Lors du second, d'autres valeurs. Même code, résultats différents."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## return : renvoyer un résultat\n\n`print` **affiche** une valeur. `return` **renvoie** une valeur au code qui a appelé la fonction, pour qu'il puisse s'en servir : la ranger dans une variable, faire un calcul…"},{"t":"code","code":"def prix_ttc(prix_ht, taux=0.18):\n    return prix_ht * (1 + taux)\n\na = prix_ttc(1000)\nb = prix_ttc(2500)\nprint(a + b)\nprint(round(prix_ttc(99.99), 2))","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"`return` termine la fonction immédiatement : les lignes suivantes du bloc ne sont pas exécutées."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Quelle est la différence entre `print` et `return` dans une fonction ?","options":["Aucune, c'est pareil","`print` affiche à l'écran ; `return` renvoie une valeur réutilisable par le programme","`return` affiche en plus gros"],"answer":1,"explain":"Une fonction qui fait seulement `print` ne renvoie rien (en réalité, elle renvoie `None`). On ne peut pas réutiliser son résultat dans un calcul. Les fonctions utiles renvoient presque toujours leur résultat avec `return`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## print ou return ? La preuve"},{"t":"code","code":"def double_print(x):\n    print(x * 2)\n\ndef double_return(x):\n    return x * 2\n\nresultat1 = double_print(5)\nresultat2 = double_return(5)\nprint(\"resultat1 =\", resultat1)\nprint(\"resultat2 =\", resultat2)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`double_print` affiche 10, mais `resultat1` vaut `None` (rien). `double_return` n'affiche rien, mais `resultat2` vaut bien 10."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Une fonction peut contenir de la logique\n\nUne fonction peut contenir des conditions, des boucles, et plusieurs `return`."},{"t":"code","code":"def mention(note):\n    if note >= 16:\n        return \"Très bien\"\n    elif note >= 14:\n        return \"Bien\"\n    elif note >= 12:\n        return \"Assez bien\"\n    elif note >= 10:\n        return \"Admis\"\n    return \"Ajourné\"\n\nfor n in [17, 13, 8, 10]:\n    print(n, \"→\", mention(n))","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercices"},{"t":"exercise","id":"fonctions-ex1","title":"Surface d'un terrain","prompt":"Écris une fonction `surface(longueur, largeur)` qui **renvoie** (avec `return`) la surface d'un terrain rectangulaire.\n\nPuis affiche la surface d'un terrain de 25 m sur 18 m.","starter":"def surface(longueur, largeur):\n    ...\n\nprint(surface(25, 18))","solution":"def surface(longueur, largeur):\n    return longueur * largeur\n\nprint(surface(25, 18))","check":"assert \"surface\" in dir() and callable(surface), \"Définis une fonction nommée surface.\"\nassert surface(25, 18) == 450, f\"surface(25, 18) devrait renvoyer 450, elle renvoie {surface(25, 18)}.\"\nassert surface(3, 4) == 12, \"surface(3, 4) devrait renvoyer 12.\"\nassert \"450\" in OUT, \"Affiche le résultat de surface(25, 18).\"","hint":"Remplace les ... par return longueur * largeur","inputs":""},{"t":"exercise","id":"fonctions-ex2","title":"Est-ce un nombre pair ?","prompt":"Écris une fonction `est_pair(n)` qui renvoie `True` si `n` est pair, `False` sinon.\n\nPuis utilise-la dans une compréhension de liste pour créer `pairs`, la liste des nombres pairs de `nombres`.","starter":"def est_pair(n):\n    ...\n\nnombres = [3, 8, 15, 22, 41, 60]\npairs = \nprint(pairs)","solution":"def est_pair(n):\n    return n % 2 == 0\n\nnombres = [3, 8, 15, 22, 41, 60]\npairs = [n for n in nombres if est_pair(n)]\nprint(pairs)","check":"assert est_pair(4) is True and est_pair(7) is False and est_pair(0) is True, \"est_pair doit renvoyer True pour les nombres pairs et False pour les impairs.\"\nassert pairs == [8, 22, 60], f\"pairs devrait valoir [8, 22, 60], tu obtiens {pairs}.\"\nassert \"est_pair(\" in CODE.split(\"pairs =\")[1], \"Utilise ta fonction est_pair dans la compréhension de liste.\"","hint":"return n % 2 == 0, puis [n for n in nombres if est_pair(n)]","inputs":""},{"t":"exercise","id":"fonctions-ex3","title":"Le tarif du taxi","prompt":"Un taxi facture **500 francs de prise en charge**, plus **250 francs par kilomètre**. La nuit, le prix total est **majoré de 20 %**.\n\nÉcris une fonction `prix_course(km, nuit)` qui renvoie le prix, où `nuit` vaut `True` ou `False`.","starter":"def prix_course(km, nuit):\n    ...\n\nprint(prix_course(10, False))\nprint(prix_course(10, True))","solution":"def prix_course(km, nuit):\n    prix = 500 + 250 * km\n    if nuit:\n        prix = prix * 1.2\n    return prix\n\nprint(prix_course(10, False))\nprint(prix_course(10, True))","check":"assert prix_course(10, False) == 3000, f\"Pour 10 km de jour : 500 + 2500 = 3000. Ta fonction renvoie {prix_course(10, False)}.\"\nassert abs(prix_course(10, True) - 3600) < 1e-6, f\"Pour 10 km de nuit : 3000 × 1.2 = 3600. Ta fonction renvoie {prix_course(10, True)}.\"\nassert prix_course(0, False) == 500, \"Pour 0 km, il reste la prise en charge de 500 francs.\"","hint":"Calcule d'abord prix = 500 + 250 * km. Puis, si nuit est vrai, multiplie par 1.2. Enfin, return prix.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"fonctions-plus","title":"Fonctions, la suite","subtitle":"Valeurs par défaut, plusieurs résultats, portée et documentation.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Les paramètres par défaut\n\nUn paramètre peut avoir une **valeur par défaut**, utilisée si on ne la précise pas à l'appel."},{"t":"code","code":"def convertir(montant, taux=655.957):\n    return round(montant * taux)\n\nprint(convertir(10))           # taux par défaut\nprint(convertir(10, 600))      # taux précisé\nprint(convertir(taux=2.0, montant=5))  # par nom, dans n'importe quel ordre","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Les appels **par nom** (`taux=2.0`) rendent le code plus lisible quand une fonction a beaucoup de paramètres. Tu en croiseras partout dans pandas."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Renvoyer plusieurs valeurs\n\nUne fonction peut renvoyer plusieurs valeurs d'un coup, séparées par des virgules. En réalité, elle renvoie un **tuple**, qu'on déballe à l'arrivée."},{"t":"code","code":"def resume(valeurs):\n    return min(valeurs), max(valeurs), sum(valeurs) / len(valeurs)\n\nventes = [120, 95, 210, 180, 75]\nmini, maxi, moyenne = resume(ventes)\nprint(f\"min {mini}, max {maxi}, moyenne {moyenne}\")","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## La portée des variables\n\nUne variable créée **dans** une fonction n'existe que dans cette fonction. On dit qu'elle est **locale**."},{"t":"code","code":"def calcul():\n    secret = 42\n    return secret * 2\n\nprint(calcul())\nprint(secret)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`NameError` : à l'extérieur, `secret` n'existe pas. C'est une protection : chaque fonction a son espace à elle, et ne risque pas d'écraser les variables du reste du programme. Pour faire sortir une valeur, on utilise `return`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Documenter avec une docstring\n\nUne **docstring** est un texte entre triples guillemets placé juste sous le `def`. Elle explique ce que fait la fonction. C'est une excellente habitude."},{"t":"code","code":"def imc(poids, taille):\n    \"\"\"Calcule l'indice de masse corporelle.\n\n    poids : en kilogrammes\n    taille : en mètres\n    \"\"\"\n    return round(poids / taille ** 2, 1)\n\nprint(imc(70, 1.75))\nhelp(imc)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les fonctions lambda\n\nUne **lambda** est une mini-fonction sans nom, écrite sur une ligne. On l'utilise surtout pour dire à `sorted` (ou à pandas) **selon quoi** trier."},{"t":"code","code":"produits = [(\"Espresso\", 2.5), (\"Sandwich\", 5.0), (\"Beignet\", 1.0)]\npar_prix = sorted(produits, key=lambda p: p[1])\nprint(par_prix)\n\neleves = [{\"nom\": \"Awa\", \"note\": 15}, {\"nom\": \"Kofi\", \"note\": 12}, {\"nom\": \"Gloire\", \"note\": 17}]\nclassement = sorted(eleves, key=lambda e: e[\"note\"], reverse=True)\nprint([e[\"nom\"] for e in classement])","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`lambda p: p[1]` signifie : « à partir de `p`, renvoie `p[1]` », c'est-à-dire le prix."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercices"},{"t":"exercise","id":"fonctions-plus-ex1","title":"Les statistiques","prompt":"Écris une fonction `statistiques(valeurs)` qui renvoie **trois valeurs** : le minimum, le maximum et la moyenne **arrondie à 1 décimale**.","starter":"def statistiques(valeurs):\n    ...\n\nmini, maxi, moy = statistiques([12, 18, 7, 15, 10])\nprint(mini, maxi, moy)","solution":"def statistiques(valeurs):\n    moyenne = round(sum(valeurs) / len(valeurs), 1)\n    return min(valeurs), max(valeurs), moyenne\n\nmini, maxi, moy = statistiques([12, 18, 7, 15, 10])\nprint(mini, maxi, moy)","check":"r = statistiques([12, 18, 7, 15, 10])\nassert isinstance(r, tuple) and len(r) == 3, \"La fonction doit renvoyer trois valeurs séparées par des virgules.\"\nassert r == (7, 18, 12.4), f\"Attendu (7, 18, 12.4), obtenu {r}.\"\nassert statistiques([1, 2]) == (1, 2, 1.5), \"Teste ta fonction avec d'autres valeurs : statistiques([1, 2]) doit donner (1, 2, 1.5).\"","hint":"return min(valeurs), max(valeurs), round(sum(valeurs) / len(valeurs), 1)","inputs":""},{"t":"exercise","id":"fonctions-plus-ex2","title":"La remise","prompt":"Écris une fonction `appliquer_remise(prix, pourcentage=10)` qui renvoie le prix après remise, arrondi à 2 décimales. Par défaut, la remise est de 10 %.\n\nExemples : `appliquer_remise(50)` renvoie `45.0` ; `appliquer_remise(80, 25)` renvoie `60.0`.","starter":"def appliquer_remise(prix, pourcentage=10):\n    ...\n\nprint(appliquer_remise(50))\nprint(appliquer_remise(80, 25))","solution":"def appliquer_remise(prix, pourcentage=10):\n    return round(prix * (1 - pourcentage / 100), 2)\n\nprint(appliquer_remise(50))\nprint(appliquer_remise(80, 25))","check":"assert appliquer_remise(50) == 45.0, f\"appliquer_remise(50) devrait valoir 45.0, obtenu {appliquer_remise(50)}.\"\nassert appliquer_remise(80, 25) == 60.0, f\"appliquer_remise(80, 25) devrait valoir 60.0, obtenu {appliquer_remise(80, 25)}.\"\nassert appliquer_remise(19.99, 15) == 16.99, f\"appliquer_remise(19.99, 15) devrait valoir 16.99, obtenu {appliquer_remise(19.99, 15)}.\"","hint":"Une remise de 10 % revient à multiplier par (1 - 10 / 100), soit 0.9.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"modules","title":"Modules et bibliothèques","subtitle":"Utiliser le code des autres pour aller beaucoup plus vite.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Ne réinvente pas la roue\n\nPython est livré avec des centaines de **modules** : des fichiers de fonctions toutes prêtes, rangées par thème. On les charge avec `import`."},{"t":"code","code":"import math\n\nprint(math.sqrt(144))      # racine carrée\nprint(math.pi)\nprint(math.ceil(4.1))      # arrondi au-dessus\nprint(math.floor(4.9))     # arrondi en dessous","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"On écrit le nom du module, un point, puis la fonction : `math.sqrt`. Comme une adresse : « dans le module math, la fonction sqrt »."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Trois façons d'importer"},{"t":"code","code":"import statistics\nprint(statistics.mean([12, 15, 9]))\n\nfrom statistics import median\nprint(median([12, 15, 9, 20]))\n\nimport statistics as st\nprint(st.stdev([12, 15, 9, 20]))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"- `import module` : on écrit `module.fonction` ;\n- `from module import fonction` : on écrit directement `fonction` ;\n- `import module as alias` : un surnom plus court.\n\nLa troisième forme est celle que tu verras partout en data : `import pandas as pd`, `import numpy as np`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Le hasard avec random"},{"t":"code","code":"import random\n\nrandom.seed(7)  # rend le hasard reproductible\nprint(random.randint(1, 6))            # un dé\nprint(random.choice([\"pile\", \"face\"]))  # un choix au hasard\nequipe = [\"Awa\", \"Kofi\", \"Inès\", \"David\"]\nrandom.shuffle(equipe)\nprint(equipe)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`random.seed()` fixe le point de départ du hasard : avec la même graine, on obtient toujours les mêmes tirages. Indispensable pour qu'une analyse soit **reproductible**."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les dates avec datetime"},{"t":"code","code":"from datetime import date\n\naujourdhui = date.today()\nprint(aujourdhui)\nindependance = date(1960, 6, 30)\nprint(independance.year)\necart = aujourdhui - independance\nprint(f\"{ecart.days} jours depuis le 30 juin 1960\")\nprint(independance.strftime(\"%d/%m/%Y\"))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Soustraire deux dates donne une durée, dont on lit le nombre de jours avec `.days`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Et les bibliothèques externes ?\n\nAu-delà des modules fournis avec Python, il existe des centaines de milliers de **bibliothèques** créées par la communauté. On les installe avec l'outil **pip**. Sur ton ordinateur, dans un terminal :"},{"t":"code","code":"pip install pandas matplotlib","run":false,"lang":"bash"},{"t":"md","v":"Sur ce site, pandas, NumPy et Matplotlib sont déjà disponibles : tu les utiliseras au prochain chapitre."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Que fait `import pandas as pd` ?","options":["Il installe pandas","Il charge la bibliothèque pandas et lui donne le surnom `pd`","Il crée une variable pd qui vaut \"pandas\""],"answer":1,"explain":"`import` charge une bibliothèque déjà installée (l'installation se fait avec pip). `as pd` permet ensuite d'écrire `pd.read_csv(...)` au lieu de `pandas.read_csv(...)`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : simuler des dés"},{"t":"exercise","id":"modules-ex1","title":"1000 lancers de dé","prompt":"Simule **1000 lancers** d'un dé à 6 faces avec `random.randint(1, 6)`, après avoir fixé la graine avec `random.seed(42)`.\n\nCompte dans `nb_six` le nombre de fois où le dé tombe sur 6, puis calcule `pourcentage` (le pourcentage de 6, arrondi à 1 décimale).\n\nEn théorie, on attend environ 16,7 %.","starter":"import random\n\nrandom.seed(42)\nnb_six = 0\n\nprint(nb_six, pourcentage)","solution":"import random\n\nrandom.seed(42)\nnb_six = 0\nfor i in range(1000):\n    if random.randint(1, 6) == 6:\n        nb_six += 1\npourcentage = round(nb_six / 1000 * 100, 1)\n\nprint(nb_six, pourcentage)","check":"import random as _r\n_r.seed(42)\nattendu = sum(1 for _ in range(1000) if _r.randint(1, 6) == 6)\nassert nb_six == attendu, f\"Avec la graine 42 et 1000 lancers, on obtient {attendu} six. Tu en comptes {nb_six}. As-tu bien appelé random.seed(42) avant la boucle, et une seule fois randint par tour ?\"\nassert pourcentage == round(attendu / 10, 1), f\"Le pourcentage devrait être {round(attendu / 10, 1)}.\"","hint":"Une boucle for i in range(1000), un if random.randint(1, 6) == 6, et un compteur. Le pourcentage : nb_six / 1000 * 100.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : jours restants"},{"t":"exercise","id":"modules-ex2","title":"Compte à rebours","prompt":"Avec le module `datetime`, calcule `jours` : le nombre de jours entre le **1er janvier 2026** et le **14 juillet 2026**.","starter":"from datetime import date\n\njours = \nprint(jours)","solution":"from datetime import date\n\njours = (date(2026, 7, 14) - date(2026, 1, 1)).days\nprint(jours)","check":"assert jours == 194, f\"Il y a 194 jours entre ces deux dates, tu obtiens {jours}.\"\nassert \"date(\" in CODE, \"Utilise date(...) du module datetime.\"","hint":"Crée les deux dates avec date(année, mois, jour), soustrais-les, puis prends .days","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"fichiers","title":"Fichiers et gestion des erreurs","subtitle":"Lire des données et ne pas planter au premier imprévu.","duration":14,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Lire un fichier texte\n\nLes données vivent souvent dans des **fichiers**. Sur ce site, quelques fichiers sont déjà là pour toi. Lisons `courses.txt`, une liste de courses :"},{"t":"code","code":"with open(\"courses.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fichier:\n    contenu = fichier.read()\nprint(contenu)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"- `open(nom)` ouvre le fichier ;\n- `with ... as fichier:` garantit que le fichier sera **refermé** proprement à la fin du bloc ;\n- `.read()` lit tout le contenu d'un coup, sous forme de texte."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Ligne par ligne\n\nSouvent, on traite un fichier **ligne par ligne** :"},{"t":"code","code":"with open(\"courses.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fichier:\n    for numero, ligne in enumerate(fichier, start=1):\n        article = ligne.strip()\n        print(f\"{numero}. {article.capitalize()}\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Chaque ligne lue se termine par un retour à la ligne invisible (`\\n`). `.strip()` l'enlève."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Écrire dans un fichier\n\nAvec le mode `\"w\"` (*write*), on crée (ou on écrase) un fichier. Le mode `\"a\"` (*append*) ajoute à la fin."},{"t":"code","code":"with open(\"journal.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"Lundi : 120 ventes\\n\")\n    f.write(\"Mardi : 95 ventes\\n\")\n\nwith open(\"journal.txt\", \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"Mercredi : 210 ventes\\n\")\n\nwith open(\"journal.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    print(f.read())","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les fichiers CSV\n\nLe format **CSV** (*Comma-Separated Values*) est le format de données le plus répandu : un tableau en texte, une ligne par enregistrement, des virgules entre les colonnes. Tu peux l'ouvrir avec Excel.\n\nVoici le début du fichier `ventes.csv`, qui contient les ventes 2024 d'une chaîne de cafés :"},{"t":"code","code":"with open(\"ventes.csv\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    for i in range(6):\n        print(f.readline().strip())","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Le module `csv` lit chaque ligne comme un dictionnaire, avec les noms de colonnes comme clés :"},{"t":"code","code":"import csv\n\ntotal_quantite = 0\nwith open(\"ventes.csv\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    for ligne in csv.DictReader(f):\n        total_quantite += int(ligne[\"quantite\"])\nprint(\"Articles vendus en 2024 :\", total_quantite)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Tout est lu comme du texte : d'où le `int(...)`. Au prochain chapitre, pandas fera tout ça (et bien plus) en une ligne."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Gérer les erreurs : try / except\n\nLes données réelles sont pleines de surprises : une case vide, un « N/A », une faute de frappe. Plutôt que de laisser le programme planter, on peut **attraper** l'erreur avec `try` / `except`."},{"t":"code","code":"valeurs = [\"12\", \"7\", \"N/A\", \"15\", \"\", \"9\"]\ntotal = 0\nignorees = 0\nfor v in valeurs:\n    try:\n        total += int(v)\n    except ValueError:\n        ignorees += 1\nprint(f\"Total : {total} ({ignorees} valeurs ignorées)\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Python **essaie** le bloc `try`. Si une `ValueError` survient, il exécute le bloc `except` au lieu de planter, et continue."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"N'attrape que les erreurs que tu attends (ici `ValueError`). Un `except:` tout seul cacherait aussi tes vraies fautes de programmation."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : la liste de courses"},{"t":"exercise","id":"fichiers-ex1","title":"Compter les articles","prompt":"Lis le fichier `courses.txt` ligne par ligne et crée la liste `articles` qui contient chaque article **en majuscules**, sans le retour à la ligne.\n\nPuis affiche le nombre d'articles.","starter":"articles = []\n\nprint(len(articles))","solution":"articles = []\nwith open(\"courses.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    for ligne in f:\n        articles.append(ligne.strip().upper())\n\nprint(len(articles))","check":"assert \"open(\" in CODE, \"Ouvre le fichier avec open().\"\nassert articles == [\"RIZ\", \"HUILE\", \"TOMATES\", \"OIGNONS\", \"PAIN\", \"LAIT\", \"CAFÉ\"], f\"articles devrait contenir les 7 articles en majuscules. Obtenu : {articles}\"\nassert \"7\" in OUT, \"Affiche le nombre d'articles.\"","hint":"with open(\"courses.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f: puis for ligne in f: puis articles.append(ligne.strip().upper())","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : une conversion sans risque"},{"t":"exercise","id":"fichiers-ex2","title":"Le convertisseur prudent","prompt":"Écris une fonction `en_nombre(texte)` qui convertit un texte en nombre à virgule avec `float()`. Si la conversion échoue, elle renvoie `None` au lieu de planter.\n\nEnsuite, calcule `total` : la somme des valeurs de `brut` qui ont pu être converties.","starter":"def en_nombre(texte):\n    ...\n\nbrut = [\"12.5\", \"abc\", \"7\", \"\", \"3.25\", \"N/A\"]\ntotal = \nprint(total)","solution":"def en_nombre(texte):\n    try:\n        return float(texte)\n    except ValueError:\n        return None\n\nbrut = [\"12.5\", \"abc\", \"7\", \"\", \"3.25\", \"N/A\"]\ntotal = sum(x for x in (en_nombre(t) for t in brut) if x is not None)\nprint(total)","check":"assert en_nombre(\"3.5\") == 3.5, \"en_nombre(\\\"3.5\\\") doit renvoyer 3.5.\"\nassert en_nombre(\"abc\") is None, \"en_nombre(\\\"abc\\\") doit renvoyer None (et ne pas planter).\"\nassert \"try\" in CODE and \"except\" in CODE, \"Utilise try / except dans ta fonction.\"\nassert abs(total - 22.75) < 1e-9, f\"Le total des valeurs valides est 22.75, tu obtiens {total}.\"","hint":"Dans la fonction : try: return float(texte), puis except ValueError: return None. Pour le total, une boucle qui ajoute seulement les résultats différents de None.","inputs":""}]]}]},{"id":"data","icon":"📊","title":"Analyser des données","desc":"NumPy, pandas et matplotlib sur de vraies données, jusqu'au projet final.","badge":"Analyste de données","data":true,"lessons":[{"id":"numpy","title":"NumPy, calculer sur des tableaux","subtitle":"Faire un calcul sur un million de nombres en une ligne.","duration":14,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Bienvenue dans la data\n\nTu connais maintenant les bases de Python. Place aux outils des analystes de données. Le premier s'appelle **NumPy** (*Numerical Python*). Presque toutes les bibliothèques de data reposent dessus, pandas compris.\n\nSon objet central : le **tableau** (*array*), une sorte de liste de nombres ultra-rapide."},{"t":"code","code":"import numpy as np\n\nprix = np.array([2.5, 3.5, 2.0, 2.2, 1.8])\nprint(prix)\nprint(type(prix))","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"info","title":"","v":"La première fois, le chargement de NumPy prend quelques secondes. Ensuite, c'est instantané."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Calculer sur tout le tableau d'un coup\n\nAvec une liste, pour augmenter chaque prix de 10 %, il fallait une boucle. Avec NumPy, l'opération s'applique **à chaque élément automatiquement** :"},{"t":"code","code":"import numpy as np\n\nprix = np.array([2.5, 3.5, 2.0, 2.2, 1.8])\nquantites = np.array([10, 4, 7, 12, 20])\n\nprint(prix * 1.1)\nprint(prix * quantites)\nprint(\"Chiffre d'affaires :\", (prix * quantites).sum())","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`prix * quantites` multiplie les éléments **deux à deux** : le premier prix par la première quantité, etc. On appelle ça la **vectorisation**. C'est plus court à écrire, et des centaines de fois plus rapide sur de gros volumes."},{"t":"code","code":"import numpy as np\n\nliste = list(range(1000000))\ntableau = np.arange(1000000)\nprint(sum(x * 2 for x in liste))\nprint((tableau * 2).sum())","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les statistiques en une ligne"},{"t":"code","code":"import numpy as np\n\nnotes = np.array([12, 15, 9, 17, 14, 11, 16, 8, 13, 18])\nprint(\"Moyenne :\", notes.mean())\nprint(\"Médiane :\", np.median(notes))\nprint(\"Écart-type :\", round(notes.std(), 2))\nprint(\"Min / max :\", notes.min(), notes.max())","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"- La **moyenne** est la somme divisée par le nombre de valeurs.\n- La **médiane** est la valeur du milieu quand on trie : la moitié des valeurs est en dessous, l'autre au-dessus. Elle résiste mieux aux valeurs extrêmes.\n- L'**écart-type** mesure la dispersion : petit, les valeurs sont proches de la moyenne ; grand, elles sont étalées."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Dans une entreprise, 9 salariés gagnent 400 000 francs par mois et le directeur 8 000 000. Quel indicateur décrit le mieux le salaire « typique » ?","options":["La moyenne","La médiane","Le maximum"],"answer":1,"explain":"La moyenne (1 160 000) est tirée vers le haut par un seul salaire extrême. La médiane (400 000) reflète bien la situation de la majorité. Un réflexe essentiel d'analyste !"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Filtrer avec un masque\n\nUne comparaison sur un tableau donne un tableau de `True` / `False`, qu'on appelle un **masque**. On l'utilise entre crochets pour **filtrer**."},{"t":"code","code":"import numpy as np\n\ntemperatures = np.array([28, 31, 35, 30, 27, 33, 36])\nmasque = temperatures > 32\nprint(masque)\nprint(temperatures[masque])\nprint(\"Jours chauds :\", masque.sum())","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`masque.sum()` compte les `True` (chaque `True` vaut 1). Cette technique de filtrage est **exactement** celle qu'utilise pandas. Retiens-la bien."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : conversion d'une semaine"},{"t":"exercise","id":"numpy-ex1","title":"Les prix en francs CFA","prompt":"Le tableau `prix_eur` contient des prix en euros. Sans boucle, crée :\n\n- `prix_fcfa` : les prix convertis en francs CFA (1 euro = 655.957 francs), arrondis à l'unité avec `np.round` ;\n- `nb_chers` : le nombre de prix supérieurs à 2000 francs CFA.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\nprix_eur = np.array([2.5, 3.5, 2.0, 2.2, 1.8, 1.0, 5.0])\n\nprix_fcfa = \nnb_chers = \nprint(prix_fcfa)\nprint(nb_chers)","solution":"import numpy as np\n\nprix_eur = np.array([2.5, 3.5, 2.0, 2.2, 1.8, 1.0, 5.0])\n\nprix_fcfa = np.round(prix_eur * 655.957)\nnb_chers = (prix_fcfa > 2000).sum()\nprint(prix_fcfa)\nprint(nb_chers)","check":"import numpy as _np\nassert \"for\" not in CODE, \"Pas de boucle : laisse NumPy calculer sur tout le tableau.\"\nattendu = _np.round(_np.array([2.5, 3.5, 2.0, 2.2, 1.8, 1.0, 5.0]) * 655.957)\nassert _np.allclose(prix_fcfa, attendu), f\"prix_fcfa attendu : {attendu}\"\nassert int(nb_chers) == 2, f\"Il y a 2 prix au-dessus de 2000 francs CFA, tu en comptes {nb_chers}.\"","hint":"np.round(prix_eur * 655.957), puis (prix_fcfa > 2000).sum()","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"pandas","title":"Pandas, ton tableur en Python","subtitle":"Charger, explorer et comprendre un fichier de données.","duration":15,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Le DataFrame\n\n**Pandas** est LA bibliothèque d'analyse de données en Python. Son objet principal, le **DataFrame**, est un tableau avec des lignes et des colonnes nommées. Comme une feuille Excel, mais pilotée par du code."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"produit\": [\"Espresso\", \"Croissant\", \"Sandwich\"],\n    \"prix\": [2.5, 1.8, 5.0],\n    \"stock\": [120, 40, 15],\n})\ndf","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Remarque : ici, pas de `print`. Quand la **dernière ligne** d'un bloc est une valeur, elle s'affiche automatiquement, et pandas la présente sous forme de joli tableau. C'est le fonctionnement des notebooks Jupyter, l'outil préféré des analystes."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Charger un vrai fichier\n\nEn pratique, on ne tape pas les données : on les **charge**. `pd.read_csv` lit un fichier CSV et crée un DataFrame. Voici les ventes 2024 d'une chaîne de cafés présente dans cinq villes."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\n\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes.head()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`head()` affiche les 5 premières lignes (`head(10)` pour 10). `tail()` affiche les dernières. Ce sont les premières choses qu'on regarde avec un nouveau fichier."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les premières questions à poser\n\nFace à un nouveau jeu de données, un analyste vérifie toujours :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\nprint(\"Dimensions :\", ventes.shape)       # (lignes, colonnes)\nprint(\"Colonnes :\", list(ventes.columns))\nprint()\nprint(ventes.dtypes)                      # type de chaque colonne","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`shape` donne le nombre de lignes et de colonnes. `dtypes` donne le type de chaque colonne : `int64` (entiers), `float64` (nombres à virgule), `object` (texte)."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes.info()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`info()` résume tout : nombre de lignes, colonnes, types, et nombre de valeurs **non manquantes** par colonne."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Une colonne = une Series\n\nUne colonne s'obtient avec des crochets et son nom. Elle est de type **Series** : un tableau NumPy avec des étiquettes. Tout ce que tu sais de NumPy s'applique."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\nq = ventes[\"quantite\"]\nprint(\"Total vendu :\", q.sum())\nprint(\"Moyenne par vente :\", round(q.mean(), 2))\nprint(\"Plus grosse vente :\", q.max())","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Et `describe()` calcule d'un coup les statistiques principales de toutes les colonnes numériques :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes.describe()","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Compter les valeurs\n\nPour une colonne de texte, la question naturelle est : combien de fois apparaît chaque valeur ? C'est le rôle de `value_counts()`."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"ville\"].value_counts()","run":true},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nprint(\"Produits différents :\", ventes[\"produit\"].nunique())\nventes[\"produit\"].value_counts(normalize=True).round(3)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`nunique()` compte les valeurs distinctes. `normalize=True` donne des proportions plutôt que des nombres."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Tu reçois un nouveau fichier de données. Quelle instruction montre ses premières lignes ?","options":["`df.shape`","`df.head()`","`df.describe()`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`head()` affiche les premières lignes. `shape` donne les dimensions, `describe()` les statistiques."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : les étudiants"},{"t":"exercise","id":"pandas-ex1","title":"Première exploration","prompt":"Charge le fichier `etudiants.csv` dans un DataFrame nommé `df`. Puis calcule :\n\n- `nb_etudiants` : le nombre de lignes ;\n- `moyenne_python` : la moyenne de la colonne `note_python`, arrondie à 2 décimales ;\n- `ville_top` : la ville qui compte le plus d'étudiants (astuce : `value_counts()` trie du plus grand au plus petit, et `.index[0]` donne la première étiquette).","starter":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = \n\nnb_etudiants = \nmoyenne_python = \nville_top = \n\nprint(nb_etudiants, moyenne_python, ville_top)","solution":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"etudiants.csv\")\n\nnb_etudiants = len(df)\nmoyenne_python = round(df[\"note_python\"].mean(), 2)\nville_top = df[\"ville\"].value_counts().index[0]\n\nprint(nb_etudiants, moyenne_python, ville_top)","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_ref = _pd.read_csv(\"etudiants.csv\")\nassert isinstance(df, _pd.DataFrame), \"df doit être un DataFrame créé avec pd.read_csv(\\\"etudiants.csv\\\").\"\nassert nb_etudiants == len(_ref), f\"Il y a {len(_ref)} étudiants, tu en comptes {nb_etudiants}.\"\nassert moyenne_python == round(_ref[\"note_python\"].mean(), 2), f\"La moyenne attendue est {round(_ref['note_python'].mean(), 2)}.\"\nassert ville_top == _ref[\"ville\"].value_counts().index[0], f\"La ville la plus représentée est {_ref['ville'].value_counts().index[0]}.\"","hint":"pd.read_csv(\"etudiants.csv\") ; len(df) ou df.shape[0] ; round(df[\"note_python\"].mean(), 2)","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"selection","title":"Sélectionner, filtrer, trier","subtitle":"Poser des questions précises à tes données.","duration":15,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Choisir des colonnes\n\nUne colonne : des crochets simples. Plusieurs colonnes : une **liste** de noms entre les crochets (d'où les doubles crochets)."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\nventes[[\"date\", \"ville\", \"produit\"]].head()","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Filtrer des lignes\n\nC'est la technique du **masque** vue avec NumPy. La comparaison donne une Series de `True` / `False`, et on la met entre crochets pour garder les lignes `True`."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\na_dakar = ventes[ventes[\"ville\"] == \"Dakar\"]\nprint(len(a_dakar), \"ventes à Dakar\")\na_dakar.head()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Lis-le comme une phrase : « les ventes **où** la ville **est égale à** Dakar »."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\ngrosses = ventes[ventes[\"quantite\"] >= 15]\ngrosses.head()","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Plusieurs conditions\n\nPour combiner des conditions, pandas utilise `&` (et), `|` (ou) et `~` (non), **avec des parenthèses autour de chaque condition**."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\nfiltre = (ventes[\"ville\"] == \"Kinshasa\") & (ventes[\"categorie\"] == \"Viennoiserie\")\nventes[filtre].head()","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"En pandas, on n'utilise pas `and` / `or` mais `&` / `|`. Et les parenthèses autour de chaque condition sont obligatoires. Oublier l'un ou l'autre est l'erreur la plus fréquente des débutants."},{"t":"md","v":"Pour tester l'appartenance à une liste de valeurs, `isin` est bien plus pratique qu'une série de `|` :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\nafrique = ventes[ventes[\"ville\"].isin([\"Kinshasa\", \"Abidjan\", \"Dakar\"])]\nprint(len(afrique), \"ventes en Afrique sur\", len(ventes))","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Trier\n\n`sort_values` trie selon une ou plusieurs colonnes. Par défaut, du plus petit au plus grand ; `ascending=False` inverse l'ordre."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\nventes.sort_values(\"quantite\", ascending=False).head(5)","run":true},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\nventes.sort_values([\"ville\", \"quantite\"], ascending=[True, False]).head(8)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`nlargest(n, colonne)` est un raccourci pour « les n plus grandes valeurs »."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## loc : lignes et colonnes en même temps\n\n`df.loc[lignes, colonnes]` sélectionne des lignes (souvent avec un filtre) **et** des colonnes en une fois :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\nventes.loc[ventes[\"produit\"] == \"Jus de bissap\", [\"date\", \"ville\", \"quantite\"]].head()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`iloc` fait la même chose avec des **positions** numériques : `ventes.iloc[0:3, 0:2]` donne les 3 premières lignes et les 2 premières colonnes."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Quelle ligne sélectionne les ventes de Paris de plus de 10 articles ?","options":["`ventes[ventes[\"ville\"] == \"Paris\" and ventes[\"quantite\"] > 10]`","`ventes[(ventes[\"ville\"] == \"Paris\") & (ventes[\"quantite\"] > 10)]`","`ventes[\"Paris\", \"quantite\" > 10]`"],"answer":1,"explain":"Avec pandas : `&` à la place de `and`, et des parenthèses autour de chaque condition."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercices"},{"t":"exercise","id":"selection-ex1","title":"Les bons élèves","prompt":"À partir de `etudiants.csv` :\n\n1. crée `excellents` : les étudiants qui ont **au moins 15** en `note_python`, triés du meilleur au moins bon ;\n2. crée `abidjan_travailleurs` : les étudiants d'**Abidjan** qui ont étudié **plus de 10 heures** (`heures_etude > 10`).","starter":"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"etudiants.csv\")\n\nexcellents = \nabidjan_travailleurs = \n\nexcellents","solution":"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"etudiants.csv\")\n\nexcellents = df[df[\"note_python\"] >= 15].sort_values(\"note_python\", ascending=False)\nabidjan_travailleurs = df[(df[\"ville\"] == \"Abidjan\") & (df[\"heures_etude\"] > 10)]\n\nexcellents","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_d = _pd.read_csv(\"etudiants.csv\")\n_e = _d[_d[\"note_python\"] >= 15].sort_values(\"note_python\", ascending=False)\nassert isinstance(excellents, _pd.DataFrame), \"excellents doit être un DataFrame filtré.\"\nassert list(excellents[\"nom\"]) == list(_e[\"nom\"]), \"excellents doit contenir les étudiants avec note_python >= 15, du meilleur au moins bon.\"\n_a = _d[(_d[\"ville\"] == \"Abidjan\") & (_d[\"heures_etude\"] > 10)]\nassert set(abidjan_travailleurs[\"nom\"]) == set(_a[\"nom\"]) and len(abidjan_travailleurs) == len(_a), f\"abidjan_travailleurs devrait contenir {len(_a)} étudiant(s) : {list(_a['nom'])}.\"","hint":"df[df[\"note_python\"] >= 15].sort_values(\"note_python\", ascending=False). Pour le second : deux conditions entre parenthèses, reliées par &.","inputs":""},{"t":"exercise","id":"selection-ex2","title":"La plus grosse vente de bissap","prompt":"Dans `ventes.csv`, trouve la vente de **Jus de bissap** avec la plus grande quantité. Range sa **date** dans `date_record` et sa **ville** dans `ville_record`.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\nbissap = \nmeilleure = \ndate_record = \nville_record = \nprint(date_record, ville_record)","solution":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\nbissap = ventes[ventes[\"produit\"] == \"Jus de bissap\"]\nmeilleure = bissap.sort_values(\"quantite\", ascending=False).iloc[0]\ndate_record = meilleure[\"date\"]\nville_record = meilleure[\"ville\"]\nprint(date_record, ville_record)","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n_b = _v[_v[\"produit\"] == \"Jus de bissap\"]\n_max = _b[\"quantite\"].max()\n_ok = _b[_b[\"quantite\"] == _max]\nassert any((r[\"date\"] == date_record and r[\"ville\"] == ville_record) for _, r in _ok.iterrows()), f\"La plus grosse vente de bissap fait {_max} unités. Vérifie ta date et ta ville.\"","hint":"Filtre les lignes du produit, trie par quantité décroissante, puis .iloc[0] donne la première ligne. On lit ensuite ses colonnes avec [\"date\"] et [\"ville\"].","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"nettoyage","title":"Nettoyer les données","subtitle":"Valeurs manquantes, doublons et fautes de saisie.","duration":16,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## 80 % du travail\n\nOn dit souvent qu'un analyste passe 80 % de son temps à **nettoyer** les données. Les fichiers réels sont saisis par des humains, et ils contiennent des trous, des doublons et des incohérences.\n\nVoici `clients.csv`, le fichier clients d'une boutique. Regarde bien :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nclients = pd.read_csv(\"clients.csv\")\nclients.head(12)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Tu remarques des cases `NaN` (*Not a Number*) : ce sont des **valeurs manquantes**. Et la colonne `ville` a l'air… étrange."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Repérer les valeurs manquantes"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nclients = pd.read_csv(\"clients.csv\")\n\nprint(clients.isna().sum())\nprint()\nprint(\"Lignes complètes :\", len(clients.dropna()), \"sur\", len(clients))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`isna()` renvoie `True` pour chaque case vide ; `.sum()` les compte par colonne. Trois stratégies possibles :\n\n- **supprimer** les lignes incomplètes avec `dropna()` (simple, mais on perd des données) ;\n- **remplir** les trous avec `fillna(valeur)`, par exemple la médiane de la colonne ;\n- **garder** les trous, si l'analyse le permet (la plupart des calculs pandas les ignorent)."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nclients = pd.read_csv(\"clients.csv\")\n\nmediane = clients[\"age\"].median()\nclients[\"age\"] = clients[\"age\"].fillna(mediane)\nprint(\"Âges manquants restants :\", clients[\"age\"].isna().sum())","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les doublons\n\nUne même ligne saisie deux fois fausse tous les comptes."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nclients = pd.read_csv(\"clients.csv\")\n\nprint(\"Doublons :\", clients.duplicated().sum())\nclients = clients.drop_duplicates()\nprint(\"Après nettoyage :\", len(clients), \"lignes\")","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Harmoniser le texte\n\nCombien y a-t-il de villes différentes ?"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nclients = pd.read_csv(\"clients.csv\")\nclients[\"ville\"].value_counts()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Pour pandas, `\"Kinshasa\"`, `\" kinshasa\"` et `\"KINSHASA\"` sont trois villes différentes ! Les méthodes de texte vues au chapitre 3 s'appliquent à toute une colonne grâce à `.str` :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nclients = pd.read_csv(\"clients.csv\")\n\nclients[\"ville\"] = clients[\"ville\"].str.strip().str.title()\nclients[\"ville\"].value_counts()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Cinq villes, comme dans la réalité."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les bons types\n\nLes dates sont lues comme du texte. `pd.to_datetime` les convertit en vraies dates, ce qui permet d'en extraire l'année, le mois ou le jour de la semaine :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nclients = pd.read_csv(\"clients.csv\")\n\nclients[\"inscription\"] = pd.to_datetime(clients[\"inscription\"])\nclients[\"annee\"] = clients[\"inscription\"].dt.year\nclients[\"mois\"] = clients[\"inscription\"].dt.month\nclients[[\"inscription\", \"annee\", \"mois\"]].head()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Tu viens aussi de voir comment **créer une colonne** : `df[\"nouvelle\"] = ...`. On peut y mettre un calcul sur d'autres colonnes, comme avec NumPy."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Quelle instruction transforme `\"  dakar \"` en `\"Dakar\"` dans toute la colonne `ville` ?","options":["`clients[\"ville\"].strip().title()`","`clients[\"ville\"].str.strip().str.title()`","`clients.title(\"ville\")`"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sur une colonne pandas, les méthodes de texte passent par `.str`. Sans lui, Python cherche une méthode `strip` sur la Series elle-même, qui n'existe pas."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : le grand nettoyage"},{"t":"exercise","id":"nettoyage-ex1","title":"Nettoyer clients.csv","prompt":"Crée un DataFrame `propre` à partir de `clients.csv`, en appliquant dans l'ordre :\n\n1. suppression des doublons ;\n2. harmonisation de la colonne `ville` (espaces retirés, majuscule au début) ;\n3. remplacement des âges manquants par la **médiane** des âges (calculée après suppression des doublons) ;\n4. suppression des lignes dont la `depense` est manquante.\n\nAffiche ensuite le nombre de lignes et le nombre de villes distinctes.","starter":"import pandas as pd\n\npropre = pd.read_csv(\"clients.csv\")\n# 1.\n# 2.\n# 3.\n# 4.\n\nprint(len(propre), propre[\"ville\"].nunique())","solution":"import pandas as pd\n\npropre = pd.read_csv(\"clients.csv\")\npropre = propre.drop_duplicates()\npropre[\"ville\"] = propre[\"ville\"].str.strip().str.title()\npropre[\"age\"] = propre[\"age\"].fillna(propre[\"age\"].median())\npropre = propre.dropna(subset=[\"depense\"])\n\nprint(len(propre), propre[\"ville\"].nunique())","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_c = _pd.read_csv(\"clients.csv\").drop_duplicates()\n_c[\"ville\"] = _c[\"ville\"].str.strip().str.title()\n_c[\"age\"] = _c[\"age\"].fillna(_c[\"age\"].median())\n_c = _c.dropna(subset=[\"depense\"])\nassert propre.duplicated().sum() == 0, \"Il reste des doublons : utilise drop_duplicates().\"\nassert propre[\"ville\"].nunique() == 5, f\"Il devrait rester 5 villes distinctes, il y en a {propre['ville'].nunique()} : {sorted(propre['ville'].unique())}\"\nassert propre[\"age\"].isna().sum() == 0, \"Il reste des âges manquants : remplis-les avec fillna.\"\nassert propre[\"depense\"].isna().sum() == 0, \"Il reste des dépenses manquantes : supprime ces lignes avec dropna(subset=[\\\"depense\\\"]).\"\nassert len(propre) == len(_c), f\"Le tableau nettoyé devrait avoir {len(_c)} lignes, le tien en a {len(propre)}.\"\nassert abs(propre[\"age\"].sum() - _c[\"age\"].sum()) < 1e-6, \"Les âges manquants doivent être remplacés par la médiane calculée après suppression des doublons.\"","hint":"drop_duplicates() ; .str.strip().str.title() ; fillna(propre[\"age\"].median()) ; dropna(subset=[\"depense\"]) pour ne regarder que cette colonne.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"groupby","title":"Regrouper et résumer","subtitle":"groupby, la question « par ville », « par mois », « par produit ».","duration":16,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## La question la plus fréquente\n\n« Quel est le chiffre d'affaires **par ville** ? » « Combien de ventes **par mois** ? » « Quelle note moyenne **par classe** ? »\n\nToutes ces questions ont la même forme : **découper** les données en groupes, **calculer** quelque chose dans chaque groupe, puis **rassembler** les résultats. En pandas, c'est `groupby`.\n\nCommençons par ajouter une colonne `total` (le montant de chaque vente) :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\nventes.head()","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Un premier groupby"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n\nventes.groupby(\"ville\")[\"total\"].sum()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Lis-le de gauche à droite : « dans `ventes`, **regroupe par** ville, prends la colonne `total`, et fais la **somme** ». Le résultat est une Series avec une valeur par ville."},{"t":"html","v":"<div class=\"flow\">\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Découper</b><span>Une pile de lignes par ville.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Calculer</b><span>La somme des totaux dans chaque pile.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Rassembler</b><span>Un tableau : une ligne par ville.</span></div>\n</div>"},{"t":"md","v":"On peut ensuite trier le résultat :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n\nventes.groupby(\"produit\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False).round(2)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Les fonctions d'agrégation\n\nÀ la place de `sum()`, on peut utiliser toutes les fonctions de résumé :\n\n| Fonction | Calcule |\n|---|---|\n| `sum()` | la somme |\n| `mean()` | la moyenne |\n| `median()` | la médiane |\n| `count()` | le nombre de lignes |\n| `min()` / `max()` | le minimum / maximum |\n| `nunique()` | le nombre de valeurs distinctes |\n\nEt `agg` en calcule plusieurs d'un coup :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n\nventes.groupby(\"ville\").agg(\n    nb_ventes=(\"total\", \"count\"),\n    chiffre_affaires=(\"total\", \"sum\"),\n    panier_moyen=(\"total\", \"mean\"),\n).round(2)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Regrouper par mois\n\nPour regrouper dans le temps, on convertit d'abord la date, puis on en extrait le mois avec `.dt.month` :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\nventes[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(ventes[\"date\"])\nventes[\"mois\"] = ventes[\"date\"].dt.month\n\nventes.groupby(\"mois\")[\"total\"].sum().round(0)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Observe les chiffres : certains mois vendent nettement plus. On creusera ça dans le projet final."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Plusieurs critères : le tableau croisé\n\nOn peut regrouper selon **deux** colonnes à la fois. `pivot_table` présente le résultat sous forme de tableau croisé, lignes × colonnes, comme dans Excel :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n\npd.pivot_table(ventes, values=\"total\", index=\"ville\", columns=\"categorie\", aggfunc=\"sum\").round(0)","run":true},{"t":"quiz","q":"Quelle instruction donne la **quantité moyenne** vendue pour chaque produit ?","options":["`ventes.groupby(\"quantite\")[\"produit\"].mean()`","`ventes.groupby(\"produit\")[\"quantite\"].mean()`","`ventes[\"produit\"].mean(\"quantite\")`"],"answer":1,"explain":"On regroupe par ce qui définit les groupes (le produit), puis on choisit la colonne à résumer (la quantité) et la fonction (la moyenne)."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercices"},{"t":"exercise","id":"groupby-ex1","title":"Le produit star","prompt":"À partir de `ventes.csv` :\n\n1. crée la colonne `total` (quantité × prix unitaire) ;\n2. crée `ca_produit` : le chiffre d'affaires par produit, trié du plus grand au plus petit ;\n3. range dans `produit_star` le nom du produit qui rapporte le plus.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\nca_produit = \nproduit_star = \nprint(produit_star)\nca_produit","solution":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n\nca_produit = ventes.groupby(\"produit\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False)\nproduit_star = ca_produit.index[0]\nprint(produit_star)\nca_produit","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n_v[\"total\"] = _v[\"quantite\"] * _v[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n_r = _v.groupby(\"produit\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False)\nassert \"total\" in ventes.columns, \"Crée la colonne total dans ventes.\"\nassert list(ca_produit.index) == list(_r.index), \"ca_produit doit être trié du plus grand au plus petit chiffre d'affaires.\"\nassert abs(ca_produit.iloc[0] - _r.iloc[0]) < 1e-6, \"Vérifie le calcul du chiffre d'affaires par produit.\"\nassert produit_star == _r.index[0], f\"Le produit qui rapporte le plus est {_r.index[0]}.\"","hint":"ventes.groupby(\"produit\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False), puis .index[0] pour le premier nom.","inputs":""},{"t":"exercise","id":"groupby-ex2","title":"Travail et réussite","prompt":"Dans `etudiants.csv`, on veut savoir si les heures d'étude comptent.\n\n1. crée une colonne `groupe` qui vaut `\"plus de 10 h\"` si `heures_etude > 10`, et `\"10 h ou moins\"` sinon (astuce : `np.where(condition, valeur_si_vrai, valeur_si_faux)`) ;\n2. crée `moyennes` : la moyenne de `note_python` par groupe, arrondie à 1 décimale.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"etudiants.csv\")\n\ndf[\"groupe\"] = \nmoyennes = \nmoyennes","solution":"import pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"etudiants.csv\")\n\ndf[\"groupe\"] = np.where(df[\"heures_etude\"] > 10, \"plus de 10 h\", \"10 h ou moins\")\nmoyennes = df.groupby(\"groupe\")[\"note_python\"].mean().round(1)\nmoyennes","check":"import pandas as _pd, numpy as _np\n_d = _pd.read_csv(\"etudiants.csv\")\n_d[\"groupe\"] = _np.where(_d[\"heures_etude\"] > 10, \"plus de 10 h\", \"10 h ou moins\")\n_m = _d.groupby(\"groupe\")[\"note_python\"].mean().round(1)\nassert set(df[\"groupe\"]) == {\"plus de 10 h\", \"10 h ou moins\"}, \"La colonne groupe doit contenir exactement \\\"plus de 10 h\\\" et \\\"10 h ou moins\\\".\"\nassert (df[\"groupe\"] == _d[\"groupe\"]).all(), \"Vérifie la condition : heures_etude > 10.\"\nassert moyennes.to_dict() == _m.to_dict(), f\"Moyennes attendues : {_m.to_dict()}\"","hint":"np.where(df[\"heures_etude\"] > 10, \"plus de 10 h\", \"10 h ou moins\"), puis un groupby(\"groupe\") sur note_python.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"visualisation","title":"Visualiser avec Matplotlib","subtitle":"Un bon graphique vaut mieux qu'un long tableau.","duration":16,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Pourquoi visualiser ?\n\nUn tableau de chiffres demande un effort. Un graphique se comprend en une seconde. **Matplotlib** est la bibliothèque de graphiques de référence en Python."},{"t":"code","code":"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nmois = [\"Jan\", \"Fév\", \"Mar\", \"Avr\", \"Mai\", \"Juin\"]\nventes = [120, 135, 160, 158, 190, 240]\n\nplt.plot(mois, ventes, marker=\"o\")\nplt.title(\"Ventes du premier semestre\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Mois\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Nombre de ventes\")\nplt.show()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"La recette est toujours la même : créer le graphique (`plt.plot`, `plt.bar`…), ajouter un **titre** et des **étiquettes d'axes**, puis `plt.show()`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Choisir le bon graphique\n\n| Question | Graphique | Fonction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Comment ça évolue dans le temps ? | courbe | `plt.plot` |\n| Comment se comparent des catégories ? | barres | `plt.bar` / `plt.barh` |\n| Comment se répartissent les valeurs ? | histogramme | `plt.hist` |\n| Deux variables sont-elles liées ? | nuage de points | `plt.scatter` |"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\npar_ville = ventes.groupby(\"ville\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values()\n\nplt.barh(par_ville.index, par_ville.values, color=\"#2347B8\")\nplt.title(\"Chiffre d'affaires 2024 par ville (euros)\")\nplt.show()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Des barres **horizontales triées** : la ville en tête saute aux yeux, et les noms se lisent sans pencher la tête."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## La répartition : l'histogramme\n\nUn histogramme découpe les valeurs en intervalles et compte combien tombent dans chacun."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"etudiants.csv\")\nplt.hist(df[\"note_python\"], bins=8, color=\"#C92E50\", edgecolor=\"white\")\nplt.title(\"Répartition des notes de Python\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Note sur 20\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Nombre d'étudiants\")\nplt.show()","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## La relation : le nuage de points\n\nLes étudiants qui travaillent plus ont-ils de meilleures notes ? Chaque point est un étudiant."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"etudiants.csv\")\nplt.scatter(df[\"heures_etude\"], df[\"note_python\"], color=\"#2347B8\")\nplt.title(\"Heures d'étude et note de Python\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Heures d'étude par semaine\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Note sur 20\")\nplt.grid(alpha=0.3)\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"Corrélation :\", round(df[\"heures_etude\"].corr(df[\"note_python\"]), 2))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Les points montent vers la droite : plus d'heures, meilleure note. La **corrélation** mesure ce lien entre -1 et 1 ; proche de 1, le lien est fort et positif. Compare avec `note_math` : y a-t-il un lien ?"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"Corrélation n'est pas causalité. Deux choses qui varient ensemble n'ont pas forcément de lien de cause à effet. Ici, c'est plausible, mais il faut toujours rester prudent dans ses conclusions."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Le raccourci pandas\n\nLes Series et DataFrames de pandas ont une méthode `.plot()` qui appelle Matplotlib pour toi :"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(ventes[\"date\"])\nventes[\"mois\"] = ventes[\"date\"].dt.month\nventes.groupby(\"mois\")[\"quantite\"].sum().plot(kind=\"line\", marker=\"o\", title=\"Articles vendus par mois\")\nplt.show()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`kind` peut valoir `\"line\"`, `\"bar\"`, `\"barh\"`, `\"hist\"`, `\"pie\"`…"},{"t":"quiz","q":"Tu veux montrer l'évolution du nombre d'utilisateurs d'une application sur 12 mois. Quel graphique choisis-tu ?","options":["Une courbe","Un histogramme","Un nuage de points"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pour une évolution dans le temps, la courbe est le choix naturel : l'œil suit la tendance de gauche à droite."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercice : ton premier graphique"},{"t":"exercise","id":"visualisation-ex1","title":"Les ventes par catégorie","prompt":"Crée un **graphique en barres** (`plt.bar`) qui montre le **nombre d'articles vendus** (somme de `quantite`) pour chaque `categorie` de `ventes.csv`.\n\nAjoute un titre avec `plt.title(...)`.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\npar_categorie = \n\nplt.show()","solution":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\npar_categorie = ventes.groupby(\"categorie\")[\"quantite\"].sum()\n\nplt.bar(par_categorie.index, par_categorie.values, color=\"#2347B8\")\nplt.title(\"Articles vendus par catégorie\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Quantité\")\nplt.show()","check":"import matplotlib.pyplot as _plt\nimport pandas as _pd\nassert _plt.get_fignums(), \"Aucun graphique n'a été créé. Utilise plt.bar(...).\"\n_ax = _plt.gcf().axes[0]\n_bars = [p for p in _ax.patches]\nassert len(_bars) == 3, f\"Il y a 3 catégories, donc 3 barres attendues. Ton graphique en a {len(_bars)}.\"\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\").groupby(\"categorie\")[\"quantite\"].sum()\nassert sorted(round(b.get_height()) for b in _bars) == sorted(int(x) for x in _v.values), \"La hauteur des barres doit être la somme des quantités par catégorie.\"\nassert _ax.get_title().strip(), \"Ajoute un titre avec plt.title(\\\"...\\\").\"","hint":"par_categorie = ventes.groupby(\"categorie\")[\"quantite\"].sum(), puis plt.bar(par_categorie.index, par_categorie.values)","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"projet-final","title":"Projet final, le rapport du Café Baobab","subtitle":"Tu es l'analyste. La direction attend tes réponses.","duration":25,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Ta mission\n\nLe **Café Baobab** possède cinq cafés : Kinshasa, Abidjan, Dakar, Paris et Montréal. La directrice prépare le budget 2025 et te confie le fichier des ventes 2024, `ventes.csv`. Elle a quatre questions :\n\n1. Quel est le **chiffre d'affaires** total de l'année ?\n2. Quelle **ville** rapporte le plus ?\n3. Y a-t-il des **saisons** plus fortes que d'autres ?\n4. Le **jus de bissap**, nouveau produit, a-t-il un comportement particulier ?\n\nTu vas suivre la démarche de l'analyste vue au chapitre 1 : question, données, nettoyage, analyse, visualisation, décision. Chaque étape est un exercice. Tu peux utiliser l'aide-mémoire (menu du haut) à tout moment."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Étape 1 : préparer les données"},{"t":"exercise","id":"projet-final-ex1","title":"Préparer le tableau","prompt":"Charge `ventes.csv` dans `ventes`, puis ajoute trois colonnes :\n\n- `date` convertie en vraie date avec `pd.to_datetime` ;\n- `mois` : le numéro du mois (1 à 12), extrait de la date ;\n- `total` : le montant de chaque vente (quantité × prix unitaire).\n\nEnfin, calcule `ca_total`, le chiffre d'affaires de l'année, arrondi à l'euro près.","starter":"import pandas as pd\n\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n\nca_total = \nprint(f\"Chiffre d'affaires 2024 : {ca_total} euros\")","solution":"import pandas as pd\n\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(ventes[\"date\"])\nventes[\"mois\"] = ventes[\"date\"].dt.month\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n\nca_total = round(ventes[\"total\"].sum())\nprint(f\"Chiffre d'affaires 2024 : {ca_total} euros\")","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n_v[\"total\"] = _v[\"quantite\"] * _v[\"prix_unitaire\"]\nfor col in (\"mois\", \"total\"):\n    assert col in ventes.columns, f\"Il manque la colonne {col}.\"\nassert str(ventes[\"date\"].dtype).startswith(\"datetime\"), \"La colonne date doit être convertie avec pd.to_datetime.\"\nassert ventes[\"mois\"].min() == 1 and ventes[\"mois\"].max() == 12, \"La colonne mois doit contenir les numéros de mois (1 à 12).\"\nassert ca_total == round(_v[\"total\"].sum()), f\"Le chiffre d'affaires attendu est {round(_v['total'].sum())} euros.\"","hint":"pd.to_datetime(ventes[\"date\"]) ; ventes[\"date\"].dt.month ; ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"] ; round(ventes[\"total\"].sum())","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Étape 2 : les villes"},{"t":"exercise","id":"projet-final-ex2","title":"Le classement des villes","prompt":"Calcule `ca_ville` : le chiffre d'affaires par ville, trié du plus grand au plus petit. Puis calcule `part_top` : la **part** (en %) de la première ville dans le chiffre d'affaires total, arrondie à 1 décimale.\n\nLe code de l'étape 1 est déjà là pour que cet exercice fonctionne tout seul.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n\nca_ville = \npart_top = \nprint(ca_ville.round(0))\nprint(f\"La première ville représente {part_top} % des ventes\")","solution":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n\nca_ville = ventes.groupby(\"ville\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False)\npart_top = round(ca_ville.iloc[0] / ca_ville.sum() * 100, 1)\nprint(ca_ville.round(0))\nprint(f\"La première ville représente {part_top} % des ventes\")","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n_v[\"total\"] = _v[\"quantite\"] * _v[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n_c = _v.groupby(\"ville\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False)\nassert list(ca_ville.index) == list(_c.index), \"ca_ville doit être trié du plus grand au plus petit.\"\nassert part_top == round(_c.iloc[0] / _c.sum() * 100, 1), f\"La part attendue est {round(_c.iloc[0] / _c.sum() * 100, 1)} %.\"","hint":"Un groupby(\"ville\") sur total, trié. La part : ca_ville.iloc[0] / ca_ville.sum() * 100","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Étape 3 : les saisons"},{"t":"exercise","id":"projet-final-ex3","title":"La courbe de l'année","prompt":"Trace la **courbe** du chiffre d'affaires par **mois** (de 1 à 12), avec un titre. Range aussi dans `meilleur_mois` le numéro du mois qui rapporte le plus.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(ventes[\"date\"])\nventes[\"mois\"] = ventes[\"date\"].dt.month\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n\nca_mois = \nmeilleur_mois = \n\nplt.show()\nprint(\"Meilleur mois :\", meilleur_mois)","solution":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(ventes[\"date\"])\nventes[\"mois\"] = ventes[\"date\"].dt.month\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n\nca_mois = ventes.groupby(\"mois\")[\"total\"].sum()\nmeilleur_mois = ca_mois.idxmax()\n\nplt.plot(ca_mois.index, ca_mois.values, marker=\"o\", color=\"#2347B8\")\nplt.title(\"Chiffre d'affaires par mois, 2024\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Mois\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Euros\")\nplt.xticks(range(1, 13))\nplt.show()\nprint(\"Meilleur mois :\", meilleur_mois)","check":"import pandas as _pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as _plt\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n_v[\"mois\"] = _pd.to_datetime(_v[\"date\"]).dt.month\n_v[\"total\"] = _v[\"quantite\"] * _v[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n_m = _v.groupby(\"mois\")[\"total\"].sum()\nassert int(meilleur_mois) == int(_m.idxmax()), f\"Le meilleur mois est le mois {_m.idxmax()}.\"\nassert _plt.get_fignums(), \"Trace la courbe avec plt.plot(...).\"\n_ax = _plt.gcf().axes[0]\nassert _ax.get_lines() and len(_ax.get_lines()[0].get_xdata()) == 12, \"La courbe doit avoir 12 points, un par mois.\"\nassert _ax.get_title().strip(), \"Ajoute un titre à ton graphique.\"","hint":"ca_mois = ventes.groupby(\"mois\")[\"total\"].sum() ; meilleur_mois = ca_mois.idxmax() (l'étiquette de la plus grande valeur) ; plt.plot(ca_mois.index, ca_mois.values)","inputs":""},{"t":"md","v":"Que vois-tu ? Les mois d'été (juin à août) et décembre se détachent. Une hypothèse : vacances et fêtes de fin d'année. Pour la directrice, c'est une information concrète : **prévoir plus de personnel et de stock** à ces périodes."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Étape 4 : le cas du bissap"},{"t":"exercise","id":"projet-final-ex4","title":"Le jus de bissap","prompt":"Le jus de bissap se vend-il différemment selon la saison ?\n\nCalcule `bissap_mois` : la **quantité** de Jus de bissap vendue par mois, puis `ratio` : la quantité moyenne par mois **de mars à septembre** (mois 3 à 9) divisée par la quantité moyenne par mois **des autres mois**, arrondie à 2 décimales.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"mois\"] = pd.to_datetime(ventes[\"date\"]).dt.month\n\nbissap = ventes[ventes[\"produit\"] == \"Jus de bissap\"]\nbissap_mois = \n\nsaison = bissap_mois[(bissap_mois.index >= 3) & (bissap_mois.index <= 9)]\nhors_saison = \nratio = \nprint(bissap_mois)\nprint(\"Ratio saison chaude / reste de l'année :\", ratio)","solution":"import pandas as pd\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"mois\"] = pd.to_datetime(ventes[\"date\"]).dt.month\n\nbissap = ventes[ventes[\"produit\"] == \"Jus de bissap\"]\nbissap_mois = bissap.groupby(\"mois\")[\"quantite\"].sum()\n\nsaison = bissap_mois[(bissap_mois.index >= 3) & (bissap_mois.index <= 9)]\nhors_saison = bissap_mois[(bissap_mois.index < 3) | (bissap_mois.index > 9)]\nratio = round(saison.mean() / hors_saison.mean(), 2)\nprint(bissap_mois)\nprint(\"Ratio saison chaude / reste de l'année :\", ratio)","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\n_v[\"mois\"] = _pd.to_datetime(_v[\"date\"]).dt.month\n_b = _v[_v[\"produit\"] == \"Jus de bissap\"].groupby(\"mois\")[\"quantite\"].sum()\n_s = _b[(_b.index >= 3) & (_b.index <= 9)]\n_h = _b[(_b.index < 3) | (_b.index > 9)]\nassert bissap_mois.to_dict() == _b.to_dict(), \"bissap_mois doit donner la somme des quantités de bissap pour chaque mois.\"\nassert ratio == round(_s.mean() / _h.mean(), 2), f\"Le ratio attendu est {round(_s.mean() / _h.mean(), 2)}.\"","hint":"bissap.groupby(\"mois\")[\"quantite\"].sum(). Pour les autres mois : index < 3 OU index > 9 (avec |). Le ratio : saison.mean() / hors_saison.mean()","inputs":""},{"t":"md","v":"Un ratio bien supérieur à 1 confirme l'intuition : le bissap est une **boisson de saison chaude**. Recommandation possible : le mettre en avant au printemps et en été, et réduire les stocks en hiver pour éviter le gaspillage."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Étape 5 : le rapport\n\nUn analyste ne s'arrête pas aux chiffres : il **raconte** ce qu'ils signifient. Voici un modèle de conclusion que tu pourrais envoyer à la directrice."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"","v":"Madame la directrice,\n1. Kinshasa est notre première ville, loin devant Dakar et Abidjan : un quart du chiffre d'affaires.\n2. Les ventes culminent en été et en décembre : renforçons équipes et stocks à ces périodes.\n3. Le jus de bissap se vend bien plus de mars à septembre : mettons-le en avant pendant la saison chaude."},{"t":"md","v":"Exécute ce dernier bloc : il produit le tableau de bord complet en un seul graphique. Modifie-le, ajoute ce qui te semble utile. C'est ton rapport."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nventes = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\nventes[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(ventes[\"date\"])\nventes[\"mois\"] = ventes[\"date\"].dt.month\nventes[\"total\"] = ventes[\"quantite\"] * ventes[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(11, 4))\nventes.groupby(\"ville\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values().plot(kind=\"barh\", ax=axes[0], color=\"#2347B8\", title=\"CA par ville\")\nventes.groupby(\"mois\")[\"total\"].sum().plot(ax=axes[1], marker=\"o\", color=\"#C92E50\", title=\"CA par mois\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Et maintenant ?\n\nFélicitations. Tu as parcouru tout le chemin : comprendre ce qu'est la programmation, apprendre Python, puis analyser de vraies données jusqu'à des recommandations concrètes.\n\nPour continuer :\n\n- **Installe Python sur ton ordinateur.** Le plus simple pour la data : installer **Anaconda** (qui contient Python, pandas et Jupyter), ou bien Python et l'éditeur **VS Code**.\n- **Travaille dans des notebooks Jupyter.** C'est l'outil standard des analystes, et il fonctionne exactement comme les blocs de code de ce cours.\n- **Analyse des données qui t'intéressent.** Le site Kaggle propose des milliers de jeux de données gratuits : football, musique, santé, climat… Choisis un sujet qui te passionne et pose-lui des questions.\n- **Apprends SQL**, le langage des bases de données, souvent demandé avec Python dans les offres d'emploi d'analyste.\n- **Reviens au bac à sable** de ce site quand tu veux tester une idée."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"","v":"Un dernier conseil : code un peu chaque jour. Dix minutes par jour valent mieux que trois heures une fois par mois.\nTon certificat t'attend. Bravo !"}]]}]}],"pages":{"aide":"## Afficher et commenter\n\n```python\nprint(\"Bonjour\")              # affiche du texte\nprint(\"Total :\", 3 * 4)       # plusieurs éléments séparés par des virgules\n# Ceci est un commentaire : Python l'ignore\n```\n\n## Variables et types\n\n```python\nprenom = \"Awa\"       # str   (texte)\nage = 24             # int   (entier)\ntaille = 1.68        # float (nombre à virgule)\ninscrit = True       # bool  (True ou False)\nprint(type(age))     # <class 'int'>\nage = age + 1        # ou : age += 1\n```\n\nConversions : `int(\"12\")`, `float(\"3.5\")`, `str(12)`. La fonction `input()` renvoie toujours du texte.\n\n## Calculs\n\n```python\n7 + 2, 7 - 2, 7 * 2   # 9, 5, 14\n7 / 2                 # 3.5  division\n7 // 2                # 3    division entière\n7 % 2                 # 1    reste (modulo)\n2 ** 10               # 1024 puissance\nround(3.14159, 2)     # 3.14\n```\n\n## Texte\n\n```python\nnom = \"  aWa diallo \"\nnom.strip().title()       # \"Awa Diallo\"\nnom.upper(), nom.lower()\n\"café\".replace(\"é\", \"e\")\nlen(\"Python\")             # 6\n\"Python\"[0], \"Python\"[-1] # \"P\", \"n\"\n\"Python\"[0:3]             # \"Pyt\"\nf\"{prenom} a {age} ans\"   # f-string\nf\"{3.14159:.2f}\"          # \"3.14\"\n```\n\n## Comparaisons et logique\n\n```python\n==  !=  <  >  <=  >=      # comparaisons\nage >= 18 and inscrit     # et\nage < 12 or age > 65      # ou\nnot inscrit               # non\n```\n\n## Conditions\n\n```python\nif note >= 16:\n    print(\"Très bien\")\nelif note >= 10:\n    print(\"Admis\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Ajourné\")\n```\n\n## Boucles\n\n```python\nfor i in range(1, 6):      # 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\n    print(i)\n\ntotal = 0\nfor x in [3, 8, 2]:\n    total += x\n\nwhile solde < objectif:\n    solde += 1000\n\n# break : sortir de la boucle ; continue : passer au tour suivant\n```\n\n## Listes\n\n```python\nnotes = [12, 15, 9]\nnotes.append(17)          # ajouter\nnotes[0]                  # premier élément\nnotes[-1]                 # dernier\nlen(notes), sum(notes), min(notes), max(notes)\nsorted(notes, reverse=True)\n[n * 2 for n in notes if n >= 10]   # compréhension\nfor rang, n in enumerate(notes, start=1): ...\nfor a, b in zip(liste1, liste2): ...\n```\n\n## Dictionnaires, tuples, ensembles\n\n```python\nproduit = {\"nom\": \"Croissant\", \"prix\": 1.8}\nproduit[\"prix\"]                 # 1.8\nproduit[\"stock\"] = 30           # ajouter / modifier\nproduit.get(\"couleur\", \"?\")     # valeur par défaut\nfor cle, valeur in produit.items(): ...\n\npoint = (4.3, 15.3)             # tuple (non modifiable)\nlat, lon = point                # déballage\nvilles = set([\"Dakar\", \"Dakar\", \"Paris\"])   # {\"Dakar\", \"Paris\"}\n```\n\n## Fonctions\n\n```python\ndef prix_ttc(prix, taux=0.18):\n    \"\"\"Renvoie le prix toutes taxes comprises.\"\"\"\n    return prix * (1 + taux)\n\nprix_ttc(1000)            # 1180.0\nprix_ttc(1000, taux=0.2)  # 1200.0\nsorted(eleves, key=lambda e: e[\"note\"])\n```\n\n## Modules, fichiers, erreurs\n\n```python\nimport math\nfrom datetime import date\nimport random\n\nwith open(\"courses.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    for ligne in f:\n        print(ligne.strip())\n\ntry:\n    n = int(\"abc\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    n = 0\n```\n\n## NumPy\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\na = np.array([2.5, 3.5, 2.0])\na * 2, a.sum(), a.mean(), a.std()\na[a > 2.2]                # filtrer avec un masque\n```\n\n## Pandas : charger et explorer\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"ventes.csv\")\ndf.head(), df.shape, df.columns, df.dtypes\ndf.info()\ndf.describe()\ndf[\"ville\"].value_counts()\ndf[\"ville\"].nunique()\n```\n\n## Pandas : sélectionner et trier\n\n```python\ndf[\"quantite\"]                          # une colonne\ndf[[\"ville\", \"quantite\"]]               # plusieurs colonnes\ndf[df[\"ville\"] == \"Dakar\"]              # filtrer\ndf[(df[\"quantite\"] > 10) & (df[\"ville\"] == \"Paris\")]\ndf[df[\"ville\"].isin([\"Dakar\", \"Abidjan\"])]\ndf.sort_values(\"quantite\", ascending=False)\ndf.loc[df[\"quantite\"] > 10, [\"date\", \"ville\"]]\n```\n\n## Pandas : nettoyer et transformer\n\n```python\ndf.isna().sum()                         # valeurs manquantes\ndf.dropna(subset=[\"depense\"])\ndf[\"age\"].fillna(df[\"age\"].median())\ndf.drop_duplicates()\ndf[\"ville\"].str.strip().str.title()\ndf[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(df[\"date\"])\ndf[\"mois\"] = df[\"date\"].dt.month\ndf[\"total\"] = df[\"quantite\"] * df[\"prix_unitaire\"]\n```\n\n## Pandas : regrouper\n\n```python\ndf.groupby(\"ville\")[\"total\"].sum()\ndf.groupby(\"ville\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False)\ndf.groupby(\"ville\").agg(nb=(\"total\", \"count\"), ca=(\"total\", \"sum\"))\npd.pivot_table(df, values=\"total\", index=\"ville\", columns=\"categorie\", aggfunc=\"sum\")\n```\n\n## Graphiques\n\n```python\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nplt.plot(x, y, marker=\"o\")       # courbe (évolution)\nplt.bar(categories, valeurs)     # barres (comparaison)\nplt.barh(categories, valeurs)    # barres horizontales\nplt.hist(valeurs, bins=10)       # histogramme (répartition)\nplt.scatter(x, y)                # nuage de points (relation)\nplt.title(\"Titre\"); plt.xlabel(\"x\"); plt.ylabel(\"y\")\nplt.show()\n\nserie.plot(kind=\"bar\", title=\"Titre\")   # raccourci pandas\n```\n"}},"en":{"modules":[{"id":"comprendre","icon":"🧠","title":"Understanding programming","desc":"What a computer really does, what an algorithm is and how a programmer thinks. Almost no code at first.","badge":"Programmer's mindset","lessons":[{"id":"ordinateur","title":"What is a computer, really?","subtitle":"The fastest machine in the world… and the most obedient.","duration":8,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## You use computers all day long\n\nYour phone, the supermarket checkout, the cash machine, your internet box, your neighbour's new car… They are all **computers**.\n\nThey look smart: they recognize your face, translate sentences, suggest exactly the video you feel like watching. So, does a computer think?"},{"t":"quiz","q":"In your opinion, is a computer intelligent?","options":["Yes, it understands what we ask it","No, it carries out instructions without understanding them","Only recent computers"],"answer":1,"explain":"A computer understands nothing. It **carries out instructions**, one by one, at an insane speed: billions per second. All the \"intelligence\" comes from the people who wrote those instructions: **programmers**."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Fast, but terribly literal\n\nPicture a cook who can chop a thousand onions per second, but has **no imagination at all**: they do exactly what they are told, nothing more, nothing less.\n\nIf you tell them \"make an omelette\", they don't move. They don't know what an omelette is. You have to say:\n\n1. Take 3 eggs from the fridge.\n2. Crack each egg over the bowl.\n3. Throw the shells in the bin.\n4. Beat the eggs for 30 seconds.\n5. …\n\nAnd if you forget step 3? There will be shells in the omelette. They will never think \"hmm, that's weird\"."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"","v":"A computer does what you say, not what you mean.\nThat's THE sentence to remember from this lesson."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Input, processing, output\n\nEvery program, from the simplest to the most impressive, follows the same pattern:"},{"t":"html","v":"<div class=\"flow\">\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Input</b><span>What we give the computer: a click, typed text, a photo, a file.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Processing</b><span>The program's instructions transform that data.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Output</b><span>The result: a display, a sound, a message sent, a file.</span></div>\n</div>"},{"t":"md","v":"Take a calculator: you type `12 × 4` (input), it calculates (processing), it displays `48` (output)."},{"t":"quiz","q":"You send a voice message on WhatsApp. What is the **input**?","options":["Your voice picked up by the microphone","The message arriving on your friend's phone","The internet network"],"answer":0,"explain":"The input is what the program receives: your voice recorded by the microphone. Processing compresses and sends it. The output is the sound played on your friend's phone."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## A program is a list of instructions\n\nA **program** is a sequence of instructions written for a computer. **Programming** means writing those instructions, in the right order, without forgetting anything.\n\nTry it right now. This little robot wants its cup of coffee ☕. It only knows three instructions: move forward one square, turn left, turn right. Build its program, then launch it."},{"t":"widget","name":"robot","config":{"levels":[{"grid":["S..G"],"dir":"E","hint":"The robot is facing right (the red arrow). How many squares must it move?"},{"grid":["S..","#.#","..G"],"dir":"E","hint":"Turning doesn't move the robot: it spins on the spot."},{"grid":["S.#G","#.#.","#..."],"dir":"E","hint":"Take your time: follow the path with your finger and write down each instruction."}]}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## What you just experienced\n\nIf the robot crashed into a rock, that's normal, and valuable. You just discovered three truths about programming:\n\n- **Order matters.** \"Move then turn\" is not \"turn then move\".\n- **Nothing is guessed.** The robot never \"understood\" where the coffee was. It followed your instructions.\n- **You make mistakes, you fix them, you try again.** Professional programmers spend a big part of their time doing exactly that. It's called **debugging**."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Your program makes the robot run into a rock. What do you do?","options":["I start everything again from scratch","I look for the instruction that causes the problem and fix it","I switch to another robot"],"answer":1,"explain":"You don't throw everything away: you find the mistake (the \"bug\"), fix it, and run again. That's every programmer's normal cycle."}]]},{"id":"algorithmes","title":"Algorithms, recipes for machines","subtitle":"Before writing code, you write a method.","duration":10,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## A fancy word for a simple idea\n\nAn **algorithm** is a sequence of precise steps to solve a problem. You use them every day without knowing it:\n\n- a **cooking recipe** is an algorithm to make a dish;\n- **directions** (\"straight on, then left at the roundabout\") are an algorithm to get somewhere;\n- the **long division method** you learned at school is a calculation algorithm.\n\nA program is an algorithm translated into a language the computer understands."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Put the recipe back in order\n\nHere is the algorithm to make a cup of tea. The steps have been shuffled. Put them back in the right order (with the arrows, or by dragging them)."},{"t":"widget","name":"order","config":{"prompt":"Making a cup of tea","items":["Fill the kettle with water","Switch the kettle on and wait for the water to boil","Pour the boiling water into the cup","Dip the tea bag into the hot water","Wait 3 minutes","Take the tea bag out of the cup"]}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Precision above all\n\nA human understands \"add a pinch of salt\". A computer doesn't: how many grams? Which salt? Added where?\n\nThere's an old programmer joke:\n\n> Someone tells their programmer friend: \"Go buy a loaf of bread. If they have eggs, get six.\"\n> The programmer comes back with six loaves of bread."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Why does the programmer come back with six loaves?","options":["They were very hungry","The instruction was ambiguous: \"get six\" could refer to the loaves","There were no eggs"],"answer":1,"explain":"They had eggs, so the programmer applied \"get six\"… to the bread, the only item mentioned. A computer would have done the same. An instruction must have **only one possible meaning**."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Deciding and repeating\n\nLook closely at recipes: they often contain two magic words.\n\n- **If**: \"*If* you like sweet tea, add a sugar.\" This is a **condition**: the action is done only in certain cases.\n- **While** / **Repeat**: \"*While* the water isn't boiling, wait.\" This is a **loop**: an action is repeated.\n\nHere is a famous algorithm: finding the largest number in a list, for example the grades of a class. Put the steps in order."},{"t":"widget","name":"order","config":{"prompt":"Finding the best grade in the class","items":["Read the first grade and remember it as the \"best grade so far\"","Move on to the next grade","**If** it is higher than the remembered grade, remember it instead","**Repeat** the two previous steps until the last grade","Announce the remembered grade: it's the best one"],"success":"Exactly. You just wrote a real algorithm with a condition and a loop. In a few chapters, you'll write it in Python."}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## The robot learns to repeat\n\nWriting \"Move forward\" six times in a row is tedious. Programmers hate repeating themselves! In this new challenge, you can click the small **×1** button on an instruction to repeat it 2, 3, 4 or 5 times.\n\nCareful: each level sets a maximum number of blocks."},{"t":"widget","name":"robot","config":{"loops":true,"levels":[{"grid":["S.....G"],"dir":"E","maxBlocks":1,"hint":"A single instruction, repeated."},{"grid":["S....",".#.#.","....G"],"dir":"E","maxBlocks":3},{"grid":["S..#","##.#","G..#"],"dir":"E","maxBlocks":5,"hint":"The robot has to turn back along the bottom."}]}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Key takeaways\n\n- An **algorithm** is a method made of precise, unambiguous steps.\n- The **order** of the steps matters.\n- Two tools come up everywhere: the **condition** (if…) and the **loop** (repeat…)."},{"t":"quiz","q":"\"While there are customers in the queue, serve the next customer.\" What is this?","options":["A condition","A loop","A mistake"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"While\" repeats an action several times: it's a **loop**. It stops when the queue is empty."}]]},{"id":"langages","title":"How do we talk to a computer?","subtitle":"From 0s and 1s all the way to Python.","duration":10,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## It's all electricity\n\nInside a computer there are billions of tiny switches. Each one has only two states: **off** or **on**. We write them **0** and **1**.\n\nA single 0 or 1 is called a **bit**. A group of 8 bits is called a **byte**. You already know that word: a 3 MB (megabyte) file contains about 3 million bytes.\n\nWith 8 switches, you can write every number from 0 to 255. Each switch has a value: 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1. You add up the values of the switches that are on."},{"t":"widget","name":"binary","config":{"target":5,"showChar":false,"success":"Well done: 00000101 is 4 + 1 = 5. You can read binary!"}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Letters are numbers too\n\nIf the computer only knows numbers, how does it display text? Thanks to a convention: each letter matches a number. For example, **A** is 65, **B** is 66, **a** is 97.\n\nSwitch on the right switches to write the letter **A** (65)."},{"t":"widget","name":"binary","config":{"target":65,"success":"That's it: 01000001 = 64 + 1 = 65 = \"A\". When you type a message, your phone actually stores numbers like this one."}},{"t":"md","v":"It's the same for everything else: a photo is a grid of coloured dots (**pixels**), and each colour is a number. A song is a sequence of numbers that describe the sound. **To a computer, everything is a number.**"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Nobody writes in binary\n\nWriting programs with 0s and 1s would be a nightmare. So people invented **programming languages**: languages closer to human language, which are then automatically translated into binary.\n\nHere is the same program, \"display Hello\", in four languages. Compare them."},{"t":"widget","name":"tabs","config":{"tabs":[{"label":"Assembly","code":"section .data\n    msg db \"Hello\", 10\nsection .text\n    global _start\n_start:\n    mov rax, 1\n    mov rdi, 1\n    mov rsi, msg\n    mov rdx, 6\n    syscall\n    mov rax, 60\n    xor rdi, rdi\n    syscall","note":"Very close to the machine: you directly handle the processor's memory slots. Powerful, but hard to read."},{"label":"C","code":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"Hello\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","note":"A language from the 1970s, still used for operating systems and connected devices."},{"label":"JavaScript","code":"console.log(\"Hello\");","note":"The language of web pages: it runs in every browser."},{"label":"Python","code":"print(\"Hello\")","note":"One single line, almost plain English. That's why Python is so popular with beginners… and with data scientists."}]}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## High-level and low-level languages\n\nLanguages are classified by how far they are from the machine:\n\n| | Low level | High level |\n|---|---|---|\n| Examples | Assembly, C | Python, JavaScript |\n| Close to | the machine | humans |\n| Easy to read | no | yes |\n| Fine control of the hardware | yes | less |\n\nNone is \"better\": you choose the tool for the job. For analyzing data, Python is the number one choice in the world."},{"t":"quiz","q":"What is the main advantage of a high-level language like Python?","options":["It is closer to 0s and 1s","It is easier for a human to read and write","It doesn't need to be translated"],"answer":1,"explain":"A high-level language is made for humans. It still has to be translated for the machine, but that job is done automatically."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## The translator: the Python interpreter\n\nWho translates your Python code into machine language? A program called the **Python interpreter**.\n\nThink of an **interpreter** at an international conference: they listen to a sentence and translate it immediately, sentence after sentence. The Python interpreter does the same: it reads your code **line by line, from top to bottom**, and runs each line.\n\nAn important consequence: if line 5 contains an error, lines 1 to 4 have already run, and Python stops at line 5."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Your program has 10 lines. Line 7 contains an error. What happens?","options":["Nothing runs","Lines 1 to 6 run, then Python stops and reports the error on line 7","Python skips line 7 and carries on"],"answer":1,"explain":"Python reads and runs from top to bottom. It stops at the first error and tells you its line number. Very handy to find it."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"","v":"Good news: on this site, the Python interpreter runs right inside your web page. Nothing to install."}]]},{"id":"super-pouvoirs","title":"The programmer's five superpowers","subtitle":"Every program in the world is built from these bricks.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## The best-kept secret\n\nA banking app, a video game, an airline's website… They look very different. Yet they are all built from the same five basic bricks:\n\n1. **sequence**: doing things in order;\n2. **variables**: remembering things;\n3. **conditions**: making decisions;\n4. **loops**: repeating;\n5. **functions**: giving a name to a skill so you can reuse it.\n\nYou already know the first three without realizing it. Let's discover them with real Python code. No need to understand everything: just look at the idea."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Sequence and variables\n\nA **variable** is a box with a label. You store a value in it to remember it later. Go step by step and watch the computer's memory."},{"t":"widget","name":"stepper","config":{"code":"price = 500\nquantity = 3\ntotal = price * quantity\nprint(total)","steps":[{"line":1,"vars":{},"note":"Python reads line 1: it is going to create a box called price."},{"line":2,"vars":{"price":"500"},"note":"The price box holds 500. Line 2: a second box."},{"line":3,"vars":{"price":"500","quantity":"3"},"note":"Line 3: Python reads what is in both boxes and multiplies them (* means \"times\")."},{"line":4,"vars":{"price":"500","quantity":"3","total":"1500"},"note":"The result is stored in a new box, total. Line 4: we display it."},{"line":4,"vars":{"price":"500","quantity":"3","total":"1500"},"out":"1500","note":"print displays what total contains. End of the program!"}]}},{"t":"md","v":"Notice the **sequence**: Python runs the lines one by one, from top to bottom. Line 3 needs `price` and `quantity`: it has to come after them."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Conditions\n\nA **condition** lets the program choose what to do depending on the situation. In Python, we write `if`, `elif` (\"else if\") and `else`.\n\nMove the slider and see which part of the code runs."},{"t":"widget","name":"condition","config":{"name":"temperature","label":"Temperature outside:","min":-5,"max":42,"value":22,"branches":[{"code":"if temperature < 15:","lt":15,"out":"It's chilly, take a jacket!"},{"code":"elif temperature < 30:","lt":30,"out":"It's nice, enjoy!"},{"code":"else:","out":"It's very hot, drink lots of water!"}]}},{"t":"md","v":"The program runs **only one** of the three branches: the first one whose condition is true."},{"t":"quiz","q":"With this program, what is displayed if `temperature` is exactly 30?","options":["It's chilly, take a jacket!","It's nice, enjoy!","It's very hot, drink lots of water!"],"answer":2,"explain":"30 is not less than 15, nor less than 30 (30 < 30 is false). So we end up in the `else`. \"Right on the boundary\" cases are classic traps!"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Loops\n\nA **loop** repeats instructions. Here, `for` repeats the indented line once for each number from 1 to 3. Watch the `lap` variable change on each pass."},{"t":"widget","name":"stepper","config":{"code":"for lap in range(1, 4):\n    print(\"Lap number\", lap)\nprint(\"Done!\")","steps":[{"line":1,"vars":{},"note":"range(1, 4) produces the numbers 1, 2, 3 (4 is excluded)."},{"line":2,"vars":{"lap":"1"},"note":"First pass: lap is 1."},{"line":1,"vars":{"lap":"1"},"out":"Lap number 1","note":"Back to the top of the loop for the next number."},{"line":2,"vars":{"lap":"2"},"out":"Lap number 1","note":"Second pass: lap is 2."},{"line":1,"vars":{"lap":"2"},"out":"Lap number 1\nLap number 2"},{"line":2,"vars":{"lap":"3"},"out":"Lap number 1\nLap number 2","note":"Third and last pass."},{"line":3,"vars":{"lap":"3"},"out":"Lap number 1\nLap number 2\nLap number 3","note":"No more numbers: we leave the loop and carry on after it."},{"line":3,"vars":{"lap":"3"},"out":"Lap number 1\nLap number 2\nLap number 3\nDone!","note":"To repeat 1000 times, you'd only change a single number."}]}}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Functions\n\nA **function** is a skill you give a name to. Think of a juicer: you put fruit in (the **input**), it does its job, and it gives you juice (the **output**). You don't need to know how it works inside.\n\nYou've already seen a function: `print`. Someone wrote it once and for all, and millions of programmers use it every day."},{"t":"code","code":"def price_with_tax(price):\n    return price * 1.18\n\nprint(price_with_tax(1000))\nprint(price_with_tax(2500))","run":false},{"t":"md","v":"Here, we create (with `def`) a function that adds 18% tax to a price. We can then use it as many times as we like."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Spot the superpowers"},{"t":"quiz","q":"An app sends a \"Drink some water\" reminder **every hour**. Which superpower is at work?","options":["A variable","A condition","A loop"],"answer":2,"explain":"Repeating the same action at regular intervals is a **loop**."},{"t":"quiz","q":"\"**If** the account balance is lower than the amount, refuse the payment.\"","options":["A condition","A loop","A function"],"answer":0,"explain":"The program makes a decision depending on the situation: it's a **condition**."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Your delivery app remembers the address you typed so it can show it on the map. It uses…","options":["a loop","a variable","a function"],"answer":1,"explain":"Keeping a value in memory to use it later is the job of a **variable**."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## The real skill: breaking things down\n\nFaced with a big problem, a programmer doesn't rush in head first. They **break it down** into small, simple problems. For example, \"build a food delivery app\" becomes:\n\n- show the list of restaurants (a loop over the restaurants);\n- remember the customer's basket (variables);\n- check that the payment is accepted (a condition);\n- calculate the total price (a function);\n- …\n\nEach small piece becomes easy. This way of thinking is called **computational thinking**, and it will help you even outside programming."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"","v":"Sequence, variables, conditions, loops, functions.\nYou now have the map of the territory. The rest of the course is the journey."}]]},{"id":"python-data","title":"Python, data and your first code","subtitle":"Why this language, why data, and off we go.","duration":10,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Why Python?\n\nThere are hundreds of languages. Python is one of the most used in the world, for good reasons:\n\n- **It reads almost like English.** Less time on syntax, more time on the problem.\n- **It's used everywhere**: websites (Instagram), scientific research (NASA), banks, artificial intelligence, automating office tasks…\n- **It's the king of data analysis.** The pandas, NumPy and Matplotlib libraries, which you'll use at the end of the course, are world standards.\n- **The community is huge.** Whatever your question, someone has already asked it and someone has answered it."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## What is data?\n\n**Data** is recorded information: a shop's sales, mobile money transactions, a class's grades, each day's weather, bus journeys…\n\nA **data analyst** turns that raw data into useful answers. Their work almost always follows the same path:"},{"t":"html","v":"<div class=\"flow\">\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Question</b><span>Which product sells best on Saturdays?</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Collection</b><span>Get the sales file.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Cleaning</b><span>Fix errors and gaps.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Analysis</b><span>Calculate, compare, group.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Visualization</b><span>A clear chart.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Decision</b><span>Order more of that product.</span></div>\n</div>"},{"t":"quiz","q":"A pharmacy wants to know which medicines to order before the rainy season. What is the first step?","options":["Ask a precise question","Make a chart","Delete the old data"],"answer":0,"explain":"Everything starts with a clear **question**, for example: \"Which medicines sold the most during previous rainy seasons?\" Without a question, you get lost in the data."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Your journey\n\nHere's the path you'll follow:\n\n1. **First steps**: display, comment, understand errors, use variables.\n2. **Types**: numbers, text, true and false.\n3. **Deciding and repeating**: conditions and loops.\n4. **Organizing your data**: lists and dictionaries.\n5. **Functions, modules and files**: reusable code.\n6. **Analyzing data**: pandas and charts, all the way to the final project.\n\nEach lesson takes about ten minutes. One a day is enough to make fast progress."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Your very first Python program\n\nHere is a line of real Python. Press **Run** to launch it. The first time, Python takes a few seconds to wake up."},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"Hello! I am learning Python.\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`print` means \"display\". The text between quotes is displayed as is.\n\nNow **edit the text** between the quotes (write whatever you like) and run it again. You can break the code, it doesn't matter: the **Reset** button brings back the original."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Your first exercise\n\nExercises are checked automatically, as if by a teacher with a red pen, but instantly."},{"t":"exercise","id":"python-data-ex1","title":"Introduce yourself","prompt":"Write a program that displays a sentence containing **your first name**, for example `My name is Awa`.\n\nDon't forget the quotes around the text, and the parentheses of `print`.","starter":"# Write your line below this comment","solution":"print(\"My name is Awa\")","check":"assert \"print\" in CODE, \"Use the print() function to display your text.\"\nassert OUT.strip() != \"\", \"Your program doesn't display anything yet. Write a line with print(\\\"...\\\").\"\nassert len(OUT.strip()) >= 5, \"Write a real sentence with your first name.\"","hint":"Copy the shape of the previous example: print(\"your text here\")","inputs":""},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"","v":"That's it, you are a programmer.\nNot an expert yet, but you've written and run code. In the next chapter, things get serious."}]]}]},{"id":"premiers-pas","icon":"🐍","title":"First steps in Python","desc":"Display, comment, read errors and store values in variables.","badge":"Python beginner","lessons":[{"id":"print","title":"Displaying with print()","subtitle":"Every programmer's first instruction.","duration":10,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Make your program talk\n\n`print()` displays something on the screen. It's the simplest way to see what your program is doing. You'll use it all the time, even as an expert, to check your calculations."},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"Welcome to chapter 2!\")\nprint(\"Python reads lines from top to bottom.\")\nprint(\"One line, one print, one line displayed.\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Three `print`s, three lines displayed, in order. Try swapping two lines, then run."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Text between quotes\n\nThe text we display must be between **quotes**. In programming, a piece of text is called a **string**.\n\nYou can use double quotes `\"` or single quotes `'`. Double quotes are handier when your text contains an apostrophe."},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"It's easy with double quotes\")\nprint('Single quotes work too')","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"If you write `print('It's broken')`, Python thinks the text ends after `It`. Result: an error. When your text contains apostrophes, use double quotes."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Numbers, without quotes\n\nNumbers are written **without** quotes. And here comes the magic: Python can calculate."},{"t":"code","code":"print(12 + 30)\nprint(\"12 + 30\")","run":true},{"t":"quiz","code":"print(7 * 6)","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["7 * 6","42","\"42\""],"answer":1,"explain":"Without quotes, Python calculates `7 * 6` (the `*` sign means \"times\") and displays the result: 42."},{"t":"quiz","code":"print(\"7 * 6\")","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["7 * 6","42","An error"],"answer":0,"explain":"With quotes, it's text: Python displays it as is, without calculating anything."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Several things at once\n\nYou can give `print` several items, separated by **commas**. Python displays them on the same line, separated by a space."},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"I am\", 25, \"years old\")\nprint(\"A week has\", 7 * 24, \"hours\")\nprint()\nprint(\"The empty print() above displayed a blank line.\")","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"","v":"The comma lets you mix text and numbers in a single `print`. Python adds the spaces for you."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: a receipt"},{"t":"exercise","id":"print-ex1","title":"Your first receipt","prompt":"Write a program that displays **three lines**:\n\n1. `Baobab Café`\n2. `2 coffees: 5 euros`\n3. `Thank you, see you soon!`\n\nMatch the text exactly (capital letters and punctuation included).","starter":"# One line per print","solution":"print(\"Baobab Café\")\nprint(\"2 coffees: 5 euros\")\nprint(\"Thank you, see you soon!\")","check":"lines = [l.strip() for l in OUT.strip().split(\"\\n\")]\nassert len(lines) == 3, f\"You need exactly 3 lines displayed, your program displays {len(lines)}.\"\nassert lines[0] == \"Baobab Café\", f\"The first line must be \\\"Baobab Café\\\", not \\\"{lines[0]}\\\".\"\nassert lines[1] == \"2 coffees: 5 euros\", f\"The second line must be \\\"2 coffees: 5 euros\\\", not \\\"{lines[1]}\\\".\"\nassert lines[2] == \"Thank you, see you soon!\", f\"The third line must be \\\"Thank you, see you soon!\\\", not \\\"{lines[2]}\\\".\"","hint":"You need three print instructions, one per line, with each text between quotes.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: let Python calculate"},{"t":"exercise","id":"print-ex2","title":"How many minutes?","prompt":"A day has 24 hours, and an hour has 60 minutes. Display the sentence:\n\n`A day has 1440 minutes`\n\nBut **don't calculate it yourself**: write `24 * 60` in your `print` and let Python do the maths.","starter":"print(\"A day has\", ...)","solution":"print(\"A day has\", 24 * 60, \"minutes\")","check":"assert \"24 * 60\" in CODE.replace(\"24*60\", \"24 * 60\") or \"60 * 24\" in CODE.replace(\"60*24\", \"60 * 24\"), \"Write the calculation 24 * 60 in your code instead of the result.\"\nassert OUT.strip() == \"A day has 1440 minutes\", f\"The program must display \\\"A day has 1440 minutes\\\". It displays: \\\"{OUT.strip()}\\\"\"","hint":"Replace the ... with the calculation, then add a comma and the text \"minutes\".","inputs":""},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"","v":"Well done. You can now display text, numbers and calculations. That's already half of what a program does: show results."}]]},{"id":"erreurs","title":"Comments and errors","subtitle":"Writing notes, and no longer fearing red messages.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Comments\n\nEverything after a `#` on a line is a **comment**: Python ignores it completely. It's a note for humans, including you in three months' time."},{"t":"code","code":"# This program calculates the price of an order\nunit_price = 3      # in euros\nprint(unit_price * 4)   # 4 cappuccinos\n# print(\"This line doesn't run, it is commented out\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Commenting out a line (putting a `#` in front of it) is also a handy way to **disable** code temporarily, without deleting it."},{"t":"quiz","q":"What does Python do with the line `# print(\"Hello\")`?","options":["It displays Hello","Nothing at all: it's a comment","It displays an error"],"answer":1,"explain":"The `#` turns the whole line into a comment. Python doesn't run it."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Errors are your friends\n\nYou will make errors. Lots of them. Everyone does, every day, including Google's developers. The difference between a beginner and a pro: the pro **reads the error message**.\n\nRun this code. It contains a deliberate error."},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"Hello)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Look at the message. It tells you three things:\n\n1. **The error type**: `SyntaxError`, a \"grammar\" mistake.\n2. **The line** where Python got stuck.\n3. **An explanation**: here, a string that is never closed.\n\nFix the code (add the missing quote) and run it again."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## The three most common errors\n\n**1. NameError: an unknown name.** Python doesn't know this word. Often a typo, or an extra capital letter: to Python, `print` and `Print` are two different words."},{"t":"code","code":"Print(\"Python tells upper and lower case apart\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"**2. Forgetting the quotes.** Without quotes, Python thinks `Hello` is the name of something."},{"t":"code","code":"print(Hello)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"**3. IndentationError: an extra shift.** In Python, spaces at the start of a line have a meaning. A line must not be shifted for no reason."},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"Line 1\")\n  print(\"Line 2\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Fix these three examples one by one."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Guess the output"},{"t":"quiz","code":"print(\"A\")\nprint(\"B\")\nprnt(\"C\")\nprint(\"D\")","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["Nothing is displayed","A and B are displayed, then a NameError","A, B and D are displayed"],"answer":1,"explain":"Python runs from top to bottom. Lines 1 and 2 work. On line 3, `prnt` is unknown: error, and the program stops. Line 4 never runs."},{"t":"quiz","q":"To Python, `Print(\"x\")` and `print(\"x\")` are…","options":["the same thing","not the same: Python tells upper and lower case apart","both errors"],"answer":1,"explain":"Python is **case sensitive**: `Print`, `PRINT` and `print` are three different names. Only `print` exists."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## The detective method\n\nWhen you hit an error, always follow these steps:\n\n1. **Read the error type** and the explanation.\n2. **Go to the line shown** (and also look at the line just before: the error often comes from there).\n3. **Compare** with an example that works.\n4. **Change one thing** at a time, then run again."},{"t":"exercise","id":"erreurs-ex1","title":"The broken code","prompt":"This program contains **three errors**. Fix them so that it displays:\n\n    I debug\n    like a pro\n    3 errors fixed\n\nRun it first to read the error messages, one by one.","starter":"print(\"I debug)\nPrint(\"like a pro\")\n print(3, \"errors fixed\")","solution":"print(\"I debug\")\nprint(\"like a pro\")\nprint(3, \"errors fixed\")","check":"expected = [\"I debug\", \"like a pro\", \"3 errors fixed\"]\nlines = [l.strip() for l in OUT.strip().split(\"\\n\")]\nassert lines == expected, \"The output isn't exactly the one asked for yet. Compare line by line.\"","hint":"A missing quote, an extra capital letter, a space at the start of a line.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"variables","title":"Variables","subtitle":"Labelled boxes to store your data.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## A box with a label\n\nA **variable** is a name given to a value so you can remember and reuse it. Picture a box: the label is the **name**, the content is the **value**."},{"t":"code","code":"first_name = \"Awa\"\nage = 24\nprint(first_name)\nprint(age)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"The `=` sign doesn't mean \"equals\" as in maths. It means **\"store the value on the right in the box on the left\"**. We say we **assign** a value to a variable."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Using a variable\n\nOnce created, the variable is used in place of its value. No quotes around the name: otherwise it's text!"},{"t":"code","code":"city = \"Abidjan\"\nprint(\"I live in\", city)\nprint(\"city\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Why bother? If the city changes, you edit **a single line** and the whole program follows. Replace `\"Abidjan\"` with your city and run."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## A variable can change\n\nA variable can receive a new value: the old one is replaced. Watch the memory step by step."},{"t":"widget","name":"stepper","config":{"code":"score = 0\nscore = 10\nscore = score + 5\nprint(score)","steps":[{"line":1,"vars":{},"note":"We create the score box."},{"line":2,"vars":{"score":"0"},"note":"score is 0. We're going to replace it with 10."},{"line":3,"vars":{"score":"10"},"note":"Python first works out the right side: score + 5, so 10 + 5 = 15…"},{"line":4,"vars":{"score":"15"},"note":"…then stores 15 in score. The old value is gone."},{"line":4,"vars":{"score":"15"},"out":"15","note":"score = score + 5 is a very common line: \"increase score by 5\"."}]}},{"t":"quiz","code":"coins = 3\ncoins = coins * 2\ncoins = coins - 1\nprint(coins)","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["3","6","5"],"answer":2,"explain":"3, then 3 × 2 = 6, then 6 − 1 = 5. Each line starts from the previous value."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Calculating with variables\n\nVariables make calculations readable. Compare `print(4 * 3.5)` with this:"},{"t":"code","code":"number_of_coffees = 4\ncoffee_price = 3.5\ntotal = number_of_coffees * coffee_price\nprint(\"Total to pay:\", total)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"The code almost reads like a sentence. That's what a good program is: **understandable by a human**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"info","title":"","v":"In Python, decimal numbers are written with a **dot**: `3.5`, never `3,5`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Naming variables well\n\nPython has a few rules:\n\n- only letters, digits and the underscore `_`;\n- no spaces, no dash `-` (Python would think it's a subtraction);\n- don't start with a digit;\n- upper and lower case matter: `age` and `Age` are two different variables.\n\nAnd one convention: names are written in lower case, with `_` between words. This is **snake_case**. Examples: `total_price`, `number_of_students`."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Which of these variable names is **valid** in Python?","options":["`total price`","`2nd_price`","`total_price`","`total-price`"],"answer":2,"explain":"`total price` contains a space, `2nd_price` starts with a digit, `total-price` contains a dash. Only `total_price` follows the rules."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"","v":"Choose names that say what's in the box. `x` and `stuff` mean nothing; `average_grade` explains itself."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: your ID card"},{"t":"exercise","id":"variables-ex1","title":"Your ID card","prompt":"Create three variables:\n\n- `first_name` holding your first name (text);\n- `city` holding your city (text);\n- `age` holding your age (a number, **without quotes**).\n\nThen display a sentence that uses them, for example: `Awa lives in Dakar and is 24 years old`.","starter":"first_name = \ncity = \nage = \n\nprint(first_name, \"lives in\", ...)","solution":"first_name = \"Awa\"\ncity = \"Dakar\"\nage = 24\n\nprint(first_name, \"lives in\", city, \"and is\", age, \"years old\")","check":"for name in (\"first_name\", \"city\", \"age\"):\n    assert name in dir(), f\"The variable \\\"{name}\\\" doesn't exist. Create it with {name} = ...\"\nassert isinstance(first_name, str) and first_name.strip(), \"first_name must hold text between quotes.\"\nassert isinstance(city, str) and city.strip(), \"city must hold text between quotes.\"\nassert isinstance(age, int), \"age must be a whole number, written without quotes (for example age = 24).\"\nassert first_name in OUT and city in OUT and str(age) in OUT, \"The sentence displayed must contain your first name, your city and your age (use the variables in the print).\"\nassert \"...\" not in CODE, \"There are still ... in your code: replace them.\"","hint":"For text, use quotes: first_name = \"Awa\". For the age, no quotes: age = 24. In the print, separate each piece with a comma.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the change"},{"t":"exercise","id":"variables-ex2","title":"Giving change","prompt":"A customer buys 3 croissants at 1.8 euros each and pays with a 10-euro note.\n\n1. Create a variable `total` that calculates the price of the 3 croissants (with a calculation, not by writing the result yourself).\n2. Create a variable `change` that calculates what we give back.\n3. Display `change`.","starter":"croissant_price = 1.8\nquantity = 3\nnote = 10\n\ntotal = \nchange = \nprint(change)","solution":"croissant_price = 1.8\nquantity = 3\nnote = 10\n\ntotal = croissant_price * quantity\nchange = note - total\nprint(change)","check":"assert \"total\" in dir() and \"change\" in dir(), \"You need to create the variables total and change.\"\nassert abs(total - 5.4) < 1e-9, f\"total should be 3 × 1.8 = 5.4, it is {total}.\"\nassert abs(change - 4.6) < 1e-9, f\"change should be 10 − 5.4 = 4.6, it is {change}.\"\nassert \"croissant_price\" in CODE.split(\"total =\")[1] if \"total =\" in CODE else True, \"Calculate total from the variables croissant_price and quantity.\"","hint":"total = croissant_price * quantity. Then the change is the note minus the total.","inputs":""},{"t":"callout","kind":"info","title":"","v":"You may have seen `4.6` or a strange number like `4.6000000000000005`. It's not a bug in your code: computers store decimal numbers approximately. You'll see how to round in the next chapter."}]]}]},{"id":"types","icon":"🔢","title":"Numbers, text and booleans","desc":"The basic data types: calculate, work with text, say true or false.","badge":"Master of types","lessons":[{"id":"nombres","title":"Numbers and calculations","subtitle":"Python, the best calculator in the world.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Two kinds of numbers\n\nPython has two types of numbers:\n\n- **integers** (`int`): `7`, `-3`, `2026`;\n- **decimal numbers** (`float`, for *floating-point number*): `3.5`, `-0.25`, `20.0`.\n\nThe `type()` function tells you what type a value is."},{"t":"code","code":"print(type(42))\nprint(type(3.14))\nprint(type(\"42\"))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Watch the last one: `\"42\"` between quotes is **text** (`str`), not a number."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Operations\n\n| Symbol | Operation | Example | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `+` | addition | `7 + 2` | `9` |\n| `-` | subtraction | `7 - 2` | `5` |\n| `*` | multiplication | `7 * 2` | `14` |\n| `/` | division | `7 / 2` | `3.5` |\n| `//` | integer division | `7 // 2` | `3` |\n| `%` | remainder (modulo) | `7 % 2` | `1` |\n| `**` | power | `7 ** 2` | `49` |"},{"t":"code","code":"print(7 / 2)\nprint(7 // 2)\nprint(7 % 2)\nprint(2 ** 10)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`//` and `%` go together: 7 divided by 2 gives 3, **remainder** 1. Just like in primary school!"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## What's the remainder for?\n\nThe modulo `%` is far more useful than it looks:\n\n- a number is **even** if `number % 2` is 0;\n- converting minutes into hours: `135 // 60` gives 2 hours, `135 % 60` gives 15 minutes."},{"t":"code","code":"minutes = 135\nhours = minutes // 60\nremaining = minutes % 60\nprint(minutes, \"minutes =\", hours, \"h\", remaining, \"min\")","run":true},{"t":"quiz","code":"print(17 % 5)","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["3","2","3.4"],"answer":1,"explain":"17 divided by 5: 3 times (15), remainder 2. `17 // 5` is 3 and `17 % 5` is 2."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Order of operations\n\nLike in maths, multiplication comes before addition. **Parentheses** force the order you want."},{"t":"quiz","code":"print(2 + 3 * 4)","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["20","14","24"],"answer":1,"explain":"`3 * 4` first (12), then `2 + 12` = 14. To get 20, you'd write `(2 + 3) * 4`."},{"t":"code","code":"grade1 = 12\ngrade2 = 15\ngrade3 = 17\nwrong_average = grade1 + grade2 + grade3 / 3\naverage = (grade1 + grade2 + grade3) / 3\nprint(wrong_average)\nprint(average)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"The first calculation is wrong: only `grade3` was divided by 3. A classic!"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Rounding\n\n`round(number, digits)` rounds a number. Without the second argument, it rounds to a whole number."},{"t":"code","code":"average = 14.666666666\nprint(round(average))\nprint(round(average, 1))\nprint(round(average, 2))\nprint(round(0.1 + 0.2, 2))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"A few other handy functions: `abs()` gives the absolute value, `min()` and `max()` the smallest and the largest."},{"t":"code","code":"print(abs(-12))\nprint(min(4, 9, 2))\nprint(max(4, 9, 2))","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the length of the film"},{"t":"exercise","id":"nombres-ex1","title":"Hours and minutes","prompt":"A film lasts `duration = 158` minutes. Using `//` and `%`, calculate:\n\n- `hours`: the number of full hours;\n- `minutes`: the remaining minutes.\n\nThen display `2 h 38 min` (with a `print` that uses your two variables).","starter":"duration = 158\n\nhours = \nminutes = \nprint(hours, \"h\", minutes, \"min\")","solution":"duration = 158\n\nhours = duration // 60\nminutes = duration % 60\nprint(hours, \"h\", minutes, \"min\")","check":"assert hours == 2, f\"hours should be 2, it is {hours}.\"\nassert minutes == 38, f\"minutes should be 38, it is {minutes}.\"\nassert \"duration\" in CODE.split(\"hours =\")[1].split(\"\\n\")[0], \"Calculate hours from the duration variable (not by writing 2 directly).\"\nassert OUT.strip() == \"2 h 38 min\", f\"The expected output is \\\"2 h 38 min\\\", got \\\"{OUT.strip()}\\\".\"","hint":"An hour has 60 minutes. duration // 60 gives the hours, duration % 60 the remainder.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the average"},{"t":"exercise","id":"nombres-ex2","title":"Term average","prompt":"Awa got three grades out of 20: `13.5`, `16` and `11`.\n\nCalculate her average in a variable `average`, **rounded to 2 decimal places**, then display it.","starter":"grade1 = 13.5\ngrade2 = 16\ngrade3 = 11\n\naverage = \nprint(average)","solution":"grade1 = 13.5\ngrade2 = 16\ngrade3 = 11\n\naverage = round((grade1 + grade2 + grade3) / 3, 2)\nprint(average)","check":"assert average == 13.5, f\"The rounded average should be 13.5 (40.5 / 3), you get {average}. Did you put parentheses around the sum?\"\nassert \"round\" in CODE, \"Use round(..., 2) to round.\"\nassert \"13.5\" in OUT, \"Display the average with print(average).\"","hint":"Don't forget the parentheses around the sum, then wrap everything in round(..., 2).","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"textes","title":"Text and f-strings","subtitle":"Joining, cleaning and slicing strings.","duration":14,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Joining texts\n\n`+` between two texts **sticks** them together (we say *concatenate*). `*` repeats a text."},{"t":"code","code":"name = \"Moussa\"\ngreeting = \"Hello \" + name + \"!\"\nprint(greeting)\nprint(\"Ha\" * 3)\nprint(\"-\" * 20)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"But careful: you can't join text and a number with `+`."},{"t":"code","code":"age = 30\nprint(\"I am \" + age + \" years old\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Python refuses (`TypeError`): it doesn't know whether to add or to join. You need to convert the number to text with `str(age)`. Fix the code above."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## f-strings: the modern way\n\nThere's a much handier way: **f-strings**. You put an `f` before the quotes, and write the variables **between curly braces** `{ }` directly in the text."},{"t":"code","code":"name = \"Moussa\"\nage = 30\ncity = \"Kinshasa\"\nprint(f\"My name is {name}, I am {age} and I live in {city}.\")\nprint(f\"In 10 years, I'll be {age + 10}.\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"You can even calculate inside the braces, and format numbers. `:.2f` shows 2 digits after the decimal point:"},{"t":"code","code":"price = 7.5\nquantity = 3\nprint(f\"Total: {price * quantity:.2f} euros\")","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"","v":"f-strings are the most common way to display results in Python. You'll see them everywhere, especially in data analysis."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## String methods\n\nA piece of text has **methods**: actions you apply to it with a dot. They return a new text."},{"t":"code","code":"name = \"  aWa diallo  \"\nprint(name.upper())\nprint(name.lower())\nprint(name.strip())\nprint(name.strip().title())\nprint(\"coffee with milk\".replace(\"milk\", \"honey\"))\nprint(\"banana\".count(\"a\"))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"| Method | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `.upper()` | all upper case |\n| `.lower()` | all lower case |\n| `.title()` | Capital At Each Word |\n| `.strip()` | removes spaces at the start and end |\n| `.replace(a, b)` | replaces a with b |\n| `.count(x)` | counts the x |\n\nThese methods are essential to **clean data**: in a real file, you'll find \"Dakar\", \"dakar\" and \"DAKAR  \" all meaning the same city."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Length, positions and slicing\n\n`len()` gives the number of characters. Each character has a **position** (an *index*), and counting starts at **0**."},{"t":"code","code":"word = \"Python\"\nprint(len(word))\nprint(word[0])\nprint(word[1])\nprint(word[-1])\nprint(word[0:3])\nprint(word[2:])","run":true},{"t":"html","v":"<div class=\"flow\" aria-label=\"Positions of the letters in the word Python\">\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>P</b><span>0 or -6</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>y</b><span>1 or -5</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>t</b><span>2 or -4</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>h</b><span>3 or -3</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>o</b><span>4 or -2</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>n</b><span>5 or -1</span></div>\n</div>"},{"t":"md","v":"`word[0:3]` takes from position 0 **up to 3, excluded**: `Pyt`. Negative positions count from the end."},{"t":"quiz","code":"city = \"Abidjan\"\nprint(city[0] + city[-1])","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["Ab","An","Aj"],"answer":1,"explain":"`city[0]` is the first character (A), `city[-1]` the last (n). Joined: \"An\"."},{"t":"md","v":"To find out whether a text contains another, use `in`:"},{"t":"code","code":"print(\"juice\" in \"bissap juice\")\nprint(\"tea\" in \"coffee\")","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the badge"},{"t":"exercise","id":"textes-ex1","title":"The conference badge","prompt":"The variables `name` and `company` hold a participant's details. With **an f-string**, display exactly:\n\n`Welcome Grace (Orange Digital)!`","starter":"name = \"Grace\"\ncompany = \"Orange Digital\"\n\nprint(f\"...\")","solution":"name = \"Grace\"\ncompany = \"Orange Digital\"\n\nprint(f\"Welcome {name} ({company})!\")","check":"assert 'f\"' in CODE or \"f'\" in CODE, \"Use an f-string: f\\\"...\\\".\"\nassert OUT.strip() == \"Welcome Grace (Orange Digital)!\", f\"Expected \\\"Welcome Grace (Orange Digital)!\\\", got \\\"{OUT.strip()}\\\".\"\nassert \"{name}\" in CODE.replace(\" \", \"\"), \"Use the name variable between braces rather than typing Grace by hand.\"","hint":"In an f-string, write the variables between curly braces: {name}. The parentheses around the company are plain text.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: cleaning a name"},{"t":"exercise","id":"textes-ex2","title":"A nice clean name","prompt":"A form was badly filled in: `raw = \"   kOFI aNNAN   \"`.\n\nCreate a variable `clean` that holds `Kofi Annan` (no spaces around it, a capital at each word) using string methods, then create `initials` holding `KA` by taking the first letter of each word.","starter":"raw = \"   kOFI aNNAN   \"\n\nclean = \nprint(clean)\n\ninitials = \nprint(initials)","solution":"raw = \"   kOFI aNNAN   \"\n\nclean = raw.strip().title()\nprint(clean)\n\ninitials = clean[0] + clean[5]\nprint(initials)","check":"assert clean == \"Kofi Annan\", f\"clean should be \\\"Kofi Annan\\\", it is \\\"{clean}\\\".\"\nassert initials == \"KA\", f\"initials should be \\\"KA\\\", it is \\\"{initials}\\\".\"\nassert \"Kofi Annan\" not in CODE, \"Compute clean from raw with methods, without copying the result.\"","hint":"Chain the methods: raw.strip().title(). For the initials, in \"Kofi Annan\" the K is at position 0, the A at position 5.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"booleens","title":"True or false, and conversions","subtitle":"Booleans, comparisons and the input() function.","duration":14,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Only two values\n\nA **boolean** (`bool`) can only be one of two things: `True` or `False`. With a capital letter, without quotes.\n\nYou mostly get them by **comparing** values:\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `==` | is equal to |\n| `!=` | is different from |\n| `<` / `>` | less than / greater than |\n| `<=` / `>=` | less than or equal / greater than or equal |"},{"t":"code","code":"age = 17\nprint(age >= 18)\nprint(age == 17)\nprint(\"Dakar\" == \"dakar\")\nprint(10 != 3)","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"Don't confuse `=` and `==`. A single `=` **stores** a value in a variable. Two `==` **ask a question**: \"is it equal?\"."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Combining: and, or, not\n\n- `and`: true if **both** conditions are true;\n- `or`: true if **at least one** is true;\n- `not`: swaps true and false."},{"t":"code","code":"age = 22\nhas_ticket = True\nprint(age >= 18 and has_ticket)\nprint(age < 12 or age > 65)\nprint(not has_ticket)","run":true},{"t":"quiz","q":"A discount applies if the customer has a loyalty card **or** if their basket is over 50 euros. The customer has no card and their basket is 60 euros. Do they get the discount?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"explain":"`False or True` gives `True`: with `or`, one true condition is enough."},{"t":"quiz","code":"x = 5\nprint(x > 1 and x < 4)","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["True","False","5"],"answer":1,"explain":"`x > 1` is true, but `x < 4` is false. With `and`, both must be true: the result is `False`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Converting from one type to another\n\nYou often switch from one type to another:\n\n- `int(\"25\")` turns the text `\"25\"` into the whole number 25;\n- `float(\"3.5\")` gives the decimal number 3.5;\n- `str(25)` gives the text `\"25\"`."},{"t":"code","code":"text = \"25\"\nnumber = int(text)\nprint(text * 2)\nprint(number * 2)\nprint(type(text), type(number))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Same value displayed, very different behaviour: the text repeats, the number multiplies. And if the conversion is impossible, Python says so:"},{"t":"code","code":"int(\"twenty-five\")","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Asking for a value with input()\n\n`input()` asks the user a question and waits for the answer. **The answer is always text**, even if you type a number.\n\nOn this site, since there's no \"live\" keyboard while the code runs, you type your answers in advance in the **Inputs** box under the editor, one per line. Run it, then replace the values with your first name and your year of birth."},{"t":"code","code":"name = input(\"What is your name? \")\nyear = input(\"What year were you born? \")\nage = 2026 - int(year)\nprint(f\"Nice to meet you {name}, you are (or will be) {age} this year.\")","run":true,"inputs":"Awa\n2001"},{"t":"md","v":"Without `int(year)`, the subtraction would be impossible: you can't subtract text from a number."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: can they vote?"},{"t":"exercise","id":"booleens-ex1","title":"The right to vote","prompt":"From the variable `age`, create a variable `can_vote` that is `True` if the age is greater than **or equal to** 18, and `False` otherwise. Display it.\n\nYour code must work for any age: don't write `True` by hand, use a comparison.","starter":"age = 20\n\ncan_vote = \nprint(can_vote)","solution":"age = 20\n\ncan_vote = age >= 18\nprint(can_vote)","check":"assert isinstance(can_vote, bool), \"can_vote must be a boolean (True or False), obtained with a comparison.\"\nassert \">=\" in CODE or \">\" in CODE, \"Use a comparison with age.\"\nns = {}\nexec(CODE.replace(\"age = 20\", \"age = 18\", 1).replace(\"print(\", \"(lambda *a, **k: None)(\"), ns)\nassert ns[\"can_vote\"] is True, \"With age = 18, can_vote should be True (18 counts as \\\"greater than or equal\\\").\"\nns = {}\nexec(CODE.replace(\"age = 20\", \"age = 15\", 1).replace(\"print(\", \"(lambda *a, **k: None)(\"), ns)\nassert ns[\"can_vote\"] is False, \"With age = 15, can_vote should be False.\"","hint":"A comparison directly gives True or False: can_vote = age >= 18","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the till"},{"t":"exercise","id":"booleens-ex2","title":"The cash register","prompt":"The program asks for an item's **price**, then the **quantity**, with `input()`. The answers are already in the Inputs box: `2.5` and `4`.\n\nConvert them correctly (the price can have decimals, the quantity is a whole number), calculate the total and display:\n\n`Total: 10.0 euros`","starter":"price = input(\"Item price? \")\nquantity = input(\"Quantity? \")\n\ntotal = price * quantity\nprint(f\"Total: {total} euros\")","solution":"price = float(input(\"Item price? \"))\nquantity = int(input(\"Quantity? \"))\n\ntotal = price * quantity\nprint(f\"Total: {total} euros\")","check":"assert \"Total: 10.0 euros\" in OUT, \"The program must display \\\"Total: 10.0 euros\\\" with the inputs 2.5 and 4.\"\nassert \"float(\" in CODE, \"The price must be converted with float().\"\nassert \"int(\" in CODE, \"The quantity must be converted with int().\"","hint":"input() always returns text. Wrap it in float(...) for the price and int(...) for the quantity.","inputs":"2.5\n4"}]]}]},{"id":"logique","icon":"🔀","title":"Deciding and repeating","desc":"Conditions and loops: the moment your program starts to think.","badge":"Logician","lessons":[{"id":"conditions","title":"Making decisions with if","subtitle":"if, elif, else and indentation.","duration":14,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## If… then…\n\nWith `if`, your program runs a block of code **only if** a condition is true."},{"t":"code","code":"balance = 15000\nprice = 12000\n\nif balance >= price:\n    print(\"Payment accepted\")\n    balance = balance - price\n    print(\"New balance:\", balance)\n\nprint(\"Thank you for your visit\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Three essential details:\n\n1. the `if` line ends with a **colon** `:`;\n2. the lines of the block are **shifted by 4 spaces** (we say *indented*);\n3. the first non-indented line marks the **end of the block**: `Thank you for your visit` is displayed in every case.\n\nChange the price to `20000` and run again."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Otherwise: else\n\n`else` says what to do when the condition is false."},{"t":"code","code":"balance = 15000\nprice = 20000\n\nif balance >= price:\n    print(\"Payment accepted\")\nelse:\n    missing = price - balance\n    print(f\"Insufficient balance, {missing} francs missing\")","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"Indentation isn't decoration in Python: it's what says which lines belong to the block. Always use 4 spaces (the Tab key does it for you in the editor)."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Several cases: elif\n\n`elif` (short for *else if*) chains several conditions. Python tests them **in order** and runs **only the first** block whose condition is true."},{"t":"widget","name":"condition","config":{"name":"grade","label":"Grade out of 20:","min":0,"max":20,"value":13,"branches":[{"code":"if grade >= 16:","gte":16,"out":"Excellent"},{"code":"elif grade >= 14:","gte":14,"out":"Very good"},{"code":"elif grade >= 12:","gte":12,"out":"Good"},{"code":"elif grade >= 10:","gte":10,"out":"Pass"},{"code":"else:","out":"Not passed yet"}]}},{"t":"quiz","code":"grade = 17\nif grade >= 10:\n    print(\"Pass\")\nelif grade >= 16:\n    print(\"Excellent\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Fail\")","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["Pass","Excellent","Pass, then Excellent"],"answer":0,"explain":"17 >= 10 is true: Python runs the first block and **ignores all the others**. The order of conditions matters: test the most demanding cases first."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Combined conditions\n\nThe `and`, `or` and `not` from the previous chapter fit naturally into an `if`."},{"t":"code","code":"age = 25\nstudent = True\n\nif age < 12 or age >= 65:\n    fare = 0\nelif student and age < 26:\n    fare = 500\nelse:\n    fare = 1000\n\nprint(f\"Your bus ticket costs {fare} francs\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Try different ages, and `student = False`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: even or odd?"},{"t":"exercise","id":"conditions-ex1","title":"Even or odd","prompt":"Write a program that displays `even` if the number `n` is even, and `odd` otherwise.\n\nReminder: a number is even if the remainder of its division by 2 is 0.","starter":"n = 42","solution":"n = 42\n\nif n % 2 == 0:\n    print(\"even\")\nelse:\n    print(\"odd\")","check":"assert \"if\" in CODE, \"Use an if condition.\"\ndef run_with(v):\n    import io, contextlib\n    buf = io.StringIO()\n    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):\n        exec(CODE.replace(\"n = 42\", f\"n = {v}\", 1), {})\n    return buf.getvalue().strip()\nassert run_with(42) == \"even\", \"With n = 42, the program must display \\\"even\\\".\"\nassert run_with(7) == \"odd\", \"With n = 7, the program must display \\\"odd\\\".\"\nassert run_with(0) == \"even\", \"With n = 0, the program must display \\\"even\\\".\"","hint":"if n % 2 == 0: then, below and indented, print(\"even\"). Then else: and print(\"odd\").","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: delivery fees"},{"t":"exercise","id":"conditions-ex2","title":"Delivery fees","prompt":"An online shop calculates its delivery fees from the basket amount:\n\n- **50 euros or more**: free delivery (0);\n- **between 20 (included) and 50 euros**: 3 euros;\n- **less than 20 euros**: 5 euros.\n\nComplete the program so that the variable `fee` has the right value, whatever `basket` is.","starter":"basket = 35\n\n# calculate fee with if / elif / else\n\nprint(f\"Delivery fee: {fee} euros\")","solution":"basket = 35\n\nif basket >= 50:\n    fee = 0\nelif basket >= 20:\n    fee = 3\nelse:\n    fee = 5\n\nprint(f\"Delivery fee: {fee} euros\")","check":"def fee_for(v):\n    ns = {}\n    exec(CODE.replace(\"basket = 35\", f\"basket = {v}\", 1).replace(\"print(\", \"(lambda *a, **k: None)(\"), ns)\n    return ns.get(\"fee\")\nfor v, expected in [(35, 3), (50, 0), (80, 0), (20, 3), (19.99, 5), (5, 5)]:\n    got = fee_for(v)\n    assert got == expected, f\"For a basket of {v} euros, the fee should be {expected}, your program gives {got}.\"","hint":"Start with the highest case: if basket >= 50: fee = 0, then elif basket >= 20: fee = 3, then else: fee = 5.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"boucle-for","title":"Repeating with for","subtitle":"Make the computer work for you.","duration":14,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## The for loop\n\nA **loop** repeats a block of code. The `for` loop repeats it **for each item** of a sequence."},{"t":"code","code":"for i in range(5):\n    print(\"I must not chat in class.\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Five lines from two lines of code. For 500, just change the 5. Like `if`, the `for` line ends with `:` and the repeated block is indented."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## range(), the number generator\n\n`range` produces a sequence of whole numbers:\n\n| Written | Numbers produced |\n|---|---|\n| `range(5)` | 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 |\n| `range(1, 6)` | 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 |\n| `range(0, 20, 5)` | 0, 5, 10, 15 |\n| `range(10, 0, -1)` | 10, 9, … 1 |\n\nThe end value is always **excluded**. The loop variable (here `n`) takes each value in turn:"},{"t":"code","code":"for n in range(1, 6):\n    print(n, \"squared =\", n ** 2)","run":true},{"t":"quiz","code":"for i in range(2, 8, 2):\n    print(i)","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["2 4 6 8","2 4 6","2 3 4 5 6 7"],"answer":1,"explain":"We start at 2, go up in steps of 2, and stop **before** 8: 2, 4, 6."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Looping over text\n\n`for` works on anything that contains several items. A text, for example, contains characters:"},{"t":"code","code":"for letter in \"Bissap\":\n    print(letter)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"And soon you'll loop over lists of sales, grades or customers with exactly the same syntax."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## The accumulator pattern\n\nHere is the most important pattern in all of data programming: **accumulating** a result in a variable."},{"t":"widget","name":"stepper","config":{"code":"weekly_sales = 0\nfor day in range(1, 4):\n    weekly_sales = weekly_sales + 100 * day\nprint(weekly_sales)","steps":[{"line":1,"vars":{},"note":"We prepare a box that starts at 0."},{"line":2,"vars":{"weekly_sales":"0"},"note":"Start of the loop: day will take the values 1, 2, 3."},{"line":3,"vars":{"weekly_sales":"0","day":"1"},"note":"0 + 100 × 1 = 100"},{"line":3,"vars":{"weekly_sales":"100","day":"2"},"note":"100 + 100 × 2 = 300"},{"line":3,"vars":{"weekly_sales":"300","day":"3"},"note":"300 + 100 × 3 = 600"},{"line":4,"vars":{"weekly_sales":"600","day":"3"},"out":"600","note":"The loop is over: the box holds the total."}]}},{"t":"md","v":"In Python, `total = total + x` can also be written `total += x`. It's shorter and very common."},{"t":"code","code":"total = 0\nfor n in range(1, 11):\n    total += n\nprint(\"The sum from 1 to 10 is\", total)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Counting\n\nA special case of the accumulator: the **counter**. You add 1 each time a condition is true. Here we combine `for` and `if`:"},{"t":"code","code":"sentence = \"data is all around us every day\"\ncount_a = 0\nfor letter in sentence:\n    if letter == \"a\":\n        count_a += 1\nprint(f\"The letter a appears {count_a} times\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Notice the **two levels of indentation**: the `if` is inside the loop, and `count_a += 1` is inside the `if`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the times table"},{"t":"exercise","id":"boucle-for-ex1","title":"The 7 times table","prompt":"With a `for` loop, display the 7 times table, from 1 to 10, in this form:\n\n    7 x 1 = 7\n    7 x 2 = 14\n    ...\n    7 x 10 = 70","starter":"n = 7","solution":"n = 7\n\nfor i in range(1, 11):\n    print(f\"{n} x {i} = {n * i}\")","check":"assert \"for\" in CODE, \"Use a for loop.\"\nlines = [l.strip() for l in OUT.strip().split(\"\\n\")]\nexpected = [f\"7 x {i} = {7 * i}\" for i in range(1, 11)]\nassert len(lines) == 10, f\"You need 10 lines, your program displays {len(lines)}.\"\nassert lines == expected, f\"Check the format. Expected first line: \\\"7 x 1 = 7\\\", got: \\\"{lines[0]}\\\".\"","hint":"for i in range(1, 11): then, inside the loop, print(f\"{n} x {i} = {n * i}\")","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the sum"},{"t":"exercise","id":"boucle-for-ex2","title":"The sum of the first 100 numbers","prompt":"The story goes that a 9-year-old pupil, Carl Friedrich Gauss, worked out 1 + 2 + 3 + … + 100 in his head. You have Python.\n\nCalculate this sum with a loop and an accumulator, in a variable `total`, then display it.","starter":"total = 0\n\nprint(total)","solution":"total = 0\nfor n in range(1, 101):\n    total += n\nprint(total)","check":"assert \"for\" in CODE, \"Use a for loop.\"\nassert total == 5050, f\"The sum should be 5050, you get {total}. Check your range bounds (the end is excluded).\"\nassert \"5050\" not in CODE, \"Don't type the result by hand: calculate it with the loop.\"","hint":"range(1, 101) produces the numbers from 1 to 100. On each lap: total += n.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: counting vowels"},{"t":"exercise","id":"boucle-for-ex3","title":"Vowel counter","prompt":"Count the vowels (a, e, i, o, u, y) in the variable `sentence` and store the result in `vowels`. Display it.\n\nTip: `letter in \"aeiouy\"` is `True` if the letter is a vowel.","starter":"sentence = \"python is a wonderful language\"\nvowels = 0\n\nprint(vowels)","solution":"sentence = \"python is a wonderful language\"\nvowels = 0\nfor letter in sentence:\n    if letter in \"aeiouy\":\n        vowels += 1\nprint(vowels)","check":"expected = sum(1 for c in \"python is a wonderful language\" if c in \"aeiouy\")\nassert vowels == expected, f\"There are {expected} vowels in the sentence, your program counts {vowels}.\"\nassert \"for\" in CODE, \"Use a for loop.\"","hint":"for letter in sentence: then if letter in \"aeiouy\": then vowels += 1 (watch the two levels of indentation).","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"boucle-while","title":"Repeating with while","subtitle":"While… and how to avoid the infinite loop.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## As long as the condition is true\n\n`for` repeats a number of times known in advance. But sometimes, you don't know how many laps you'll need. Then you use `while`: the block is repeated **as long as** the condition stays true."},{"t":"code","code":"savings = 0\nmonths = 0\nwhile savings < 100000:\n    savings += 15000\n    months += 1\nprint(f\"Goal reached in {months} months, with {savings} francs\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"We didn't know in advance it would take 7 months: the loop found out."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## The infinite loop\n\nIf the condition **never** becomes false, the loop never stops. That's the **infinite loop**, the classic trap."},{"t":"code","code":"counter = 1\nwhile counter <= 5:\n    print(counter)\n    # oops: we forgot counter += 1","run":false},{"t":"md","v":"Here, `counter` is always 1, so `counter <= 5` is always true. The program runs forever."},{"t":"callout","kind":"info","title":"","v":"No panic if it happens to you on this site: after a few seconds, the program is stopped automatically and Python restarts. On your own computer, you stop a stuck program with Ctrl + C."},{"t":"quiz","q":"What is most often missing in an infinite loop?","options":["The `print`","The line that changes the variable tested by the condition","The colon"],"answer":1,"explain":"For the loop to stop, each lap must bring the condition closer to \"false\": increase a counter, decrease a stock, etc."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Leaving early: break and continue\n\n- `break` stops the loop **immediately**;\n- `continue` skips **to the next lap**, without running the rest of the block."},{"t":"code","code":"# Looking for the first multiple of 7 above 50\nn = 51\nwhile True:\n    if n % 7 == 0:\n        break\n    n += 1\nprint(\"Found:\", n)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`while True` is a deliberately infinite loop: only `break` gets out of it. It's a common pattern when searching for something."},{"t":"code","code":"for n in range(1, 11):\n    if n % 3 == 0:\n        continue\n    print(n)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Multiples of 3 are skipped thanks to `continue`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## A little game\n\nLet's combine `while`, `input` and `if`. The program has picked a secret number; you get several tries. Your guesses go in the Inputs box, one per line. Run it with the suggested values, then try your own."},{"t":"code","code":"secret = 37\ntries = 0\nfound = False\n\nwhile not found:\n    guess = int(input(\"Your number? \"))\n    tries += 1\n    if guess < secret:\n        print(\"Higher!\")\n    elif guess > secret:\n        print(\"Lower!\")\n    else:\n        found = True\n\nprint(f\"Well done, found in {tries} tries!\")","run":true,"inputs":"50\n25\n37"},{"t":"md","v":"By cutting the range in half each time (50, then 25, then 37…), you find any number between 1 and 100 in at most 7 tries. It's a famous algorithm: **binary search**."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: population"},{"t":"exercise","id":"boucle-while-ex1","title":"Doubling","prompt":"A city has `population = 500000` inhabitants. It grows by **4% a year** (each year, the population is multiplied by 1.04).\n\nWith a `while` loop, calculate how many years (`years`) it takes for the population to **exceed 1 million** (strictly more than 1,000,000). Display `years`.","starter":"population = 500000\nyears = 0\n\nprint(years)","solution":"population = 500000\nyears = 0\nwhile population <= 1000000:\n    population = population * 1.04\n    years += 1\nprint(years)","check":"assert \"while\" in CODE, \"Use a while loop.\"\nassert years == 18, f\"It takes 18 years to exceed 1 million, your program finds {years}.\"","hint":"while population <= 1000000: then, inside the loop, multiply population by 1.04 and add 1 to years.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: countdown"},{"t":"exercise","id":"boucle-while-ex2","title":"Lift-off","prompt":"With a `while` loop, display a countdown from 5 to 1, one number per line, then `Lift-off!`.","starter":"count = 5","solution":"count = 5\nwhile count > 0:\n    print(count)\n    count -= 1\nprint(\"Lift-off!\")","check":"assert \"while\" in CODE, \"Use a while loop.\"\nlines = [l.strip() for l in OUT.strip().split(\"\\n\")]\nassert lines == [\"5\", \"4\", \"3\", \"2\", \"1\", \"Lift-off!\"], f\"Expected: 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 then Lift-off! (one value per line). Got: {lines}\"","hint":"while count > 0: then print(count) and count -= 1. The print(\"Lift-off!\") goes after the loop (not indented).","inputs":""}]]}]},{"id":"structures","icon":"📦","title":"Organizing your data","desc":"Lists, dictionaries, tuples and sets: Python's data containers.","badge":"Big tidy-up","lessons":[{"id":"listes","title":"Lists","subtitle":"Many values in a single variable.","duration":15,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## One variable, lots of values\n\nImagine the grades of 30 students. Creating 30 variables `grade1`, `grade2`… would be absurd. We use a **list**: an ordered sequence of values, between **square brackets**, separated by commas."},{"t":"code","code":"grades = [12, 15, 9, 17, 14]\ncities = [\"Kinshasa\", \"Abidjan\", \"Dakar\"]\nmixed = [\"Awa\", 24, True, 1.75]\n\nprint(grades)\nprint(len(cities))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`len()` gives the number of items, just like for text."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Accessing items\n\nAs with text, each item has a **position** that starts at **0**."},{"t":"code","code":"cities = [\"Kinshasa\", \"Abidjan\", \"Dakar\", \"Paris\", \"Montreal\"]\nprint(cities[0])\nprint(cities[2])\nprint(cities[-1])\nprint(cities[1:3])","run":true},{"t":"quiz","code":"fruits = [\"mango\", \"pineapple\", \"papaya\", \"banana\"]\nprint(fruits[1])","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["mango","pineapple","papaya"],"answer":1,"explain":"Position 0 is \"mango\", position 1 is \"pineapple\". Always count from 0!"},{"t":"md","v":"And what if you ask for a position that doesn't exist?"},{"t":"code","code":"cities = [\"Kinshasa\", \"Abidjan\", \"Dakar\"]\nprint(cities[3])","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`IndexError`: the list has 3 items, at positions 0, 1 and 2. Position 3 doesn't exist."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Changing a list\n\nUnlike text, lists can be **changed**."},{"t":"code","code":"shopping = [\"rice\", \"oil\"]\nshopping.append(\"tomatoes\")     # adds at the end\nshopping.insert(0, \"bread\")     # inserts at position 0\nshopping[1] = \"basmati rice\"    # replaces the item at position 1\nshopping.remove(\"oil\")          # removes this item\nlast = shopping.pop()           # removes and returns the last one\nprint(shopping)\nprint(\"Removed:\", last)\nprint(\"basmati rice\" in shopping)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"| Method | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `.append(x)` | adds x at the end |\n| `.insert(i, x)` | inserts x at position i |\n| `.remove(x)` | removes the first occurrence of x |\n| `.pop()` | removes the last item and returns it |\n| `.sort()` | sorts the list |\n| `.count(x)` | counts the x |"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Calculating on a list\n\nPython has ready-made functions for lists of numbers:"},{"t":"code","code":"sales = [120, 95, 210, 180, 75, 160, 230]\nprint(\"Total:\", sum(sales))\nprint(\"Minimum:\", min(sales))\nprint(\"Maximum:\", max(sales))\nprint(\"Average:\", round(sum(sales) / len(sales), 1))\nprint(\"Sorted:\", sorted(sales))\nprint(\"Largest to smallest:\", sorted(sales, reverse=True))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`sorted(list)` returns a **new** sorted list, while `list.sort()` sorts the list itself."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Looping over a list\n\nThe `for` loop goes through a list item by item. It's the most used combination in Python."},{"t":"code","code":"temperatures = [28, 31, 35, 30, 27, 33, 36]\nhot_days = 0\nfor t in temperatures:\n    if t > 32:\n        hot_days += 1\n        print(f\"{t} °C: it's hot!\")\nprint(f\"{hot_days} days above 32 °C\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"You can also **build** a list in a loop: start from an empty list `[]` and use `append`."},{"t":"code","code":"prices = [1000, 2500, 400]\nprices_with_tax = []\nfor p in prices:\n    prices_with_tax.append(p * 1.18)\nprint(prices_with_tax)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the best grade, by hand"},{"t":"exercise","id":"listes-ex1","title":"The chapter 1 algorithm","prompt":"In chapter 1, you put the algorithm that finds the best grade back in order. Now write it in Python!\n\nFind the largest value in the list `grades` **without using `max()`**, and store it in `best`.\n\nReminder of the algorithm: remember the first grade, then go through the grades; if a grade is higher than the remembered one, remember it instead.","starter":"grades = [12, 15, 9, 17, 14, 11, 16]\n\nbest = grades[0]\n\nprint(best)","solution":"grades = [12, 15, 9, 17, 14, 11, 16]\n\nbest = grades[0]\nfor g in grades:\n    if g > best:\n        best = g\n\nprint(best)","check":"assert \"max(\" not in CODE, \"No max() here: the goal is to write the algorithm yourself.\"\nassert \"for\" in CODE, \"Go through the list with a for loop.\"\nassert best == 17, f\"The best grade is 17, your program finds {best}.\"","hint":"for g in grades: then if g > best: then best = g","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the report card"},{"t":"exercise","id":"listes-ex2","title":"The class report card","prompt":"From the list `grades`:\n\n1. add the grade `18` of a student who was absent when they were entered (with `append`);\n2. calculate `average`, the class average rounded to 2 decimal places;\n3. calculate `passed`, the number of grades greater than or equal to 10.","starter":"grades = [12, 8, 15, 9.5, 14, 6, 17, 11]\n\naverage = \npassed = 0\n\nprint(average, passed)","solution":"grades = [12, 8, 15, 9.5, 14, 6, 17, 11]\ngrades.append(18)\n\naverage = round(sum(grades) / len(grades), 2)\npassed = 0\nfor g in grades:\n    if g >= 10:\n        passed += 1\n\nprint(average, passed)","check":"assert len(grades) == 9 and grades[-1] == 18, \"Add the grade 18 at the end of the list with grades.append(18).\"\nassert average == round(110.5 / 9, 2), f\"The expected average is {round(110.5 / 9, 2)}, you get {average}.\"\nassert passed == 6, f\"There are 6 grades greater than or equal to 10, you count {passed}.\"","hint":"grades.append(18); average = round(sum(grades) / len(grades), 2); then a counter in a for loop.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"dictionnaires","title":"Dictionaries","subtitle":"Linking a key to a value, like a real dictionary.","duration":15,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Look up by name, not by position\n\nIn a list, you find an item by its **position**. But to describe a product, you'd rather find each piece of information by its **name**: the price, the stock, the category…\n\nA **dictionary** (`dict`) links **keys** to **values**, between curly braces:"},{"t":"code","code":"product = {\n    \"name\": \"Bissap juice\",\n    \"price\": 2.2,\n    \"stock\": 48,\n    \"category\": \"Drink\",\n}\nprint(product[\"name\"])\nprint(product[\"price\"] * 3)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Like in a real dictionary: you look up a word (the key) to find its definition (the value)."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Change, add, check"},{"t":"code","code":"product = {\"name\": \"Croissant\", \"price\": 1.8, \"stock\": 30}\n\nproduct[\"stock\"] = product[\"stock\"] - 5   # change\nproduct[\"organic\"] = True                 # add a key\nprint(product)\n\nprint(\"price\" in product)                 # does the key exist?\nprint(product.get(\"colour\", \"unknown\"))   # default value if missing","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Asking for a key that doesn't exist with square brackets causes a `KeyError`. The `.get(key, default)` method avoids the error."},{"t":"code","code":"product = {\"name\": \"Croissant\", \"price\": 1.8}\nprint(product[\"Price\"])","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Capital letter! `\"Price\"` and `\"price\"` are two different keys."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Looping over a dictionary"},{"t":"code","code":"stock = {\"Espresso\": 120, \"Cappuccino\": 80, \"Mint tea\": 45}\n\nfor product in stock:\n    print(product)\n\nfor product, quantity in stock.items():\n    print(f\"{product}: {quantity} units\")\n\nprint(list(stock.keys()))\nprint(sum(stock.values()))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`.items()` gives the (key, value) pairs: it's the handiest way to loop over a dictionary."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Counting with a dictionary\n\nA very common use: counting how many times each item appears. That's exactly what an analyst does when counting sales per city."},{"t":"code","code":"sales = [\"Dakar\", \"Paris\", \"Dakar\", \"Kinshasa\", \"Dakar\", \"Paris\"]\ncount = {}\nfor city in sales:\n    if city in count:\n        count[city] += 1\n    else:\n        count[city] = 1\nprint(count)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## A list of dictionaries = a table\n\nHere's the idea that links to data analysis. A **list of dictionaries** looks exactly like a spreadsheet: each dictionary is a **row**, each key a **column**."},{"t":"code","code":"students = [\n    {\"name\": \"Awa\", \"city\": \"Dakar\", \"grade\": 15},\n    {\"name\": \"Kofi\", \"city\": \"Abidjan\", \"grade\": 12},\n    {\"name\": \"Gloire\", \"city\": \"Kinshasa\", \"grade\": 17},\n]\n\nfor s in students:\n    print(f\"{s['name']:<8} {s['city']:<10} {s['grade']}\")\n\naverage = sum(s[\"grade\"] for s in students) / len(students)\nprint(\"Average:\", round(average, 2))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"| name | city | grade |\n|---|---|---|\n| Awa | Dakar | 15 |\n| Kofi | Abidjan | 12 |\n| Gloire | Kinshasa | 17 |\n\nIn the last chapter, the **pandas** library will handle tables like this with thousands of rows, in a single instruction."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"","v":"In an f-string, `{s['name']:<8}` left-aligns the text on 8 characters. Handy for displaying tidy tables. Notice the single quotes inside the double quotes."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the stock"},{"t":"exercise","id":"dictionnaires-ex1","title":"Stock management","prompt":"The dictionary `stock` gives the quantities in store. Today:\n\n1. we sold **12 croissants** (decrease the stock);\n2. we received a new product: **`\"Sandwich\"`** with **20** units (add it);\n3. calculate `total`: the total number of units in stock (all products together).","starter":"stock = {\"Croissant\": 40, \"Doughnut\": 25, \"Espresso\": 100}\n\ntotal = \nprint(stock)\nprint(total)","solution":"stock = {\"Croissant\": 40, \"Doughnut\": 25, \"Espresso\": 100}\nstock[\"Croissant\"] -= 12\nstock[\"Sandwich\"] = 20\n\ntotal = sum(stock.values())\nprint(stock)\nprint(total)","check":"assert stock.get(\"Croissant\") == 28, f\"There should be 28 croissants left, there are {stock.get('Croissant')}.\"\nassert stock.get(\"Sandwich\") == 20, \"Add the key \\\"Sandwich\\\" with the value 20 (mind the capital letter).\"\nassert total == 173, f\"The total should be 28 + 25 + 100 + 20 = 173, you get {total}.\"","hint":"stock[\"Croissant\"] -= 12, then stock[\"Sandwich\"] = 20, and finally sum(stock.values()).","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the revenue"},{"t":"exercise","id":"dictionnaires-ex2","title":"Today's orders","prompt":"The list `orders` holds today's orders (one per dictionary). Calculate:\n\n- `revenue`: the sum of `quantity × price` for all orders;\n- `by_city`: a dictionary giving the number of orders per city, for example `{\"Dakar\": 2, ...}`.","starter":"orders = [\n    {\"city\": \"Dakar\", \"quantity\": 3, \"price\": 2.5},\n    {\"city\": \"Paris\", \"quantity\": 1, \"price\": 5.0},\n    {\"city\": \"Dakar\", \"quantity\": 2, \"price\": 1.8},\n    {\"city\": \"Kinshasa\", \"quantity\": 6, \"price\": 1.0},\n    {\"city\": \"Paris\", \"quantity\": 2, \"price\": 3.5},\n]\n\nrevenue = 0\nby_city = {}\n\nprint(revenue)\nprint(by_city)","solution":"orders = [\n    {\"city\": \"Dakar\", \"quantity\": 3, \"price\": 2.5},\n    {\"city\": \"Paris\", \"quantity\": 1, \"price\": 5.0},\n    {\"city\": \"Dakar\", \"quantity\": 2, \"price\": 1.8},\n    {\"city\": \"Kinshasa\", \"quantity\": 6, \"price\": 1.0},\n    {\"city\": \"Paris\", \"quantity\": 2, \"price\": 3.5},\n]\n\nrevenue = 0\nby_city = {}\nfor o in orders:\n    revenue += o[\"quantity\"] * o[\"price\"]\n    if o[\"city\"] in by_city:\n        by_city[o[\"city\"]] += 1\n    else:\n        by_city[o[\"city\"]] = 1\n\nprint(revenue)\nprint(by_city)","check":"assert abs(revenue - 29.1) < 1e-6, f\"The revenue should be 29.1, you get {revenue}.\"\nassert by_city == {\"Dakar\": 2, \"Paris\": 2, \"Kinshasa\": 1}, f\"by_city should be {{'Dakar': 2, 'Paris': 2, 'Kinshasa': 1}}, you get {by_city}.\"","hint":"A single for o in orders loop is enough. Inside: revenue += o[\"quantity\"] * o[\"price\"], then the counter pattern with the dictionary.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"tuples-ensembles","title":"Tuples and sets","subtitle":"Two cousins of the list, each with its own superpower.","duration":10,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## The tuple: a frozen list\n\nA **tuple** is written between **parentheses**. It looks like a list, but it can't be changed once created. It's used for values that belong together and must not change: GPS coordinates, a date, a colour…"},{"t":"code","code":"kinshasa = (-4.32, 15.31)\nprint(kinshasa[0])\nprint(len(kinshasa))\nkinshasa[0] = 0","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"The error is deliberate: a tuple is **immutable**. That guarantee prevents accidental changes."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Unpacking\n\nThe tuple's superpower: you can **unpack** it into several variables at once."},{"t":"code","code":"point = (-4.32, 15.31)\nlatitude, longitude = point\nprint(\"Latitude:\", latitude)\nprint(\"Longitude:\", longitude)\n\n# Swap two variables in one line\na, b = 1, 2\na, b = b, a\nprint(a, b)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"You've already used unpacking without knowing it: in `for product, quantity in stock.items()`, each pair is an unpacked tuple."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## The set: zero duplicates\n\nA **set** is written between curly braces, without keys. It keeps **only one copy** of each value, and has no order."},{"t":"code","code":"customer_cities = [\"Dakar\", \"Paris\", \"Dakar\", \"Abidjan\", \"Paris\", \"Dakar\"]\nunique_cities = set(customer_cities)\nprint(unique_cities)\nprint(\"Number of different cities:\", len(unique_cities))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`len(set(list))` is the classic trick to count **distinct values**. A question an analyst asks every day: how many different customers? How many distinct products sold?"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Set operations\n\nSets can do the operations of Venn diagrams:"},{"t":"code","code":"january_customers = {\"Awa\", \"Kofi\", \"Grace\", \"Moussa\"}\nfebruary_customers = {\"Grace\", \"Moussa\", \"Inès\", \"David\"}\n\nprint(\"Came both months:\", january_customers & february_customers)\nprint(\"All customers:\", january_customers | february_customers)\nprint(\"Lost in February:\", january_customers - february_customers)","run":true},{"t":"quiz","q":"Which type would you choose to store the **different countries** a website's visitors come from?","options":["A list","A tuple","A set"],"answer":2,"explain":"A set automatically removes duplicates: each country appears only once."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Summary of structures\n\n| Type | Syntax | Ordered | Changeable | Duplicates |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| list | `[1, 2, 3]` | yes | yes | yes |\n| tuple | `(1, 2, 3)` | yes | no | yes |\n| set | `{1, 2, 3}` | no | yes | no |\n| dictionary | `{\"a\": 1}` | yes | yes | unique keys |"},{"t":"exercise","id":"tuples-ensembles-ex1","title":"Loyal customers","prompt":"Two lists hold the names of a café's customers in May and in June (with duplicates, since a customer can come several times).\n\nCalculate:\n\n- `may_count`: the number of **different** customers in May;\n- `loyal`: the **set** of customers who came in May **and** in June.","starter":"may = [\"Awa\", \"Kofi\", \"Awa\", \"Inès\", \"Moussa\", \"Kofi\", \"Awa\"]\njune = [\"Inès\", \"David\", \"Awa\", \"Inès\", \"Rachel\"]\n\nmay_count = \nloyal = \n\nprint(may_count)\nprint(loyal)","solution":"may = [\"Awa\", \"Kofi\", \"Awa\", \"Inès\", \"Moussa\", \"Kofi\", \"Awa\"]\njune = [\"Inès\", \"David\", \"Awa\", \"Inès\", \"Rachel\"]\n\nmay_count = len(set(may))\nloyal = set(may) & set(june)\n\nprint(may_count)\nprint(loyal)","check":"assert may_count == 4, f\"There are 4 different customers in May (Awa, Kofi, Inès, Moussa), you count {may_count}.\"\nassert isinstance(loyal, set), \"loyal must be a set.\"\nassert loyal == {\"Awa\", \"Inès\"}, f\"The customers who came both months are Awa and Inès, you get {loyal}.\"","hint":"Turn each list into a set with set(...). len() gives the number of items, & gives the intersection.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"comprehensions","title":"List comprehensions","subtitle":"Transform and filter in a single line.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## A loop in one line\n\nYou've often written this pattern: create an empty list, fill it in a loop."},{"t":"code","code":"prices = [1000, 2500, 400, 1200]\nwith_tax = []\nfor p in prices:\n    with_tax.append(p * 1.18)\nprint(with_tax)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Python offers a very elegant shortcut, the **list comprehension**:"},{"t":"code","code":"prices = [1000, 2500, 400, 1200]\nwith_tax = [p * 1.18 for p in prices]\nprint(with_tax)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"It reads almost like a sentence: \"`p * 1.18` **for each** `p` **in** `prices`\"."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Filtering with if\n\nYou can add a condition at the end to keep only some items."},{"t":"code","code":"temperatures = [28, 31, 35, 30, 27, 33, 36]\nhot = [t for t in temperatures if t > 32]\nprint(hot)\n\nnames = [\"Awa\", \"Kofi\", \"Amina\", \"Jean\", \"Aïcha\"]\nstarts_with_a = [n for n in names if n.startswith(\"A\")]\nprint(starts_with_a)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"The general structure: `[expression for item in sequence if condition]`."},{"t":"quiz","code":"numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\nprint([n * 10 for n in numbers if n % 2 == 0])","q":"Que va afficher ce code ?","options":["[10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60]","[20, 40, 60]","[2, 4, 6]"],"answer":1,"explain":"We keep only the even numbers (2, 4, 6), then multiply them by 10."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Two precious tools: enumerate and zip\n\n`enumerate` gives the position **and** the item. Handy for numbering a ranking:"},{"t":"code","code":"podium = [\"Gloire\", \"Awa\", \"Kofi\"]\nfor rank, name in enumerate(podium, start=1):\n    print(f\"{rank}. {name}\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`zip` goes through two lists **side by side**, item by item:"},{"t":"code","code":"products = [\"Espresso\", \"Croissant\", \"Sandwich\"]\nprices = [2.5, 1.8, 5.0]\nfor product, p in zip(products, prices):\n    print(f\"{product}: {p} euros\")\n\nmenu = dict(zip(products, prices))\nprint(menu)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Dictionary comprehensions\n\nSame principle with curly braces and a `key: value` pair:"},{"t":"code","code":"prices_eur = {\"Espresso\": 2.5, \"Croissant\": 1.8, \"Sandwich\": 5.0}\nprices_cfa = {product: round(p * 655.96) for product, p in prices_eur.items()}\nprint(prices_cfa)","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"","v":"Comprehensions are handy, but readability comes first. If your comprehension gets too long, a classic loop is often clearer."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercises"},{"t":"exercise","id":"comprehensions-ex1","title":"Converting temperatures","prompt":"The list `celsius` holds temperatures in degrees Celsius. With **a list comprehension**, create `fahrenheit` holding the same temperatures in degrees Fahrenheit, rounded to 1 decimal place.\n\nFormula: `F = C × 9 / 5 + 32`","starter":"celsius = [0, 12.5, 25, 31, 37, 100]\n\nfahrenheit = \nprint(fahrenheit)","solution":"celsius = [0, 12.5, 25, 31, 37, 100]\n\nfahrenheit = [round(c * 9 / 5 + 32, 1) for c in celsius]\nprint(fahrenheit)","check":"assert \"for\" in CODE and \"[\" in CODE.split(\"fahrenheit =\")[1], \"Use a list comprehension: [... for c in celsius].\"\nassert fahrenheit == [32.0, 54.5, 77.0, 87.8, 98.6, 212.0], f\"Expected result: [32.0, 54.5, 77.0, 87.8, 98.6, 212.0]. Got: {fahrenheit}\"","hint":"[round(c * 9 / 5 + 32, 1) for c in celsius]","inputs":""},{"t":"exercise","id":"comprehensions-ex2","title":"Big orders","prompt":"With a list comprehension and a filter, create `big`: the list of **amounts** of the orders over 100 euros (strictly), **after a 10% discount** (multiply by 0.9).","starter":"amounts = [45, 120, 300, 80, 150, 99, 101]\n\nbig = \nprint(big)","solution":"amounts = [45, 120, 300, 80, 150, 99, 101]\n\nbig = [a * 0.9 for a in amounts if a > 100]\nprint(big)","check":"expected = [a * 0.9 for a in [45, 120, 300, 80, 150, 99, 101] if a > 100]\nassert [round(x, 6) for x in big] == [round(x, 6) for x in expected], f\"Expected: {expected}. Got: {big}\"","hint":"[a * 0.9 for a in amounts if a > 100]","inputs":""}]]}]},{"id":"fonctions","icon":"🧩","title":"Functions, modules and files","desc":"Write reusable code, use libraries and read files.","badge":"Architect","lessons":[{"id":"fonctions","title":"Creating your own functions","subtitle":"def, parameters and return.","duration":15,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Why functions?\n\nYou already use functions: `print()`, `len()`, `round()`, `sum()`… Someone wrote them once, and everyone reuses them.\n\nNow you'll create **your own**. A function is a piece of code given a name, so that you can:\n\n- **avoid repeating yourself**: write the code once, use it a hundred times;\n- **organize**: a big program becomes a series of small, clear functions;\n- **fix things more easily**: a bug is fixed in one single place."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Defining and calling\n\nYou **define** a function with `def`, its name, parentheses and a colon. The body is indented."},{"t":"code","code":"def welcome():\n    print(\"Hello!\")\n    print(\"Welcome to the Baobab Café.\")\n\nwelcome()\nwelcome()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Defining a function doesn't run it: you're teaching it a skill. Then you **call** it by writing its name followed by parentheses. Here, two calls, so two welcomes."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Parameters\n\n**Parameters** are the function's inputs: variables that receive the values given when it's called."},{"t":"code","code":"def welcome(name, city):\n    print(f\"Hello {name}, welcome to the {city} café!\")\n\nwelcome(\"Awa\", \"Dakar\")\nwelcome(\"Gloire\", \"Kinshasa\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"In the first call, `name` is `\"Awa\"` and `city` is `\"Dakar\"`. In the second, other values. Same code, different results."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## return: giving back a result\n\n`print` **displays** a value. `return` **gives back** a value to the code that called the function, so it can use it: store it in a variable, do a calculation…"},{"t":"code","code":"def price_with_tax(price, rate=0.18):\n    return price * (1 + rate)\n\na = price_with_tax(1000)\nb = price_with_tax(2500)\nprint(a + b)\nprint(round(price_with_tax(99.99), 2))","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"`return` ends the function immediately: the following lines of the block don't run."},{"t":"quiz","q":"What is the difference between `print` and `return` in a function?","options":["None, it's the same","`print` displays on screen; `return` gives back a value the program can reuse","`return` displays in bigger letters"],"answer":1,"explain":"A function that only does `print` returns nothing (actually, it returns `None`). You can't reuse its result in a calculation. Useful functions almost always give back their result with `return`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## print or return? The proof"},{"t":"code","code":"def double_print(x):\n    print(x * 2)\n\ndef double_return(x):\n    return x * 2\n\nresult1 = double_print(5)\nresult2 = double_return(5)\nprint(\"result1 =\", result1)\nprint(\"result2 =\", result2)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`double_print` displays 10, but `result1` is `None` (nothing). `double_return` displays nothing, but `result2` is indeed 10."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## A function can contain logic\n\nA function can contain conditions, loops, and several `return`s."},{"t":"code","code":"def grade_label(grade):\n    if grade >= 16:\n        return \"Excellent\"\n    elif grade >= 14:\n        return \"Very good\"\n    elif grade >= 12:\n        return \"Good\"\n    elif grade >= 10:\n        return \"Pass\"\n    return \"Fail\"\n\nfor g in [17, 13, 8, 10]:\n    print(g, \"→\", grade_label(g))","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercises"},{"t":"exercise","id":"fonctions-ex1","title":"Area of a plot","prompt":"Write a function `area(length, width)` that **returns** (with `return`) the area of a rectangular plot of land.\n\nThen display the area of a 25 m by 18 m plot.","starter":"def area(length, width):\n    ...\n\nprint(area(25, 18))","solution":"def area(length, width):\n    return length * width\n\nprint(area(25, 18))","check":"assert \"area\" in dir() and callable(area), \"Define a function named area.\"\nassert area(25, 18) == 450, f\"area(25, 18) should return 450, it returns {area(25, 18)}.\"\nassert area(3, 4) == 12, \"area(3, 4) should return 12.\"\nassert \"450\" in OUT, \"Display the result of area(25, 18).\"","hint":"Replace the ... with return length * width","inputs":""},{"t":"exercise","id":"fonctions-ex2","title":"Is it an even number?","prompt":"Write a function `is_even(n)` that returns `True` if `n` is even, `False` otherwise.\n\nThen use it in a list comprehension to create `evens`, the list of even numbers in `numbers`.","starter":"def is_even(n):\n    ...\n\nnumbers = [3, 8, 15, 22, 41, 60]\nevens = \nprint(evens)","solution":"def is_even(n):\n    return n % 2 == 0\n\nnumbers = [3, 8, 15, 22, 41, 60]\nevens = [n for n in numbers if is_even(n)]\nprint(evens)","check":"assert is_even(4) is True and is_even(7) is False and is_even(0) is True, \"is_even must return True for even numbers and False for odd ones.\"\nassert evens == [8, 22, 60], f\"evens should be [8, 22, 60], you get {evens}.\"\nassert \"is_even(\" in CODE.split(\"evens =\")[1], \"Use your is_even function in the list comprehension.\"","hint":"return n % 2 == 0, then [n for n in numbers if is_even(n)]","inputs":""},{"t":"exercise","id":"fonctions-ex3","title":"The taxi fare","prompt":"A taxi charges **500 francs pick-up fee**, plus **250 francs per kilometre**. At night, the total price is **increased by 20%**.\n\nWrite a function `fare(km, night)` that returns the price, where `night` is `True` or `False`.","starter":"def fare(km, night):\n    ...\n\nprint(fare(10, False))\nprint(fare(10, True))","solution":"def fare(km, night):\n    price = 500 + 250 * km\n    if night:\n        price = price * 1.2\n    return price\n\nprint(fare(10, False))\nprint(fare(10, True))","check":"assert fare(10, False) == 3000, f\"For 10 km by day: 500 + 2500 = 3000. Your function returns {fare(10, False)}.\"\nassert abs(fare(10, True) - 3600) < 1e-6, f\"For 10 km at night: 3000 × 1.2 = 3600. Your function returns {fare(10, True)}.\"\nassert fare(0, False) == 500, \"For 0 km, the 500-franc pick-up fee remains.\"","hint":"First calculate price = 500 + 250 * km. Then, if night is true, multiply by 1.2. Finally, return price.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"fonctions-plus","title":"Functions, continued","subtitle":"Default values, several results, scope and documentation.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Default parameters\n\nA parameter can have a **default value**, used when it isn't given in the call."},{"t":"code","code":"def convert(amount, rate=655.957):\n    return round(amount * rate)\n\nprint(convert(10))             # default rate\nprint(convert(10, 600))        # rate given\nprint(convert(rate=2.0, amount=5))  # by name, in any order","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Calls **by name** (`rate=2.0`) make code easier to read when a function has many parameters. You'll see them everywhere in pandas."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Returning several values\n\nA function can return several values at once, separated by commas. Actually, it returns a **tuple**, which you unpack on arrival."},{"t":"code","code":"def summary(values):\n    return min(values), max(values), sum(values) / len(values)\n\nsales = [120, 95, 210, 180, 75]\nlowest, highest, mean = summary(sales)\nprint(f\"min {lowest}, max {highest}, mean {mean}\")","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Variable scope\n\nA variable created **inside** a function only exists in that function. We say it is **local**."},{"t":"code","code":"def calculation():\n    secret = 42\n    return secret * 2\n\nprint(calculation())\nprint(secret)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`NameError`: outside, `secret` doesn't exist. It's a protection: each function has its own space, and can't overwrite the rest of the program's variables. To get a value out, use `return`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Documenting with a docstring\n\nA **docstring** is a text between triple quotes placed just below the `def`. It explains what the function does. It's an excellent habit."},{"t":"code","code":"def bmi(weight, height):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the body mass index.\n\n    weight: in kilograms\n    height: in metres\n    \"\"\"\n    return round(weight / height ** 2, 1)\n\nprint(bmi(70, 1.75))\nhelp(bmi)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Lambda functions\n\nA **lambda** is a tiny nameless function, written on one line. It's mostly used to tell `sorted` (or pandas) **what to sort by**."},{"t":"code","code":"products = [(\"Espresso\", 2.5), (\"Sandwich\", 5.0), (\"Doughnut\", 1.0)]\nby_price = sorted(products, key=lambda p: p[1])\nprint(by_price)\n\nstudents = [{\"name\": \"Awa\", \"grade\": 15}, {\"name\": \"Kofi\", \"grade\": 12}, {\"name\": \"Gloire\", \"grade\": 17}]\nranking = sorted(students, key=lambda s: s[\"grade\"], reverse=True)\nprint([s[\"name\"] for s in ranking])","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`lambda p: p[1]` means: \"from `p`, return `p[1]`\", that is, the price."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercises"},{"t":"exercise","id":"fonctions-plus-ex1","title":"Statistics","prompt":"Write a function `statistics(values)` that returns **three values**: the minimum, the maximum and the mean **rounded to 1 decimal place**.","starter":"def statistics(values):\n    ...\n\nlowest, highest, mean = statistics([12, 18, 7, 15, 10])\nprint(lowest, highest, mean)","solution":"def statistics(values):\n    mean = round(sum(values) / len(values), 1)\n    return min(values), max(values), mean\n\nlowest, highest, mean = statistics([12, 18, 7, 15, 10])\nprint(lowest, highest, mean)","check":"r = statistics([12, 18, 7, 15, 10])\nassert isinstance(r, tuple) and len(r) == 3, \"The function must return three values separated by commas.\"\nassert r == (7, 18, 12.4), f\"Expected (7, 18, 12.4), got {r}.\"\nassert statistics([1, 2]) == (1, 2, 1.5), \"Test your function with other values: statistics([1, 2]) must give (1, 2, 1.5).\"","hint":"return min(values), max(values), round(sum(values) / len(values), 1)","inputs":""},{"t":"exercise","id":"fonctions-plus-ex2","title":"The discount","prompt":"Write a function `apply_discount(price, percent=10)` that returns the price after the discount, rounded to 2 decimal places. By default, the discount is 10%.\n\nExamples: `apply_discount(50)` returns `45.0`; `apply_discount(80, 25)` returns `60.0`.","starter":"def apply_discount(price, percent=10):\n    ...\n\nprint(apply_discount(50))\nprint(apply_discount(80, 25))","solution":"def apply_discount(price, percent=10):\n    return round(price * (1 - percent / 100), 2)\n\nprint(apply_discount(50))\nprint(apply_discount(80, 25))","check":"assert apply_discount(50) == 45.0, f\"apply_discount(50) should be 45.0, got {apply_discount(50)}.\"\nassert apply_discount(80, 25) == 60.0, f\"apply_discount(80, 25) should be 60.0, got {apply_discount(80, 25)}.\"\nassert apply_discount(19.99, 15) == 16.99, f\"apply_discount(19.99, 15) should be 16.99, got {apply_discount(19.99, 15)}.\"","hint":"A 10% discount means multiplying by (1 - 10 / 100), that is 0.9.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"modules","title":"Modules and libraries","subtitle":"Use other people's code to go much faster.","duration":12,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Don't reinvent the wheel\n\nPython comes with hundreds of **modules**: files full of ready-made functions, organized by theme. You load them with `import`."},{"t":"code","code":"import math\n\nprint(math.sqrt(144))      # square root\nprint(math.pi)\nprint(math.ceil(4.1))      # round up\nprint(math.floor(4.9))     # round down","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"You write the module name, a dot, then the function: `math.sqrt`. Like an address: \"in the math module, the sqrt function\"."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Three ways to import"},{"t":"code","code":"import statistics\nprint(statistics.mean([12, 15, 9]))\n\nfrom statistics import median\nprint(median([12, 15, 9, 20]))\n\nimport statistics as st\nprint(st.stdev([12, 15, 9, 20]))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"- `import module`: you write `module.function`;\n- `from module import function`: you write `function` directly;\n- `import module as alias`: a shorter nickname.\n\nThe third form is the one you'll see everywhere in data: `import pandas as pd`, `import numpy as np`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Randomness with random"},{"t":"code","code":"import random\n\nrandom.seed(7)  # makes randomness reproducible\nprint(random.randint(1, 6))               # a die\nprint(random.choice([\"heads\", \"tails\"]))  # a random choice\nteam = [\"Awa\", \"Kofi\", \"Inès\", \"David\"]\nrandom.shuffle(team)\nprint(team)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`random.seed()` sets the starting point of the randomness: with the same seed, you always get the same draws. Essential for an analysis to be **reproducible**."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Dates with datetime"},{"t":"code","code":"from datetime import date\n\ntoday = date.today()\nprint(today)\nindependence = date(1960, 6, 30)\nprint(independence.year)\ngap = today - independence\nprint(f\"{gap.days} days since 30 June 1960\")\nprint(independence.strftime(\"%d/%m/%Y\"))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Subtracting two dates gives a duration, whose number of days you read with `.days`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## What about external libraries?\n\nBeyond the modules shipped with Python, there are hundreds of thousands of **libraries** created by the community. You install them with the **pip** tool. On your computer, in a terminal:"},{"t":"code","code":"pip install pandas matplotlib","run":false,"lang":"bash"},{"t":"md","v":"On this site, pandas, NumPy and Matplotlib are already available: you'll use them in the next chapter."},{"t":"quiz","q":"What does `import pandas as pd` do?","options":["It installs pandas","It loads the pandas library and gives it the nickname `pd`","It creates a variable pd equal to \"pandas\""],"answer":1,"explain":"`import` loads an already installed library (installing is done with pip). `as pd` then lets you write `pd.read_csv(...)` instead of `pandas.read_csv(...)`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: rolling dice"},{"t":"exercise","id":"modules-ex1","title":"1000 dice rolls","prompt":"Simulate **1000 rolls** of a 6-sided die with `random.randint(1, 6)`, after setting the seed with `random.seed(42)`.\n\nCount in `sixes` the number of times the die lands on 6, then calculate `percentage` (the percentage of sixes, rounded to 1 decimal place).\n\nIn theory, you'd expect about 16.7%.","starter":"import random\n\nrandom.seed(42)\nsixes = 0\n\nprint(sixes, percentage)","solution":"import random\n\nrandom.seed(42)\nsixes = 0\nfor i in range(1000):\n    if random.randint(1, 6) == 6:\n        sixes += 1\npercentage = round(sixes / 1000 * 100, 1)\n\nprint(sixes, percentage)","check":"import random as _r\n_r.seed(42)\nexpected = sum(1 for _ in range(1000) if _r.randint(1, 6) == 6)\nassert sixes == expected, f\"With seed 42 and 1000 rolls, you get {expected} sixes. You count {sixes}. Did you call random.seed(42) before the loop, and randint only once per lap?\"\nassert percentage == round(expected / 10, 1), f\"The percentage should be {round(expected / 10, 1)}.\"","hint":"A for i in range(1000) loop, an if random.randint(1, 6) == 6, and a counter. The percentage: sixes / 1000 * 100.","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: days to go"},{"t":"exercise","id":"modules-ex2","title":"Countdown","prompt":"With the `datetime` module, calculate `days`: the number of days between **1 January 2026** and **14 July 2026**.","starter":"from datetime import date\n\ndays = \nprint(days)","solution":"from datetime import date\n\ndays = (date(2026, 7, 14) - date(2026, 1, 1)).days\nprint(days)","check":"assert days == 194, f\"There are 194 days between these two dates, you get {days}.\"\nassert \"date(\" in CODE, \"Use date(...) from the datetime module.\"","hint":"Create both dates with date(year, month, day), subtract them, then take .days","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"fichiers","title":"Files and error handling","subtitle":"Reading data, and not crashing at the first surprise.","duration":14,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Reading a text file\n\nData often lives in **files**. On this site, a few files are already there for you. Let's read `groceries.txt`, a shopping list:"},{"t":"code","code":"with open(\"groceries.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    content = file.read()\nprint(content)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"- `open(name)` opens the file;\n- `with ... as file:` guarantees the file will be **closed** properly at the end of the block;\n- `.read()` reads the whole content at once, as text."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Line by line\n\nOften, we process a file **line by line**:"},{"t":"code","code":"with open(\"groceries.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    for number, line in enumerate(file, start=1):\n        item = line.strip()\n        print(f\"{number}. {item.capitalize()}\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Each line read ends with an invisible line break (`\\n`). `.strip()` removes it."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Writing to a file\n\nWith the `\"w\"` (*write*) mode, you create (or overwrite) a file. The `\"a\"` (*append*) mode adds at the end."},{"t":"code","code":"with open(\"log.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"Monday: 120 sales\\n\")\n    f.write(\"Tuesday: 95 sales\\n\")\n\nwith open(\"log.txt\", \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    f.write(\"Wednesday: 210 sales\\n\")\n\nwith open(\"log.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    print(f.read())","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## CSV files\n\nThe **CSV** format (*Comma-Separated Values*) is the most widespread data format: a table as text, one line per record, commas between columns. You can open it in Excel.\n\nHere's the start of the `sales.csv` file, which holds the 2024 sales of a coffee shop chain:"},{"t":"code","code":"with open(\"sales.csv\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    for i in range(6):\n        print(f.readline().strip())","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"The `csv` module reads each line as a dictionary, with the column names as keys:"},{"t":"code","code":"import csv\n\ntotal_quantity = 0\nwith open(\"sales.csv\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        total_quantity += int(row[\"quantity\"])\nprint(\"Items sold in 2024:\", total_quantity)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Everything is read as text: hence the `int(...)`. In the next chapter, pandas will do all this (and much more) in one line."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Handling errors: try / except\n\nReal data is full of surprises: an empty cell, an \"N/A\", a typo. Rather than letting the program crash, you can **catch** the error with `try` / `except`."},{"t":"code","code":"values = [\"12\", \"7\", \"N/A\", \"15\", \"\", \"9\"]\ntotal = 0\nskipped = 0\nfor v in values:\n    try:\n        total += int(v)\n    except ValueError:\n        skipped += 1\nprint(f\"Total: {total} ({skipped} values skipped)\")","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Python **tries** the `try` block. If a `ValueError` happens, it runs the `except` block instead of crashing, and carries on."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"Only catch the errors you expect (here `ValueError`). A bare `except:` would also hide your real programming mistakes."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the shopping list"},{"t":"exercise","id":"fichiers-ex1","title":"Counting the items","prompt":"Read the file `groceries.txt` line by line and create the list `items` holding each item **in upper case**, without the line break.\n\nThen display the number of items.","starter":"items = []\n\nprint(len(items))","solution":"items = []\nwith open(\"groceries.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    for line in f:\n        items.append(line.strip().upper())\n\nprint(len(items))","check":"assert \"open(\" in CODE, \"Open the file with open().\"\nassert items == [\"RICE\", \"OIL\", \"TOMATOES\", \"ONIONS\", \"BREAD\", \"MILK\", \"COFFEE\"], f\"items should hold the 7 items in upper case. Got: {items}\"\nassert \"7\" in OUT, \"Display the number of items.\"","hint":"with open(\"groceries.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f: then for line in f: then items.append(line.strip().upper())","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: a safe conversion"},{"t":"exercise","id":"fichiers-ex2","title":"The careful converter","prompt":"Write a function `to_number(text)` that converts a text into a decimal number with `float()`. If the conversion fails, it returns `None` instead of crashing.\n\nThen calculate `total`: the sum of the values in `raw` that could be converted.","starter":"def to_number(text):\n    ...\n\nraw = [\"12.5\", \"abc\", \"7\", \"\", \"3.25\", \"N/A\"]\ntotal = \nprint(total)","solution":"def to_number(text):\n    try:\n        return float(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        return None\n\nraw = [\"12.5\", \"abc\", \"7\", \"\", \"3.25\", \"N/A\"]\ntotal = sum(x for x in (to_number(t) for t in raw) if x is not None)\nprint(total)","check":"assert to_number(\"3.5\") == 3.5, \"to_number(\\\"3.5\\\") must return 3.5.\"\nassert to_number(\"abc\") is None, \"to_number(\\\"abc\\\") must return None (and not crash).\"\nassert \"try\" in CODE and \"except\" in CODE, \"Use try / except in your function.\"\nassert abs(total - 22.75) < 1e-9, f\"The total of the valid values is 22.75, you get {total}.\"","hint":"In the function: try: return float(text), then except ValueError: return None. For the total, a loop that only adds the results that are not None.","inputs":""}]]}]},{"id":"data","icon":"📊","title":"Analyzing data","desc":"NumPy, pandas and matplotlib on real data, all the way to the final project.","badge":"Data analyst","data":true,"lessons":[{"id":"numpy","title":"NumPy, calculating on arrays","subtitle":"A calculation on a million numbers, in one line.","duration":14,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Welcome to data\n\nYou now know the basics of Python. Time for the data analyst's tools. The first one is called **NumPy** (*Numerical Python*). Almost every data library is built on it, pandas included.\n\nIts central object: the **array**, a sort of ultra-fast list of numbers."},{"t":"code","code":"import numpy as np\n\nprices = np.array([2.5, 3.5, 2.0, 2.2, 1.8])\nprint(prices)\nprint(type(prices))","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"info","title":"","v":"The first time, loading NumPy takes a few seconds. After that, it's instant."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Calculating on the whole array at once\n\nWith a list, raising every price by 10% needed a loop. With NumPy, the operation applies **to every item automatically**:"},{"t":"code","code":"import numpy as np\n\nprices = np.array([2.5, 3.5, 2.0, 2.2, 1.8])\nquantities = np.array([10, 4, 7, 12, 20])\n\nprint(prices * 1.1)\nprint(prices * quantities)\nprint(\"Revenue:\", (prices * quantities).sum())","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`prices * quantities` multiplies the items **pair by pair**: the first price by the first quantity, and so on. This is called **vectorization**. It's shorter to write, and hundreds of times faster on large volumes."},{"t":"code","code":"import numpy as np\n\nnumbers = list(range(1000000))\narray = np.arange(1000000)\nprint(sum(x * 2 for x in numbers))\nprint((array * 2).sum())","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Statistics in one line"},{"t":"code","code":"import numpy as np\n\ngrades = np.array([12, 15, 9, 17, 14, 11, 16, 8, 13, 18])\nprint(\"Mean:\", grades.mean())\nprint(\"Median:\", np.median(grades))\nprint(\"Standard deviation:\", round(grades.std(), 2))\nprint(\"Min / max:\", grades.min(), grades.max())","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"- The **mean** is the sum divided by the number of values.\n- The **median** is the middle value once sorted: half the values are below, half above. It resists extreme values better.\n- The **standard deviation** measures spread: small, the values are close to the mean; large, they are spread out."},{"t":"quiz","q":"In a company, 9 employees earn 400,000 francs a month and the director earns 8,000,000. Which indicator best describes the \"typical\" salary?","options":["The mean","The median","The maximum"],"answer":1,"explain":"The mean (1,160,000) is pulled up by one extreme salary. The median (400,000) reflects the situation of the majority well. An essential analyst reflex!"}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Filtering with a mask\n\nA comparison on an array gives an array of `True` / `False`, called a **mask**. You put it between square brackets to **filter**."},{"t":"code","code":"import numpy as np\n\ntemperatures = np.array([28, 31, 35, 30, 27, 33, 36])\nmask = temperatures > 32\nprint(mask)\nprint(temperatures[mask])\nprint(\"Hot days:\", mask.sum())","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`mask.sum()` counts the `True`s (each `True` counts as 1). This filtering technique is **exactly** the one pandas uses. Remember it well."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: converting prices"},{"t":"exercise","id":"numpy-ex1","title":"Prices in CFA francs","prompt":"The array `prices_eur` holds prices in euros. Without a loop, create:\n\n- `prices_cfa`: the prices converted into CFA francs (1 euro = 655.957 francs), rounded to the unit with `np.round`;\n- `expensive`: the number of prices above 2000 CFA francs.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\nprices_eur = np.array([2.5, 3.5, 2.0, 2.2, 1.8, 1.0, 5.0])\n\nprices_cfa = \nexpensive = \nprint(prices_cfa)\nprint(expensive)","solution":"import numpy as np\n\nprices_eur = np.array([2.5, 3.5, 2.0, 2.2, 1.8, 1.0, 5.0])\n\nprices_cfa = np.round(prices_eur * 655.957)\nexpensive = (prices_cfa > 2000).sum()\nprint(prices_cfa)\nprint(expensive)","check":"import numpy as _np\nassert \"for\" not in CODE, \"No loop: let NumPy calculate on the whole array.\"\nexpected = _np.round(_np.array([2.5, 3.5, 2.0, 2.2, 1.8, 1.0, 5.0]) * 655.957)\nassert _np.allclose(prices_cfa, expected), f\"Expected prices_cfa: {expected}\"\nassert int(expensive) == 2, f\"There are 2 prices above 2000 CFA francs, you count {expensive}.\"","hint":"np.round(prices_eur * 655.957), then (prices_cfa > 2000).sum()","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"pandas","title":"Pandas, your spreadsheet in Python","subtitle":"Load, explore and understand a data file.","duration":15,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## The DataFrame\n\n**Pandas** is THE data analysis library in Python. Its main object, the **DataFrame**, is a table with named rows and columns. Like an Excel sheet, but driven by code."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"product\": [\"Espresso\", \"Croissant\", \"Sandwich\"],\n    \"price\": [2.5, 1.8, 5.0],\n    \"stock\": [120, 40, 15],\n})\ndf","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Notice: no `print` here. When the **last line** of a block is a value, it's displayed automatically, and pandas shows it as a nice table. That's how Jupyter notebooks work, the analysts' favourite tool."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Loading a real file\n\nIn practice, you don't type data in: you **load** it. `pd.read_csv` reads a CSV file and creates a DataFrame. Here are the 2024 sales of a coffee shop chain present in five cities."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\n\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales.head()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`head()` shows the first 5 rows (`head(10)` for 10). `tail()` shows the last ones. They're the first things you look at with a new file."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## The first questions to ask\n\nWith a new dataset, an analyst always checks:"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nprint(\"Shape:\", sales.shape)            # (rows, columns)\nprint(\"Columns:\", list(sales.columns))\nprint()\nprint(sales.dtypes)                     # type of each column","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`shape` gives the number of rows and columns. `dtypes` gives each column's type: `int64` (integers), `float64` (decimals), `object` (text)."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales.info()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`info()` sums everything up: number of rows, columns, types, and the number of **non-missing** values per column."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## A column = a Series\n\nYou get a column with square brackets and its name. It's a **Series**: a NumPy array with labels. Everything you know about NumPy applies."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nq = sales[\"quantity\"]\nprint(\"Total sold:\", q.sum())\nprint(\"Average per sale:\", round(q.mean(), 2))\nprint(\"Biggest sale:\", q.max())","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"And `describe()` calculates the main statistics of every numeric column at once:"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales.describe()","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Counting values\n\nFor a text column, the natural question is: how many times does each value appear? That's what `value_counts()` is for."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"city\"].value_counts()","run":true},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nprint(\"Different products:\", sales[\"product\"].nunique())\nsales[\"product\"].value_counts(normalize=True).round(3)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`nunique()` counts the distinct values. `normalize=True` gives proportions rather than counts."},{"t":"quiz","q":"You receive a new data file. Which instruction shows its first rows?","options":["`df.shape`","`df.head()`","`df.describe()`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`head()` shows the first rows. `shape` gives the dimensions, `describe()` the statistics."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the students"},{"t":"exercise","id":"pandas-ex1","title":"First exploration","prompt":"Load the file `students.csv` into a DataFrame named `df`. Then calculate:\n\n- `n_students`: the number of rows;\n- `python_mean`: the mean of the `python_grade` column, rounded to 2 decimal places;\n- `top_city`: the city with the most students (tip: `value_counts()` sorts from largest to smallest, and `.index[0]` gives the first label).","starter":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = \n\nn_students = \npython_mean = \ntop_city = \n\nprint(n_students, python_mean, top_city)","solution":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"students.csv\")\n\nn_students = len(df)\npython_mean = round(df[\"python_grade\"].mean(), 2)\ntop_city = df[\"city\"].value_counts().index[0]\n\nprint(n_students, python_mean, top_city)","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_ref = _pd.read_csv(\"students.csv\")\nassert isinstance(df, _pd.DataFrame), \"df must be a DataFrame created with pd.read_csv(\\\"students.csv\\\").\"\nassert n_students == len(_ref), f\"There are {len(_ref)} students, you count {n_students}.\"\nassert python_mean == round(_ref[\"python_grade\"].mean(), 2), f\"The expected mean is {round(_ref['python_grade'].mean(), 2)}.\"\nassert top_city == _ref[\"city\"].value_counts().index[0], f\"The most represented city is {_ref['city'].value_counts().index[0]}.\"","hint":"pd.read_csv(\"students.csv\"); len(df) or df.shape[0]; round(df[\"python_grade\"].mean(), 2)","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"selection","title":"Selecting, filtering, sorting","subtitle":"Ask your data precise questions.","duration":15,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Choosing columns\n\nOne column: single square brackets. Several columns: a **list** of names inside the brackets (hence the double brackets)."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nsales[[\"date\", \"city\", \"product\"]].head()","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Filtering rows\n\nThis is the **mask** technique seen with NumPy. The comparison gives a Series of `True` / `False`, and you put it between brackets to keep the `True` rows."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nin_dakar = sales[sales[\"city\"] == \"Dakar\"]\nprint(len(in_dakar), \"sales in Dakar\")\nin_dakar.head()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Read it like a sentence: \"the sales **where** the city **is equal to** Dakar\"."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nbig = sales[sales[\"quantity\"] >= 15]\nbig.head()","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Several conditions\n\nTo combine conditions, pandas uses `&` (and), `|` (or) and `~` (not), **with parentheses around each condition**."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nf = (sales[\"city\"] == \"Kinshasa\") & (sales[\"category\"] == \"Pastry\")\nsales[f].head()","run":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"In pandas, you don't use `and` / `or` but `&` / `|`. And the parentheses around each condition are mandatory. Forgetting either is the most common beginner mistake."},{"t":"md","v":"To test membership in a list of values, `isin` is much handier than a chain of `|`:"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nafrica = sales[sales[\"city\"].isin([\"Kinshasa\", \"Abidjan\", \"Dakar\"])]\nprint(len(africa), \"sales in Africa out of\", len(sales))","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Sorting\n\n`sort_values` sorts by one or more columns. By default, from smallest to largest; `ascending=False` reverses the order."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nsales.sort_values(\"quantity\", ascending=False).head(5)","run":true},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nsales.sort_values([\"city\", \"quantity\"], ascending=[True, False]).head(8)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`nlargest(n, column)` is a shortcut for \"the n largest values\"."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## loc: rows and columns at the same time\n\n`df.loc[rows, columns]` selects rows (often with a filter) **and** columns in one go:"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nsales.loc[sales[\"product\"] == \"Bissap juice\", [\"date\", \"city\", \"quantity\"]].head()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`iloc` does the same with numeric **positions**: `sales.iloc[0:3, 0:2]` gives the first 3 rows and the first 2 columns."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Which line selects the Paris sales of more than 10 items?","options":["`sales[sales[\"city\"] == \"Paris\" and sales[\"quantity\"] > 10]`","`sales[(sales[\"city\"] == \"Paris\") & (sales[\"quantity\"] > 10)]`","`sales[\"Paris\", \"quantity\" > 10]`"],"answer":1,"explain":"With pandas: `&` instead of `and`, and parentheses around each condition."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercises"},{"t":"exercise","id":"selection-ex1","title":"The top students","prompt":"From `students.csv`:\n\n1. create `excellent`: the students with **at least 15** in `python_grade`, sorted from best to worst;\n2. create `hardworking_abidjan`: the students from **Abidjan** who studied **more than 10 hours** (`study_hours > 10`).","starter":"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"students.csv\")\n\nexcellent = \nhardworking_abidjan = \n\nexcellent","solution":"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"students.csv\")\n\nexcellent = df[df[\"python_grade\"] >= 15].sort_values(\"python_grade\", ascending=False)\nhardworking_abidjan = df[(df[\"city\"] == \"Abidjan\") & (df[\"study_hours\"] > 10)]\n\nexcellent","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_d = _pd.read_csv(\"students.csv\")\n_e = _d[_d[\"python_grade\"] >= 15].sort_values(\"python_grade\", ascending=False)\nassert isinstance(excellent, _pd.DataFrame), \"excellent must be a filtered DataFrame.\"\nassert list(excellent[\"name\"]) == list(_e[\"name\"]), \"excellent must contain the students with python_grade >= 15, from best to worst.\"\n_a = _d[(_d[\"city\"] == \"Abidjan\") & (_d[\"study_hours\"] > 10)]\nassert set(hardworking_abidjan[\"name\"]) == set(_a[\"name\"]) and len(hardworking_abidjan) == len(_a), f\"hardworking_abidjan should contain {len(_a)} student(s): {list(_a['name'])}.\"","hint":"df[df[\"python_grade\"] >= 15].sort_values(\"python_grade\", ascending=False). For the second: two conditions in parentheses, joined with &.","inputs":""},{"t":"exercise","id":"selection-ex2","title":"The biggest bissap sale","prompt":"In `sales.csv`, find the **Bissap juice** sale with the largest quantity. Store its **date** in `record_date` and its **city** in `record_city`.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nbissap = \nbest = \nrecord_date = \nrecord_city = \nprint(record_date, record_city)","solution":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nbissap = sales[sales[\"product\"] == \"Bissap juice\"]\nbest = bissap.sort_values(\"quantity\", ascending=False).iloc[0]\nrecord_date = best[\"date\"]\nrecord_city = best[\"city\"]\nprint(record_date, record_city)","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n_b = _v[_v[\"product\"] == \"Bissap juice\"]\n_max = _b[\"quantity\"].max()\n_ok = _b[_b[\"quantity\"] == _max]\nassert any((r[\"date\"] == record_date and r[\"city\"] == record_city) for _, r in _ok.iterrows()), f\"The biggest bissap sale is {_max} units. Check your date and your city.\"","hint":"Filter the product's rows, sort by quantity in descending order, then .iloc[0] gives the first row. You then read its columns with [\"date\"] and [\"city\"].","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"nettoyage","title":"Cleaning data","subtitle":"Missing values, duplicates and typos.","duration":16,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## 80% of the work\n\nPeople often say an analyst spends 80% of their time **cleaning** data. Real files are typed in by humans, and they contain gaps, duplicates and inconsistencies.\n\nHere is `customers.csv`, a shop's customer file. Look closely:"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\ncustomers = pd.read_csv(\"customers.csv\")\ncustomers.head(12)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"You'll notice `NaN` cells (*Not a Number*): these are **missing values**. And the `city` column looks… odd."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Spotting missing values"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\ncustomers = pd.read_csv(\"customers.csv\")\n\nprint(customers.isna().sum())\nprint()\nprint(\"Complete rows:\", len(customers.dropna()), \"out of\", len(customers))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`isna()` returns `True` for each empty cell; `.sum()` counts them per column. Three possible strategies:\n\n- **remove** incomplete rows with `dropna()` (simple, but you lose data);\n- **fill** the gaps with `fillna(value)`, for example the column's median;\n- **keep** the gaps, if the analysis allows it (most pandas calculations ignore them)."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\ncustomers = pd.read_csv(\"customers.csv\")\n\nmedian = customers[\"age\"].median()\ncustomers[\"age\"] = customers[\"age\"].fillna(median)\nprint(\"Missing ages left:\", customers[\"age\"].isna().sum())","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Duplicates\n\nThe same row entered twice skews every count."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\ncustomers = pd.read_csv(\"customers.csv\")\n\nprint(\"Duplicates:\", customers.duplicated().sum())\ncustomers = customers.drop_duplicates()\nprint(\"After cleaning:\", len(customers), \"rows\")","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Harmonizing text\n\nHow many different cities are there?"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\ncustomers = pd.read_csv(\"customers.csv\")\ncustomers[\"city\"].value_counts()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"To pandas, `\"Kinshasa\"`, `\" kinshasa\"` and `\"KINSHASA\"` are three different cities! The string methods from chapter 3 apply to a whole column through `.str`:"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\ncustomers = pd.read_csv(\"customers.csv\")\n\ncustomers[\"city\"] = customers[\"city\"].str.strip().str.title()\ncustomers[\"city\"].value_counts()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Five cities, just like in reality."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## The right types\n\nDates are read as text. `pd.to_datetime` turns them into real dates, which lets you extract the year, the month or the day of the week:"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\ncustomers = pd.read_csv(\"customers.csv\")\n\ncustomers[\"signup_date\"] = pd.to_datetime(customers[\"signup_date\"])\ncustomers[\"year\"] = customers[\"signup_date\"].dt.year\ncustomers[\"month\"] = customers[\"signup_date\"].dt.month\ncustomers[[\"signup_date\", \"year\", \"month\"]].head()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"You've also just seen how to **create a column**: `df[\"new\"] = ...`. You can put a calculation on other columns in it, just as with NumPy."},{"t":"quiz","q":"Which instruction turns `\"  dakar \"` into `\"Dakar\"` in the whole `city` column?","options":["`customers[\"city\"].strip().title()`","`customers[\"city\"].str.strip().str.title()`","`customers.title(\"city\")`"],"answer":1,"explain":"On a pandas column, string methods go through `.str`. Without it, Python looks for a `strip` method on the Series itself, which doesn't exist."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: the big clean-up"},{"t":"exercise","id":"nettoyage-ex1","title":"Cleaning customers.csv","prompt":"Create a DataFrame `clean` from `customers.csv`, applying in this order:\n\n1. removal of duplicates;\n2. harmonization of the `city` column (spaces removed, capital letter at the start);\n3. replacement of missing ages with the **median** age (calculated after removing duplicates);\n4. removal of the rows whose `spending` is missing.\n\nThen display the number of rows and the number of distinct cities.","starter":"import pandas as pd\n\nclean = pd.read_csv(\"customers.csv\")\n# 1.\n# 2.\n# 3.\n# 4.\n\nprint(len(clean), clean[\"city\"].nunique())","solution":"import pandas as pd\n\nclean = pd.read_csv(\"customers.csv\")\nclean = clean.drop_duplicates()\nclean[\"city\"] = clean[\"city\"].str.strip().str.title()\nclean[\"age\"] = clean[\"age\"].fillna(clean[\"age\"].median())\nclean = clean.dropna(subset=[\"spending\"])\n\nprint(len(clean), clean[\"city\"].nunique())","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_c = _pd.read_csv(\"customers.csv\").drop_duplicates()\n_c[\"city\"] = _c[\"city\"].str.strip().str.title()\n_c[\"age\"] = _c[\"age\"].fillna(_c[\"age\"].median())\n_c = _c.dropna(subset=[\"spending\"])\nassert clean.duplicated().sum() == 0, \"There are still duplicates: use drop_duplicates().\"\nassert clean[\"city\"].nunique() == 5, f\"There should be 5 distinct cities left, there are {clean['city'].nunique()}: {sorted(clean['city'].unique())}\"\nassert clean[\"age\"].isna().sum() == 0, \"There are still missing ages: fill them with fillna.\"\nassert clean[\"spending\"].isna().sum() == 0, \"There are still missing spending values: remove those rows with dropna(subset=[\\\"spending\\\"]).\"\nassert len(clean) == len(_c), f\"The cleaned table should have {len(_c)} rows, yours has {len(clean)}.\"\nassert abs(clean[\"age\"].sum() - _c[\"age\"].sum()) < 1e-6, \"Missing ages must be replaced by the median calculated after removing duplicates.\"","hint":"drop_duplicates(); .str.strip().str.title(); fillna(clean[\"age\"].median()); dropna(subset=[\"spending\"]) to look at that column only.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"groupby","title":"Grouping and summarizing","subtitle":"groupby, for questions \"by city\", \"by month\", \"by product\".","duration":16,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## The most frequent question\n\n\"What is the revenue **by city**?\" \"How many sales **per month**?\" \"What's the average grade **per class**?\"\n\nAll these questions have the same shape: **split** the data into groups, **calculate** something in each group, then **combine** the results. In pandas, that's `groupby`.\n\nLet's start by adding a `total` column (the amount of each sale):"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\nsales.head()","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## A first groupby"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\n\nsales.groupby(\"city\")[\"total\"].sum()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Read it left to right: \"in `sales`, **group by** city, take the `total` column, and compute the **sum**\". The result is a Series with one value per city."},{"t":"html","v":"<div class=\"flow\">\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Split</b><span>One pile of rows per city.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Calculate</b><span>The sum of the totals in each pile.</span></div>\n  <div class=\"flow-step\"><b>Combine</b><span>A table: one row per city.</span></div>\n</div>"},{"t":"md","v":"You can then sort the result:"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\n\nsales.groupby(\"product\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False).round(2)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Aggregation functions\n\nInstead of `sum()`, you can use any summary function:\n\n| Function | Calculates |\n|---|---|\n| `sum()` | the sum |\n| `mean()` | the mean |\n| `median()` | the median |\n| `count()` | the number of rows |\n| `min()` / `max()` | the minimum / maximum |\n| `nunique()` | the number of distinct values |\n\nAnd `agg` calculates several at once:"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\n\nsales.groupby(\"city\").agg(\n    n_sales=(\"total\", \"count\"),\n    revenue=(\"total\", \"sum\"),\n    average_basket=(\"total\", \"mean\"),\n).round(2)","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Grouping by month\n\nTo group over time, first convert the date, then extract the month with `.dt.month`:"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\nsales[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(sales[\"date\"])\nsales[\"month\"] = sales[\"date\"].dt.month\n\nsales.groupby(\"month\")[\"total\"].sum().round(0)","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"Look at the numbers: some months sell clearly more. We'll dig into that in the final project."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Several criteria: the pivot table\n\nYou can group by **two** columns at once. `pivot_table` presents the result as a cross table, rows × columns, as in Excel:"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\n\npd.pivot_table(sales, values=\"total\", index=\"city\", columns=\"category\", aggfunc=\"sum\").round(0)","run":true},{"t":"quiz","q":"Which instruction gives the **average quantity** sold for each product?","options":["`sales.groupby(\"quantity\")[\"product\"].mean()`","`sales.groupby(\"product\")[\"quantity\"].mean()`","`sales[\"product\"].mean(\"quantity\")`"],"answer":1,"explain":"You group by what defines the groups (the product), then pick the column to summarize (the quantity) and the function (the mean)."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercises"},{"t":"exercise","id":"groupby-ex1","title":"The star product","prompt":"From `sales.csv`:\n\n1. create the `total` column (quantity × unit price);\n2. create `revenue_by_product`: the revenue per product, sorted from largest to smallest;\n3. store in `star_product` the name of the product that brings in the most.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\nrevenue_by_product = \nstar_product = \nprint(star_product)\nrevenue_by_product","solution":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\n\nrevenue_by_product = sales.groupby(\"product\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False)\nstar_product = revenue_by_product.index[0]\nprint(star_product)\nrevenue_by_product","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n_v[\"total\"] = _v[\"quantity\"] * _v[\"unit_price\"]\n_r = _v.groupby(\"product\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False)\nassert \"total\" in sales.columns, \"Create the total column in sales.\"\nassert list(revenue_by_product.index) == list(_r.index), \"revenue_by_product must be sorted from largest to smallest revenue.\"\nassert abs(revenue_by_product.iloc[0] - _r.iloc[0]) < 1e-6, \"Check the revenue-per-product calculation.\"\nassert star_product == _r.index[0], f\"The product that brings in the most is {_r.index[0]}.\"","hint":"sales.groupby(\"product\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False), then .index[0] for the first name.","inputs":""},{"t":"exercise","id":"groupby-ex2","title":"Work and success","prompt":"In `students.csv`, we want to know whether study hours matter.\n\n1. create a `group` column equal to `\"over 10 h\"` if `study_hours > 10`, and `\"10 h or less\"` otherwise (tip: `np.where(condition, value_if_true, value_if_false)`);\n2. create `means`: the mean of `python_grade` per group, rounded to 1 decimal place.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"students.csv\")\n\ndf[\"group\"] = \nmeans = \nmeans","solution":"import pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"students.csv\")\n\ndf[\"group\"] = np.where(df[\"study_hours\"] > 10, \"over 10 h\", \"10 h or less\")\nmeans = df.groupby(\"group\")[\"python_grade\"].mean().round(1)\nmeans","check":"import pandas as _pd, numpy as _np\n_d = _pd.read_csv(\"students.csv\")\n_d[\"group\"] = _np.where(_d[\"study_hours\"] > 10, \"over 10 h\", \"10 h or less\")\n_m = _d.groupby(\"group\")[\"python_grade\"].mean().round(1)\nassert set(df[\"group\"]) == {\"over 10 h\", \"10 h or less\"}, \"The group column must contain exactly \\\"over 10 h\\\" and \\\"10 h or less\\\".\"\nassert (df[\"group\"] == _d[\"group\"]).all(), \"Check the condition: study_hours > 10.\"\nassert means.to_dict() == _m.to_dict(), f\"Expected means: {_m.to_dict()}\"","hint":"np.where(df[\"study_hours\"] > 10, \"over 10 h\", \"10 h or less\"), then a groupby(\"group\") on python_grade.","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"visualisation","title":"Visualizing with Matplotlib","subtitle":"A good chart beats a long table.","duration":16,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Why visualize?\n\nA table of numbers takes effort. A chart is understood in a second. **Matplotlib** is Python's reference charting library."},{"t":"code","code":"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nmonths = [\"Jan\", \"Feb\", \"Mar\", \"Apr\", \"May\", \"Jun\"]\nsales = [120, 135, 160, 158, 190, 240]\n\nplt.plot(months, sales, marker=\"o\")\nplt.title(\"First-half sales\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Month\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Number of sales\")\nplt.show()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"The recipe is always the same: create the chart (`plt.plot`, `plt.bar`…), add a **title** and **axis labels**, then `plt.show()`."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Choosing the right chart\n\n| Question | Chart | Function |\n|---|---|---|\n| How does it change over time? | line | `plt.plot` |\n| How do categories compare? | bars | `plt.bar` / `plt.barh` |\n| How are values distributed? | histogram | `plt.hist` |\n| Are two variables related? | scatter plot | `plt.scatter` |"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\nby_city = sales.groupby(\"city\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values()\n\nplt.barh(by_city.index, by_city.values, color=\"#2347B8\")\nplt.title(\"2024 revenue by city (euros)\")\nplt.show()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"**Sorted horizontal** bars: the leading city jumps out, and the names are readable without tilting your head."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Distribution: the histogram\n\nA histogram splits the values into intervals and counts how many fall into each."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"students.csv\")\nplt.hist(df[\"python_grade\"], bins=8, color=\"#C92E50\", edgecolor=\"white\")\nplt.title(\"Distribution of Python grades\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Grade out of 20\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Number of students\")\nplt.show()","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Relationships: the scatter plot\n\nDo students who work more get better grades? Each dot is a student."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"students.csv\")\nplt.scatter(df[\"study_hours\"], df[\"python_grade\"], color=\"#2347B8\")\nplt.title(\"Study hours and Python grade\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Study hours per week\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Grade out of 20\")\nplt.grid(alpha=0.3)\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"Correlation:\", round(df[\"study_hours\"].corr(df[\"python_grade\"]), 2))","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"The dots go up to the right: more hours, better grade. **Correlation** measures this link between -1 and 1; close to 1, the link is strong and positive. Compare with `math_grade`: is there a link?"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"","v":"Correlation is not causation. Two things that vary together aren't necessarily cause and effect. Here it's plausible, but always stay careful with your conclusions."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## The pandas shortcut\n\npandas Series and DataFrames have a `.plot()` method that calls Matplotlib for you:"},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(sales[\"date\"])\nsales[\"month\"] = sales[\"date\"].dt.month\nsales.groupby(\"month\")[\"quantity\"].sum().plot(kind=\"line\", marker=\"o\", title=\"Items sold per month\")\nplt.show()","run":true},{"t":"md","v":"`kind` can be `\"line\"`, `\"bar\"`, `\"barh\"`, `\"hist\"`, `\"pie\"`…"},{"t":"quiz","q":"You want to show how an app's number of users changed over 12 months. Which chart do you choose?","options":["A line chart","A histogram","A scatter plot"],"answer":0,"explain":"For a change over time, the line is the natural choice: the eye follows the trend from left to right."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Exercise: your first chart"},{"t":"exercise","id":"visualisation-ex1","title":"Sales by category","prompt":"Create a **bar chart** (`plt.bar`) showing the **number of items sold** (sum of `quantity`) for each `category` in `sales.csv`.\n\nAdd a title with `plt.title(...)`.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nby_category = \n\nplt.show()","solution":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nby_category = sales.groupby(\"category\")[\"quantity\"].sum()\n\nplt.bar(by_category.index, by_category.values, color=\"#2347B8\")\nplt.title(\"Items sold by category\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Quantity\")\nplt.show()","check":"import matplotlib.pyplot as _plt\nimport pandas as _pd\nassert _plt.get_fignums(), \"No chart was created. Use plt.bar(...).\"\n_ax = _plt.gcf().axes[0]\n_bars = [p for p in _ax.patches]\nassert len(_bars) == 3, f\"There are 3 categories, so 3 bars are expected. Your chart has {len(_bars)}.\"\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\").groupby(\"category\")[\"quantity\"].sum()\nassert sorted(round(b.get_height()) for b in _bars) == sorted(int(x) for x in _v.values), \"The bar heights must be the sum of quantities per category.\"\nassert _ax.get_title().strip(), \"Add a title with plt.title(\\\"...\\\").\"","hint":"by_category = sales.groupby(\"category\")[\"quantity\"].sum(), then plt.bar(by_category.index, by_category.values)","inputs":""}]]},{"id":"projet-final","title":"Final project, the Baobab Café report","subtitle":"You are the analyst. Management is waiting for your answers.","duration":25,"steps":[[{"t":"md","v":"## Your mission\n\nThe **Baobab Café** runs five cafés: Kinshasa, Abidjan, Dakar, Paris and Montreal. The director is preparing the 2025 budget and hands you the 2024 sales file, `sales.csv`. She has four questions:\n\n1. What is the total **revenue** for the year?\n2. Which **city** brings in the most?\n3. Are some **seasons** stronger than others?\n4. Does **bissap juice**, a new product, behave differently?\n\nYou'll follow the analyst's method from chapter 1: question, data, cleaning, analysis, visualization, decision. Each step is an exercise. You can use the cheat sheet (top menu) at any time."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Step 1: preparing the data"},{"t":"exercise","id":"projet-final-ex1","title":"Preparing the table","prompt":"Load `sales.csv` into `sales`, then add three columns:\n\n- `date` converted into a real date with `pd.to_datetime`;\n- `month`: the month number (1 to 12), extracted from the date;\n- `total`: the amount of each sale (quantity × unit price).\n\nFinally, calculate `total_revenue`, the year's revenue, rounded to the nearest euro.","starter":"import pandas as pd\n\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n\ntotal_revenue = \nprint(f\"2024 revenue: {total_revenue} euros\")","solution":"import pandas as pd\n\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(sales[\"date\"])\nsales[\"month\"] = sales[\"date\"].dt.month\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\n\ntotal_revenue = round(sales[\"total\"].sum())\nprint(f\"2024 revenue: {total_revenue} euros\")","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n_v[\"total\"] = _v[\"quantity\"] * _v[\"unit_price\"]\nfor col in (\"month\", \"total\"):\n    assert col in sales.columns, f\"The {col} column is missing.\"\nassert str(sales[\"date\"].dtype).startswith(\"datetime\"), \"The date column must be converted with pd.to_datetime.\"\nassert sales[\"month\"].min() == 1 and sales[\"month\"].max() == 12, \"The month column must contain month numbers (1 to 12).\"\nassert total_revenue == round(_v[\"total\"].sum()), f\"The expected revenue is {round(_v['total'].sum())} euros.\"","hint":"pd.to_datetime(sales[\"date\"]); sales[\"date\"].dt.month; sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]; round(sales[\"total\"].sum())","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Step 2: the cities"},{"t":"exercise","id":"projet-final-ex2","title":"Ranking the cities","prompt":"Calculate `revenue_by_city`: the revenue per city, sorted from largest to smallest. Then calculate `top_share`: the **share** (in %) of the first city in the total revenue, rounded to 1 decimal place.\n\nThe step 1 code is already there so this exercise works on its own.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\n\nrevenue_by_city = \ntop_share = \nprint(revenue_by_city.round(0))\nprint(f\"The first city makes up {top_share}% of sales\")","solution":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\n\nrevenue_by_city = sales.groupby(\"city\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False)\ntop_share = round(revenue_by_city.iloc[0] / revenue_by_city.sum() * 100, 1)\nprint(revenue_by_city.round(0))\nprint(f\"The first city makes up {top_share}% of sales\")","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n_v[\"total\"] = _v[\"quantity\"] * _v[\"unit_price\"]\n_c = _v.groupby(\"city\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False)\nassert list(revenue_by_city.index) == list(_c.index), \"revenue_by_city must be sorted from largest to smallest.\"\nassert top_share == round(_c.iloc[0] / _c.sum() * 100, 1), f\"The expected share is {round(_c.iloc[0] / _c.sum() * 100, 1)}%.\"","hint":"A groupby(\"city\") on total, sorted. The share: revenue_by_city.iloc[0] / revenue_by_city.sum() * 100","inputs":""}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Step 3: the seasons"},{"t":"exercise","id":"projet-final-ex3","title":"The year's curve","prompt":"Plot the **line** of revenue per **month** (1 to 12), with a title. Also store in `best_month` the number of the month that brings in the most.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(sales[\"date\"])\nsales[\"month\"] = sales[\"date\"].dt.month\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\n\nrevenue_by_month = \nbest_month = \n\nplt.show()\nprint(\"Best month:\", best_month)","solution":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(sales[\"date\"])\nsales[\"month\"] = sales[\"date\"].dt.month\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\n\nrevenue_by_month = sales.groupby(\"month\")[\"total\"].sum()\nbest_month = revenue_by_month.idxmax()\n\nplt.plot(revenue_by_month.index, revenue_by_month.values, marker=\"o\", color=\"#2347B8\")\nplt.title(\"Revenue per month, 2024\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Month\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Euros\")\nplt.xticks(range(1, 13))\nplt.show()\nprint(\"Best month:\", best_month)","check":"import pandas as _pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as _plt\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n_v[\"month\"] = _pd.to_datetime(_v[\"date\"]).dt.month\n_v[\"total\"] = _v[\"quantity\"] * _v[\"unit_price\"]\n_m = _v.groupby(\"month\")[\"total\"].sum()\nassert int(best_month) == int(_m.idxmax()), f\"The best month is month {_m.idxmax()}.\"\nassert _plt.get_fignums(), \"Plot the line with plt.plot(...).\"\n_ax = _plt.gcf().axes[0]\nassert _ax.get_lines() and len(_ax.get_lines()[0].get_xdata()) == 12, \"The line must have 12 points, one per month.\"\nassert _ax.get_title().strip(), \"Add a title to your chart.\"","hint":"revenue_by_month = sales.groupby(\"month\")[\"total\"].sum(); best_month = revenue_by_month.idxmax() (the label of the largest value); plt.plot(revenue_by_month.index, revenue_by_month.values)","inputs":""},{"t":"md","v":"What do you see? The summer months (June to August) and December stand out. One hypothesis: holidays and end-of-year celebrations. For the director, that's concrete information: **plan more staff and stock** for those periods."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Step 4: the bissap case"},{"t":"exercise","id":"projet-final-ex4","title":"Bissap juice","prompt":"Does bissap juice sell differently depending on the season?\n\nCalculate `bissap_by_month`: the **quantity** of Bissap juice sold per month, then `ratio`: the average quantity per month **from March to September** (months 3 to 9) divided by the average quantity per month **in the other months**, rounded to 2 decimal places.","starter":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"month\"] = pd.to_datetime(sales[\"date\"]).dt.month\n\nbissap = sales[sales[\"product\"] == \"Bissap juice\"]\nbissap_by_month = \n\nseason = bissap_by_month[(bissap_by_month.index >= 3) & (bissap_by_month.index <= 9)]\noff_season = \nratio = \nprint(bissap_by_month)\nprint(\"Hot season / rest of the year ratio:\", ratio)","solution":"import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"month\"] = pd.to_datetime(sales[\"date\"]).dt.month\n\nbissap = sales[sales[\"product\"] == \"Bissap juice\"]\nbissap_by_month = bissap.groupby(\"month\")[\"quantity\"].sum()\n\nseason = bissap_by_month[(bissap_by_month.index >= 3) & (bissap_by_month.index <= 9)]\noff_season = bissap_by_month[(bissap_by_month.index < 3) | (bissap_by_month.index > 9)]\nratio = round(season.mean() / off_season.mean(), 2)\nprint(bissap_by_month)\nprint(\"Hot season / rest of the year ratio:\", ratio)","check":"import pandas as _pd\n_v = _pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\n_v[\"month\"] = _pd.to_datetime(_v[\"date\"]).dt.month\n_b = _v[_v[\"product\"] == \"Bissap juice\"].groupby(\"month\")[\"quantity\"].sum()\n_s = _b[(_b.index >= 3) & (_b.index <= 9)]\n_h = _b[(_b.index < 3) | (_b.index > 9)]\nassert bissap_by_month.to_dict() == _b.to_dict(), \"bissap_by_month must give the sum of bissap quantities for each month.\"\nassert ratio == round(_s.mean() / _h.mean(), 2), f\"The expected ratio is {round(_s.mean() / _h.mean(), 2)}.\"","hint":"bissap.groupby(\"month\")[\"quantity\"].sum(). For the other months: index < 3 OR index > 9 (with |). The ratio: season.mean() / off_season.mean()","inputs":""},{"t":"md","v":"A ratio well above 1 confirms the intuition: bissap is a **hot-season drink**. A possible recommendation: promote it in spring and summer, and reduce stock in winter to avoid waste."}],[{"t":"md","v":"## Step 5: the report\n\nAn analyst doesn't stop at the numbers: they **tell** what they mean. Here's a model conclusion you could send to the director."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"","v":"Dear Director,\n1. Kinshasa is our leading city, well ahead of Dakar and Abidjan: a quarter of our revenue.\n2. Sales peak in summer and in December: let's strengthen staff and stock for those periods.\n3. Bissap juice sells far more from March to September: let's promote it during the hot season."},{"t":"md","v":"Run this last block: it produces the complete dashboard in a single figure. Edit it, add whatever you find useful. It's your report."},{"t":"code","code":"import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nsales = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsales[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(sales[\"date\"])\nsales[\"month\"] = sales[\"date\"].dt.month\nsales[\"total\"] = sales[\"quantity\"] * sales[\"unit_price\"]\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(11, 4))\nsales.groupby(\"city\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values().plot(kind=\"barh\", ax=axes[0], color=\"#2347B8\", title=\"Revenue by city\")\nsales.groupby(\"month\")[\"total\"].sum().plot(ax=axes[1], marker=\"o\", color=\"#C92E50\", title=\"Revenue by month\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","run":true}],[{"t":"md","v":"## What's next?\n\nCongratulations. You've travelled the whole road: understanding what programming is, learning Python, then analyzing real data all the way to concrete recommendations.\n\nTo keep going:\n\n- **Install Python on your computer.** The simplest option for data: install **Anaconda** (which includes Python, pandas and Jupyter), or Python plus the **VS Code** editor.\n- **Work in Jupyter notebooks.** It's the analysts' standard tool, and it works exactly like the code blocks in this course.\n- **Analyze data you care about.** The Kaggle website offers thousands of free datasets: football, music, health, climate… Pick a topic you love and ask it questions.\n- **Learn SQL**, the language of databases, often asked for alongside Python in data analyst job offers.\n- **Come back to the playground** on this site whenever you want to try an idea."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"","v":"One last piece of advice: code a little every day. Ten minutes a day beats three hours once a month.\nYour certificate is waiting. Well done!"}]]}]}],"pages":{"aide":"## Display and comment\n\n```python\nprint(\"Hello\")               # displays text\nprint(\"Total:\", 3 * 4)       # several items separated by commas\n# This is a comment: Python ignores it\n```\n\n## Variables and types\n\n```python\nfirst_name = \"Awa\"   # str   (text)\nage = 24             # int   (integer)\nheight = 1.68        # float (decimal number)\nregistered = True    # bool  (True or False)\nprint(type(age))     # <class 'int'>\nage = age + 1        # or: age += 1\n```\n\nConversions: `int(\"12\")`, `float(\"3.5\")`, `str(12)`. The `input()` function always returns text.\n\n## Calculations\n\n```python\n7 + 2, 7 - 2, 7 * 2   # 9, 5, 14\n7 / 2                 # 3.5  division\n7 // 2                # 3    integer division\n7 % 2                 # 1    remainder (modulo)\n2 ** 10               # 1024 power\nround(3.14159, 2)     # 3.14\n```\n\n## Text\n\n```python\nname = \"  aWa diallo \"\nname.strip().title()      # \"Awa Diallo\"\nname.upper(), name.lower()\n\"café\".replace(\"é\", \"e\")\nlen(\"Python\")             # 6\n\"Python\"[0], \"Python\"[-1] # \"P\", \"n\"\n\"Python\"[0:3]             # \"Pyt\"\nf\"{first_name} is {age}\"  # f-string\nf\"{3.14159:.2f}\"          # \"3.14\"\n```\n\n## Comparisons and logic\n\n```python\n==  !=  <  >  <=  >=      # comparisons\nage >= 18 and registered  # and\nage < 12 or age > 65      # or\nnot registered            # not\n```\n\n## Conditions\n\n```python\nif grade >= 16:\n    print(\"Excellent\")\nelif grade >= 10:\n    print(\"Pass\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Fail\")\n```\n\n## Loops\n\n```python\nfor i in range(1, 6):      # 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\n    print(i)\n\ntotal = 0\nfor x in [3, 8, 2]:\n    total += x\n\nwhile balance < goal:\n    balance += 1000\n\n# break: leave the loop; continue: skip to the next lap\n```\n\n## Lists\n\n```python\ngrades = [12, 15, 9]\ngrades.append(17)         # add\ngrades[0]                 # first item\ngrades[-1]                # last\nlen(grades), sum(grades), min(grades), max(grades)\nsorted(grades, reverse=True)\n[g * 2 for g in grades if g >= 10]   # comprehension\nfor rank, g in enumerate(grades, start=1): ...\nfor a, b in zip(list1, list2): ...\n```\n\n## Dictionaries, tuples, sets\n\n```python\nproduct = {\"name\": \"Croissant\", \"price\": 1.8}\nproduct[\"price\"]                # 1.8\nproduct[\"stock\"] = 30           # add / change\nproduct.get(\"colour\", \"?\")      # default value\nfor key, value in product.items(): ...\n\npoint = (4.3, 15.3)             # tuple (can't be changed)\nlat, lon = point                # unpacking\ncities = set([\"Dakar\", \"Dakar\", \"Paris\"])   # {\"Dakar\", \"Paris\"}\n```\n\n## Functions\n\n```python\ndef price_with_tax(price, rate=0.18):\n    \"\"\"Returns the price including tax.\"\"\"\n    return price * (1 + rate)\n\nprice_with_tax(1000)            # 1180.0\nprice_with_tax(1000, rate=0.2)  # 1200.0\nsorted(students, key=lambda s: s[\"grade\"])\n```\n\n## Modules, files, errors\n\n```python\nimport math\nfrom datetime import date\nimport random\n\nwith open(\"groceries.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    for line in f:\n        print(line.strip())\n\ntry:\n    n = int(\"abc\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    n = 0\n```\n\n## NumPy\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\na = np.array([2.5, 3.5, 2.0])\na * 2, a.sum(), a.mean(), a.std()\na[a > 2.2]                # filter with a mask\n```\n\n## Pandas: load and explore\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\ndf.head(), df.shape, df.columns, df.dtypes\ndf.info()\ndf.describe()\ndf[\"city\"].value_counts()\ndf[\"city\"].nunique()\n```\n\n## Pandas: select and sort\n\n```python\ndf[\"quantity\"]                          # one column\ndf[[\"city\", \"quantity\"]]                # several columns\ndf[df[\"city\"] == \"Dakar\"]               # filter\ndf[(df[\"quantity\"] > 10) & (df[\"city\"] == \"Paris\")]\ndf[df[\"city\"].isin([\"Dakar\", \"Abidjan\"])]\ndf.sort_values(\"quantity\", ascending=False)\ndf.loc[df[\"quantity\"] > 10, [\"date\", \"city\"]]\n```\n\n## Pandas: clean and transform\n\n```python\ndf.isna().sum()                         # missing values\ndf.dropna(subset=[\"spending\"])\ndf[\"age\"].fillna(df[\"age\"].median())\ndf.drop_duplicates()\ndf[\"city\"].str.strip().str.title()\ndf[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(df[\"date\"])\ndf[\"month\"] = df[\"date\"].dt.month\ndf[\"total\"] = df[\"quantity\"] * df[\"unit_price\"]\n```\n\n## Pandas: group\n\n```python\ndf.groupby(\"city\")[\"total\"].sum()\ndf.groupby(\"city\")[\"total\"].sum().sort_values(ascending=False)\ndf.groupby(\"city\").agg(n=(\"total\", \"count\"), revenue=(\"total\", \"sum\"))\npd.pivot_table(df, values=\"total\", index=\"city\", columns=\"category\", aggfunc=\"sum\")\n```\n\n## Charts\n\n```python\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nplt.plot(x, y, marker=\"o\")       # line (change over time)\nplt.bar(categories, values)      # bars (comparison)\nplt.barh(categories, values)     # horizontal bars\nplt.hist(values, bins=10)        # histogram (distribution)\nplt.scatter(x, y)                # scatter plot (relationship)\nplt.title(\"Title\"); plt.xlabel(\"x\"); plt.ylabel(\"y\")\nplt.show()\n\nseries.plot(kind=\"bar\", title=\"Title\")   # pandas shortcut\n```\n"}}};
